-- [Periodic Trends] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Across period 2 the electron affinity of boron is about \(-27\ \text{kJ/mol}\), while that of fluorine is about \(-322\ \text{kJ/mol}\) — far more negative. In both atoms the incoming electron is placed into the \(n=2\) valence shell. Why does the \(n=2\) shell hold an added electron so much more tightly in fluorine than in boron?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "From boron to fluorine four more protons are added to the nucleus, but the electrons that accompany them go into the same \\(n=2\\) shell and shield one another only weakly, so the net nuclear pull on a valence electron grows substantially."}, {"label": "B", "text": "From boron to fluorine four more electrons are added to the \\(n=2\\) shell, and because same-shell electrons shield efficiently, the added shielding largely cancels the extra protons, leaving the valence electron slightly more bound."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Fluorine''s larger nuclear charge is offset by its added core electrons, so the incoming electron is bound almost entirely by the greater electron–electron attraction within the filled \\(n=2\\) shell."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fluorine''s valence electrons occupy the more penetrating \\(2s\\) orbital that boron''s do not, and the deeper penetration is what raises the pull on the incoming electron across the period."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Atoms, nuclear decay, electronic structure, and atomic chemical behavior.'' Effective nuclear charge, \(Z_\text{eff}\), is the net pull the nucleus exerts on a given electron after accounting for shielding by other electrons. Moving left to right across a period, \(Z\) rises by one proton at each step, but the accompanying electrons enter the same valence shell and same-shell electrons shield one another inefficiently — so shielding grows only slightly and \(Z_\text{eff}\) climbs. A higher \(Z_\text{eff}\) binds an incoming electron more strongly, releasing more energy and making electron affinity more negative from boron to fluorine.\n\n(Choice A) is correct: The four extra protons gained from boron to fluorine are only partially offset because the four extra electrons all enter the \(n=2\) shell and shield each other poorly; the net nuclear pull ( \(Z_\text{eff}\) ) therefore rises sharply, binding the added electron more tightly and giving fluorine the far more negative electron affinity.\n\n(Choice B) is incorrect: It inverts the key fact about shielding. Electrons in the same valence shell shield one another inefficiently, not efficiently, so the added shielding does NOT cancel the extra protons; \(Z_\text{eff}\) rises substantially rather than staying nearly constant.\n\n(Choice C) is incorrect: The dominant force binding any electron is attraction to the positively charged nucleus, not electron–electron attraction (electrons repel one another). Fluorine also gains no extra core electrons across period 2 — the added electrons are valence \(2p\) electrons — so there is no core-shielding cancellation of the nuclear charge.\n\n(Choice D) is incorrect: Both boron ( \(1s^2 2s^2 2p^1\) ) and fluorine ( \(1s^2 2s^2 2p^5\) ) already have a filled \(2s\) orbital, so no new \(2s\) penetration is introduced across the period; the trend is driven by rising \(Z_\text{eff}\) from poor same-shell shielding, not by a change in orbital penetration.\n\nThis is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between added protons, inefficient same-shell shielding, and the resulting rise in effective nuclear charge to explain the observed electron affinity data.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '9245fcb4-ff34-4cc4-aabf-6dbf8f85189d';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9245fcb4-ff34-4cc4-aabf-6dbf8f85189d';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9245fcb4-ff34-4cc4-aabf-6dbf8f85189d', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'same-shell electrons shield efficiently (inverted shielding claim)'),
  ('9245fcb4-ff34-4cc4-aabf-6dbf8f85189d', 'C', 'misconception', 'electron binding attributed to electron-electron attraction / phantom core electrons'),
  ('9245fcb4-ff34-4cc4-aabf-6dbf8f85189d', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'orbital penetration invoked where no orbital change occurs across the period');

-- [Cell Types & Electron Flow] ans=C medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A chemist sets up a Downs-type apparatus with two inert electrodes immersed in molten (liquid) sodium chloride to carry out the reaction \(\text{NaCl}(l) \rightarrow \text{Na}(l) + \tfrac{1}{2}\text{Cl}_2(g)\). Using standard reduction potentials, she calculates the cell potential for this reaction as written to be \(E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = -4.07\ \text{V}\). Based on this value, which classification of cell she must build to run the reaction, paired with the correct thermodynamic description of that reaction, is correct?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Galvanic (voltaic) cell; the reaction is non-spontaneous (\\(\\Delta G > 0\\))"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Electrolytic cell; the reaction is spontaneous (\\(\\Delta G < 0\\))"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Electrolytic cell; the reaction is non-spontaneous (\\(\\Delta G > 0\\))"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Galvanic (voltaic) cell; the reaction is spontaneous (\\(\\Delta G < 0\\))"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Electrochemistry (GC).'' Cell potential and spontaneity are linked by \(\Delta G = -nFE_{\text{cell}}\): a positive \(E_{\text{cell}}\) marks a spontaneous reaction, while a negative \(E_{\text{cell}}\) marks a non-spontaneous one. A galvanic (voltaic) cell harnesses a spontaneous reaction (\(E_{\text{cell}} > 0\)) to generate current on its own, whereas an electrolytic cell uses an external power source to force a non-spontaneous reaction to occur. (Choice C) is correct: the negative \(E^\circ_{\text{cell}}\) of \(-4.07\ \text{V}\) makes \(\Delta G = -nFE_{\text{cell}}\) positive, so the decomposition is non-spontaneous; because it will not proceed on its own, an external voltage exceeding \(4.07\ \text{V}\) must be imposed, which is precisely the job of an electrolytic cell. (Choice A) is incorrect: it correctly reads the negative potential as a non-spontaneous reaction, but a non-spontaneous reaction cannot power a galvanic cell — galvanic cells run only on spontaneous reactions, so it names the wrong device. (Choice B) is incorrect: it correctly identifies that an electrolytic cell is needed, but a reaction requiring an external power source is by definition non-spontaneous, so labeling it spontaneous contradicts the negative \(E_{\text{cell}}\) and gives \(\Delta G\) the wrong sign. (Choice D) is incorrect: it inverts both facts, treating the negative potential as if it were positive (spontaneous) and pairing it with a self-powered galvanic cell, whereas a negative \(E_{\text{cell}}\) forbids spontaneous galvanic operation. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that a negative \(E_{\text{cell}}\) corresponds to a non-spontaneous reaction (\(\Delta G > 0\)) that only an electrolytic cell, driven by an external source, can force to occur.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = '9db8051e-887c-4c6d-b2b7-89914f88589f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9db8051e-887c-4c6d-b2b7-89914f88589f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9db8051e-887c-4c6d-b2b7-89914f88589f', 'A', 'misconception', 'correct thermodynamics but wrong cell type — believes a non-spontaneous reaction can run in a galvanic cell'),
  ('9db8051e-887c-4c6d-b2b7-89914f88589f', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'correct cell type but reverses the sign relationship — treats a negative Ecell as spontaneous'),
  ('9db8051e-887c-4c6d-b2b7-89914f88589f', 'D', 'misconception', 'double error — misreads negative Ecell as spontaneous and pairs it with a galvanic cell');

-- [Periodic Trends] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'The nuclear charge \(Z\) increases from \(\text{Li}\) (3) to \(\text{Na}\) (11) to \(\text{K}\) (19). Across a period, a rising \(Z\) pulls electrons in and shrinks the atom, yet down Group 1 the atomic radius grows larger despite the same increase in \(Z\). Which statement best explains why the added protons fail to contract these atoms?',
  options = '[{"text": "Newly added core electrons shield the valence electron so effectively that the effective nuclear charge it feels stays nearly constant, leaving the more distant outer shell to set the size.", "label": "A"}, {"text": "The extra protons are offset by an equal number of neutrons, which cancels the additional nuclear pull on the valence electron.", "label": "B"}, {"text": "Electron–electron repulsion among the valence electrons grows down the group and pushes the outer shell outward faster than the nucleus can pull it in.", "label": "C"}, {"text": "The added protons raise the effective nuclear charge on the valence electron, and this stronger pull expands the electron cloud outward.", "label": "D"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Atoms, nuclear decay, electronic structure, and atomic chemical behavior.'' Effective nuclear charge \(Z_\text{eff}\) is the net pull the nucleus exerts on a given electron after inner (core) electrons partially shield it. Across a period, \(Z\) rises but shielding barely changes, so \(Z_\text{eff}\) climbs and the atom contracts; down a group, however, each element adds a full new core shell whose electrons shield the outermost electron efficiently, so \(Z_\text{eff}\) on the valence electron stays roughly constant even as \(Z\) grows. (Choice A) is correct: because the added protons are matched by added core electrons that shield well, \(Z_\text{eff}\) on the valence electron changes little, so the increase in size is governed by the valence electron occupying a larger, more distant shell rather than by any stronger inward pull. (Choice B) is incorrect: neutrons carry no charge and exert no electrostatic pull on electrons, so they cannot offset the added protons; shielding by core electrons, not neutrons, is what blunts the nuclear charge. (Choice C) is incorrect: valence electron–electron repulsion is not the driver here, since Group 1 atoms each hold only a single valence electron, so there is no growing pool of same-shell electrons to repel one another. (Choice D) is incorrect: a higher \(Z_\text{eff}\) would draw the valence electron inward and shrink the atom, the opposite of the observed trend, and \(Z_\text{eff}\) does not in fact rise appreciably down a group because shielding offsets the added protons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concepts of shielding and effective nuclear charge to reconcile why a rising nuclear charge shrinks atoms across a period but not down a group.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '232c56b1-5156-44f5-9045-8495459d7da2';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '232c56b1-5156-44f5-9045-8495459d7da2';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('232c56b1-5156-44f5-9045-8495459d7da2', 'B', 'misconception', 'neutrons offset protons'' electrostatic pull'),
  ('232c56b1-5156-44f5-9045-8495459d7da2', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'electron-electron repulsion invoked where only one valence electron exists'),
  ('232c56b1-5156-44f5-9045-8495459d7da2', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'higher Zeff would expand rather than contract the atom');

-- [Electron Configurations] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'One electron in a 2p orbital of a nitrogen atom is described by the quantum-number set \((n, l, m_l, m_s) = (2,\,1,\,-1,\,+\tfrac{1}{2})\). Which of the following quantum-number sets could describe a second electron that occupies the exact same orbital as this first electron without violating any rule of the quantum-mechanical model?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\((2,\\,1,\\,-1,\\,-\\tfrac{1}{2})\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\((2,\\,1,\\,0,\\,-\\tfrac{1}{2})\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\((2,\\,1,\\,-1,\\,+\\tfrac{1}{2})\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\((2,\\,0,\\,0,\\,-\\tfrac{1}{2})\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Atoms, nuclear decay, electronic structure, and atomic chemical behavior.'' Every electron in an atom is completely specified by the set \((n, l, m_l, m_s)\); the first three numbers identify the orbital and the fourth describes spin. Two electrons occupy the same orbital only when they share identical \(n\), \(l\), and \(m_l\), and the Pauli exclusion principle then forbids them from also sharing \(m_s\), so the spins must be opposite (\(+\tfrac{1}{2}\) and \(-\tfrac{1}{2}\)). The first electron here is \((2,1,-1,+\tfrac{1}{2})\), so a valid partner in that same orbital must match its first three numbers and flip only the spin. (Choice A) is correct: it keeps \(n=2\), \(l=1\), and \(m_l=-1\) identical—so it is the same orbital—and reverses the spin to \(-\tfrac{1}{2}\), satisfying the Pauli exclusion principle. (Choice B) is incorrect: \((2,1,0,-\tfrac{1}{2})\) is a legitimate electron, but \(m_l=0\) makes it a different 2p orbital from the \(m_l=-1\) orbital of the first electron, so the two electrons do not share the same orbital. (Choice C) is incorrect: \((2,1,-1,+\tfrac{1}{2})\) is identical to the first electron in all four quantum numbers, which the Pauli exclusion principle explicitly forbids—no two electrons in an atom may share the complete set. (Choice D) is incorrect: \((2,0,0,-\tfrac{1}{2})\) is an allowed set, but \(l=0\) designates the 2s orbital rather than a 2p orbital, so it is not the same orbital as the first electron. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of an orbital (matching \(n\), \(l\), and \(m_l\)) together with the Pauli exclusion principle to test each candidate set rather than merely naming the leftover quantum number.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8867b352-d2f7-48c6-a823-4f2881a23578';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '8867b352-d2f7-48c6-a823-4f2881a23578';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('8867b352-d2f7-48c6-a823-4f2881a23578', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'Valid electron but a different orbital (different m_l) within the same 2p subshell'),
  ('8867b352-d2f7-48c6-a823-4f2881a23578', 'C', 'misconception', 'Fails to flip spin—identical four-number set, directly violating Pauli'),
  ('8867b352-d2f7-48c6-a823-4f2881a23578', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'Valid set but l=0 places it in the 2s orbital, not the same 2p orbital');

-- [Types of Elements] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two solid rods of comparable dimensions, one of copper and one of pure silicon \(\left(\text{Si}\right)\), are each heated from room temperature to \(200\,^{\circ}\text{C}\). Over this range the copper rod''s electrical conductivity falls steadily, whereas the silicon rod''s conductivity rises. Which statement best accounts for the opposite behavior of the silicon rod?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Silicon is a metal whose delocalized conduction electrons become more mobile as thermal energy loosens their attraction to the lattice."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Silicon is a nonmetal whose covalent bonds break irreversibly on heating, permanently releasing electrons that then remain free to carry charge."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Silicon is a metalloid in which added thermal energy promotes more of its comparatively tightly held valence electrons into a mobile, charge-carrying state."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Silicon is a metalloid whose atoms ionize at these temperatures, so the resulting mobile cations rather than electrons account for the added conductivity."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.'' Silicon is a metalloid, and metalloids behave as semiconductors: their valence electrons are more tightly bound to their nuclei than those of a metallic conductor, so only a modest number are free to carry charge at room temperature. Supplying thermal energy promotes additional valence electrons into a mobile, conducting state, so a semiconductor''s conductivity rises with temperature. In a true metal such as copper, conduction electrons are already delocalized, and heating mainly increases lattice vibrations that scatter them, so metallic conductivity falls with temperature—exactly the opposite trend.\n(Choice A) is incorrect: it misclassifies silicon as a metal and borrows the (incorrect) idea that metallic conductivity improves on heating; a metal''s delocalized electrons are already mobile, and heating lowers its conductivity through increased lattice scattering, which is why copper behaves as it does here.\n(Choice B) is incorrect: heating pure silicon over this modest range does not irreversibly break its covalent framework; the rise in conductivity comes from reversible thermal promotion of electrons across the band gap, and if bonds were permanently destroyed the effect would not reverse on cooling.\n(Choice C) is correct: it identifies silicon as a metalloid/semiconductor and gives the right mechanism—thermal energy lifts more of its tightly held valence electrons into a mobile state, increasing conductivity as temperature rises.\n(Choice D) is incorrect: the mobile charge carriers generated by heating a semiconductor are electrons (and the electron vacancies they leave behind), not free cations released by ionizing atoms; silicon atoms do not ionize into a mobile cation gas at a few hundred degrees Celsius.\nThis is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the semiconductor behavior of metalloids to explain why heating raises silicon''s conductivity while lowering copper''s.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd90ee597-7f1a-4ae2-ae00-ea1916826867';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd90ee597-7f1a-4ae2-ae00-ea1916826867';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d90ee597-7f1a-4ae2-ae00-ea1916826867', 'A', 'misconception', 'treats silicon as a metal and inverts the metallic temperature-conductivity trend'),
  ('d90ee597-7f1a-4ae2-ae00-ea1916826867', 'B', 'misconception', 'attributes conductivity rise to irreversible bond breaking rather than reversible thermal promotion'),
  ('d90ee597-7f1a-4ae2-ae00-ea1916826867', 'D', 'misconception', 'invokes mobile cations from ionization instead of electron charge carriers');

-- [Periodic Table Structure & Classification] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A neutral, ground-state element sits in Period 4 and Group 13, immediately to the right of the last element of the first transition series (zinc). It has three valence electrons and forms a stable 3+ cation. Based on the subshell that receives its differentiating (last-added) electron, this element belongs to which block of the periodic table?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The s-block"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The d-block"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The p-block"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The f-block"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.'' Block assignment is set by the subshell that receives the differentiating (last-added) electron, not by whether an inner subshell happens to be occupied. The element described is gallium (Group 13, Period 4), whose ground-state configuration is \([\text{Ar}]4s^2 3d^{10} 4p^1\); the \(3d\) subshell fills completely before the final electron is added, and that completely filled \(3d\) counts as core rather than valence. Because gallium''s three valence electrons occupy the outermost \(n=4\) shell and the differentiating electron enters a \(4p\) subshell, gallium is a main-group (representative) element. (Choice A) is incorrect: the s-block comprises elements whose differentiating electron enters an \(ns\) subshell, giving valence configurations of \(ns^1\) or \(ns^2\) and a group number of 1 or 2; a Group 13 element with three valence electrons cannot have its last electron in an \(s\) subshell. (Choice B) is incorrect: sitting just after the first transition series and carrying a filled \(3d^{10}\) subshell tempts one to call this element a transition metal, but the d-block is defined by a differentiating electron entering a \(d\) subshell and by a partially filled \(d\) subshell; here \(3d\) is completely filled and closed before the final \(4p\) electron is added, so it acts as core and does not set the block. (Choice C) is correct: the p-block consists of elements whose differentiating electron enters an \(np\) subshell, yielding valence configurations of the form \(ns^2 np^{1-6}\); gallium''s last electron enters \(4p\), giving \(4s^2 4p^1\) as its three valence electrons and placing it in the p-block. (Choice D) is incorrect: the f-block (inner transition metals) is defined by a differentiating electron entering an \((n-2)f\) subshell, characteristic of the lanthanides and actinides; a Period 4 element has no occupied \(f\) subshell, so its differentiating electron cannot be an \(f\) electron. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that block is set by the differentiating electron and recognize that a completely filled inner \(d\) subshell counts as core, so a Group 13 element ends in \(4p^1\) and belongs to the p-block despite following the transition series.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '11ca04a8-689b-4872-b575-8a06913f846b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '11ca04a8-689b-4872-b575-8a06913f846b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('11ca04a8-689b-4872-b575-8a06913f846b', 'A', 'misconception', 'Confuses outermost-s occupancy with block assignment'),
  ('11ca04a8-689b-4872-b575-8a06913f846b', 'B', 'misconception', 'Treats a filled inner 3d subshell (or position after the transition series) as making the element a transition metal'),
  ('11ca04a8-689b-4872-b575-8a06913f846b', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'Associates inner-transition/f-block with any element carrying a filled inner subshell');

-- [Periodic Table Structure & Classification] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A chemist writes the ground-state electron configuration of a neutral gallium atom in order of increasing orbital energy as \([\text{Ar}]4s^2 3d^{10} 4p^1\). Working only from this configuration, in which period of the periodic table does gallium reside?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Period 3"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Period 4"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Period 5"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Period 10"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.'' For a representative element, the period number equals the highest principal quantum number \(n\) of any occupied shell, not the order in which subshells were filled by the Aufbau principle. In \([\text{Ar}]4s^2 3d^{10} 4p^1\), the \(4s\) and \(4p\) electrons occupy the \(n=4\) shell, while the \(3d\) electrons—though written after \(4s\) because they are lower in energy—belong to the already-buried \(n=3\) shell; the largest occupied value of \(n\) is \(4\). (Choice B) is correct: the outermost occupied shell is \(n=4\) (holding the \(4s\) and \(4p\) valence electrons), so gallium sits in period 4, even though the last-written subshell is \(3d\). (Choice A) is incorrect: \(n=3\) is the shell of the filled \(3d^{10}\) subshell, but those \(d\) electrons count as core, not valence, and the atom still occupies the higher \(n=4\) shell, so the period is fixed by the maximum \(n\), not by whichever subshell was written last. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(n=5\) would require electrons in a fifth shell, but no \(5s\), \(5p\), or higher orbital is occupied here; counting the number of distinct subshells written (or adding one past \(n=4\)) overshoots the actual outermost shell. (Choice D) is incorrect: the \(10\) is the superscript giving the number of electrons in the \(3d\) subshell, not a principal quantum number; treating the largest digit in the configuration as the period confuses electron count with shell number. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that the period equals the highest occupied principal quantum number, distinguishing the outermost shell from the energy-based filling order and from raw electron counts.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c439ade1-c1cc-4f17-b4aa-955a20787481';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c439ade1-c1cc-4f17-b4aa-955a20787481';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c439ade1-c1cc-4f17-b4aa-955a20787481', 'A', 'misconception', 'reads the principal quantum number of the last-written (3d) subshell instead of the highest occupied shell'),
  ('c439ade1-c1cc-4f17-b4aa-955a20787481', 'C', 'misconception', 'overshoots by counting subshells or adding one past the true outermost shell'),
  ('c439ade1-c1cc-4f17-b4aa-955a20787481', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'grabs the largest digit in the configuration, mistaking an electron-count superscript for a period number');

-- [Periodic Table Structure & Classification] ans=B medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Phosphorus (Z = 15) is a pnictogen. Which of the following ground-state electron configurations belongs to a *different* element that occupies the same group of the periodic table as phosphorus?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(1s^2\\,2s^2\\,2p^6\\,3s^2\\,3p^3\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\([\\text{Kr}]\\,5s^2\\,4d^{10}\\,5p^3\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\([\\text{Ne}]\\,3s^2\\,3p^5\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\([\\text{Ar}]\\,4s^2\\,3d^{10}\\,4p^1\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.'' Elements in the same group share the same number and type of valence electrons—those in the highest principal quantum number (\(n\)) shell—differing only in which shell \(n\) that valence pattern occupies. For a main-group element, a completely filled inner \(d^{10}\) subshell counts as core, not valence, so the valence count is read only from the outermost \(ns\) and \(np\) electrons. Phosphorus sits in group 15 with valence \(3s^2 3p^3\): five valence electrons. A group-mate must therefore be a *different* element that also has five valence electrons of the \(ns^2 np^3\) type.\n\n(Choice B) is correct: \([\text{Kr}]\,5s^2\,4d^{10}\,5p^3\) is antimony (Sb). The filled \(4d^{10}\) is core, so its valence shell is \(5s^2 5p^3\)—five valence electrons in the \(ns^2 np^3\) pattern. That reproduces phosphorus''s valence configuration at \(n=5\), placing antimony in group 15, so it is a genuine group-mate.\n\n(Choice A) is incorrect: \(1s^2 2s^2 2p^6 3s^2 3p^3\) is simply the full configuration of phosphorus itself (\(Z=15\)). Although its \(3p^3\) ending superficially matches the group, it is the *same* element, not a different one in the same group, so the question''s requirement of a different element excludes it.\n\n(Choice C) is incorrect: \([\text{Ne}]\,3s^2 3p^5\) is chlorine, whose valence \(ns^2 np^5\) gives seven valence electrons. Two extra \(p\) electrons place it in group 17 (the halogens), not group 15.\n\n(Choice D) is incorrect: \([\text{Ar}]\,4s^2\,3d^{10}\,4p^1\) is gallium; with the \(3d^{10}\) counted as core, its valence is \(4s^2 4p^1\)—only three valence electrons—placing it in group 13, not group 15.\n\nThis is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that group membership is fixed by the number and type of valence (highest-\(n\)) electrons, that a filled \(d^{10}\) subshell is core rather than valence, and that a group-mate must be a distinct element sharing phosphorus''s \(ns^2 np^3\) pattern.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = '52942f5c-e1cf-4a2e-9874-038e68b26109';
