-- [Types of Elements] ans=A easy skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A newly characterized element is lustrous, malleable, and an excellent conductor of both heat and electricity. On a periodic table, the metalloid "staircase" is the stepped diagonal line that separates the table into broad regions. Based on its properties, where relative to the staircase would this element be located, and which region is that?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "To the left of the staircase, in the metal region"}, {"label": "B", "text": "To the right of the staircase, in the nonmetal region"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Directly along the staircase, in the metalloid region"}, {"label": "D", "text": "To the right of the staircase, in the metalloid region"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.'' Elements are sorted into three classes by their properties: metals are shiny (lustrous), malleable, and good conductors of heat and electricity; nonmetals are dull and poor conductors; and metalloids are intermediate. The metalloid staircase is the stepped diagonal running roughly from \(\text{B}\) toward \(\text{At}\); metals occupy the large region to its left, nonmetals occupy the region to its right, and the metalloids themselves lie along the line. (Choice A) is correct: lustrous, malleable, and an excellent conductor are the defining properties of a metal, and metals are the elements found to the left of the staircase. (Choice B) is incorrect: the nonmetal region does lie to the right of the staircase, but nonmetals are dull and conduct poorly, so an excellent conductor cannot be placed there. (Choice C) is incorrect: elements along the staircase are the metalloids (e.g., \(\text{Si}\), \(\text{Ge}\), \(\text{As}\)), which conduct only moderately and show intermediate properties, not the strong metallic conductivity described. (Choice D) is incorrect: this both places the element on the wrong side and misclassifies it—the metalloids sit on the staircase itself, not to the right, and the described conductor is a metal rather than a metalloid. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the defining physical properties of metals and where metals are located relative to the metalloid staircase.',
  difficulty = 'easy',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = 'd40dc1c9-79ed-4c07-8cbd-9a4f25dbdced';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd40dc1c9-79ed-4c07-8cbd-9a4f25dbdced';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d40dc1c9-79ed-4c07-8cbd-9a4f25dbdced', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'correct side-name pairing but wrong side for a metal'),
  ('d40dc1c9-79ed-4c07-8cbd-9a4f25dbdced', 'C', 'misconception', 'confusing intermediate conductivity of metalloids with strong metallic conductivity'),
  ('d40dc1c9-79ed-4c07-8cbd-9a4f25dbdced', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'names a real region/class but mislocates it and misclassifies the element');

-- [Ionic Character & Bond Type] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'The electronegativity difference \(\Delta\text{EN}\) for each of the following bonds is: \(\text{H-F} \approx 1.9\), \(\text{Na-I} \approx 1.8\), \(\text{C-O} \approx 1.0\), and \(\text{N-H} \approx 0.9\). Based on the atoms involved, which bond is best described as predominantly ionic (with electron density essentially transferred rather than merely shared)?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The \\(\\text{H-F}\\) bond, because it has the largest electronegativity difference of the four."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The \\(\\text{Na-I}\\) bond, because it joins a metal to a nonmetal."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The \\(\\text{C-O}\\) bond, because oxygen is far more electronegative than carbon."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The \\(\\text{N-H}\\) bond, because the small electronegativity difference concentrates charge on nitrogen."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.'' The electronegativity difference \(\Delta\text{EN}\) is only a rough guide to bond type and has many exceptions, so the most reliable predictor of ionic versus covalent character is the identity of the bonded atoms: bonds between two nonmetals are generally covalent (electrons shared, giving partial charges), whereas a bond between a metal and a nonmetal is often ionic (electron density essentially transferred, giving full ion charges). (Choice B) is correct: sodium is a Group 1 metal and iodine is a nonmetal, so \(\text{Na-I}\) is a metal-nonmetal bond and NaI is an ionic salt; the sodium atom donates its valence electron to iodine, producing \(\text{Na}^+\) and \(\text{I}^-\) rather than a shared pair, which is what "predominantly ionic" means, even though its \(\Delta\text{EN}\) is not the largest listed. (Choice A) is incorrect: although \(\text{H-F}\) has the largest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) (\(\approx 1.9\)), both H and F are nonmetals, so the bond is polar covalent, not ionic—HF is a classic exception showing that a large \(\Delta\text{EN}\) alone does not guarantee an ionic bond. (Choice C) is incorrect: oxygen is indeed much more electronegative than carbon, but both are nonmetals, so \(\text{C-O}\) is a polar covalent bond with partial charges, not a transferred electron pair. (Choice D) is incorrect: N and H are both nonmetals and a small \(\Delta\text{EN}\) produces less charge separation, not more; \(\text{N-H}\) is polar covalent, and its modest partial charges are the opposite of ionic transfer. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that atom type and periodic-table position—not the electronegativity-difference value alone—best predict whether a bond is ionic or covalent.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '0d02a150-7eaa-4921-b83d-1d5333ed5a0d';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0d02a150-7eaa-4921-b83d-1d5333ed5a0d';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0d02a150-7eaa-4921-b83d-1d5333ed5a0d', 'A', 'misconception', 'largest electronegativity difference must mean the most ionic bond'),
  ('0d02a150-7eaa-4921-b83d-1d5333ed5a0d', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'cites a correct fact (O more electronegative than C) that does not make a nonmetal-nonmetal bond ionic'),
  ('0d02a150-7eaa-4921-b83d-1d5333ed5a0d', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'claims a small electronegativity difference concentrates charge, reversing the actual trend');

-- [Galvanic Cell Components] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A galvanic cell is built with a tin electrode in \(\text{Sn}^{2+}\) solution and a silver electrode in \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) solution, the two electrodes joined by an external wire. As the spontaneous reaction runs, tin is oxidized to \(\text{Sn}^{2+}\) in one compartment while \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) is reduced to silver metal in the other. If the two compartments are NOT joined by a salt bridge, what happens to the cell, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The reaction quickly stalls, because each compartment develops a net charge that opposes further electron flow."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The reaction speeds up, because with no salt bridge the electrons meet less resistance on their path through the wire."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The reaction runs unchanged, because the external wire already carries all the charge needed to sustain the current."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The reaction reverses direction, because silver spontaneously becomes the electrode that is oxidized."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Electrochemistry (GC).'' In a galvanic cell, oxidation at the anode releases cations into that compartment while reduction at the cathode consumes cations from the other, so charge would pile up on each side unless a salt bridge supplies a compensating internal flow of ions. Without that internal ion path, charge cannot be balanced and the cell cannot sustain current. (Choice A) is correct: oxidation of tin adds \(\text{Sn}^{2+}\) to the anode compartment (building positive charge) while reduction removes \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) from the cathode compartment (building negative charge). This growing charge separation opposes the movement of electrons, so with no salt bridge to neutralize it the reaction halts almost immediately. (Choice B) is incorrect: the salt bridge carries ions internally, not electrons through the wire, so removing it does not lower resistance in the electron path; instead it eliminates the internal circuit and stops the current rather than speeding it up. (Choice C) is incorrect: the external wire alone cannot complete the circuit, because a working cell requires both an external electron path and an internal ion path; charge accumulating in each solution is exactly what the missing salt bridge would have relieved. (Choice D) is incorrect: spontaneity is set by the relative reduction potentials of the two half-cells, and silver''s higher reduction potential keeps it as the cathode (reduction); removing the salt bridge changes nothing about which species is oxidized, it only prevents the cell from operating. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the requirement that a galvanic cell maintain charge balance through internal ion flow to predict how the cell behaves when that pathway is absent.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c0713f14-b69b-45e0-89c5-154b4d8f18d3';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c0713f14-b69b-45e0-89c5-154b4d8f18d3';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c0713f14-b69b-45e0-89c5-154b4d8f18d3', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing internal ion flow with external electron flow / resistance'),
  ('c0713f14-b69b-45e0-89c5-154b4d8f18d3', 'C', 'misconception', 'believing the external wire alone completes the circuit'),
  ('c0713f14-b69b-45e0-89c5-154b4d8f18d3', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'misattributing spontaneity/oxidation to the wrong electrode');

-- [Mesoderm Derivatives] ans=C hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During the fourth week of human development, a researcher labels the cells of a single paraxial somite flanking the neural tube and tracks every daughter cell into the mature trunk. The labeled lineage populates several mature structures. The investigator now wants the one structure that a person can consciously activate to move the vertebral column. Which of the labeled somite-derived structures fits that requirement?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The vertebrae of the axial skeleton, a somite product that the moving column pivots around rather than one that generates the movement"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The smooth muscle in the wall of the adjacent gut tube, a mesodermal muscle that contracts to push contents through the digestive tract"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The skeletal muscle of the back, a somite product that attaches to and pulls on the vertebrae to move them"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The dermis of the dorsal skin, a somite product that is passively carried along as the column moves"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' A single paraxial somite is multipotent: the OpenStax text states the somites "will further develop into the cells that form the vertebrae and ribs, the dermis of the dorsal skin, the skeletal muscles of the back, and the skeletal muscles of the body wall and limbs." So three of the four options are genuine somite products, and merely being somite-derived does not identify the answer; the scenario adds a functional constraint — a structure a person can consciously (voluntarily) contract to move the vertebral column — which selects for striated skeletal muscle specifically, since the text notes skeletal muscle is under "voluntary" control and is "found in the muscles that move bones," whereas smooth muscle contracts under "involuntary, autonomic" control. (Choice A) is incorrect: the vertebrae are indeed a somite derivative (sclerotome), but bone is a rigid structural tissue that the column pivots around, not a tissue that contracts, so it cannot actively move the column. (Choice B) is incorrect: gut-wall smooth muscle is contractile, but it is involuntary and cannot be consciously activated, and critically it is not a somite derivative — visceral smooth muscle arises from lateral-plate/splanchnic mesoderm, not from the paraxial somite the researcher labeled, so the label never reaches it. (Choice C) is correct: the skeletal (striated) muscle of the back is the somite (myotome) derivative that is both contractile and under voluntary control, so it is the tissue a person consciously shortens to pull on the vertebrae and move the spine. (Choice D) is incorrect: the dermis of the dorsal skin is a real somite derivative (dermatome), but dermis is connective tissue with no contractile machinery, so it is passively carried along as the column moves rather than driving the movement. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the somite fate map together with muscle physiology, distinguishing a somite-derived voluntary contractile tissue from same-somite non-contractile derivatives and from a contractile muscle that is neither voluntary nor somite-derived.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'aee6e174-d53c-4d67-a76c-2955b6c1491a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'aee6e174-d53c-4d67-a76c-2955b6c1491a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('aee6e174-d53c-4d67-a76c-2955b6c1491a', 'A', 'partial_truth', 'real somite derivative that is structural, not contractile'),
  ('aee6e174-d53c-4d67-a76c-2955b6c1491a', 'B', 'misconception', 'contractile muscle wrongly assumed to be a somite/trunk derivative and consciously controllable'),
  ('aee6e174-d53c-4d67-a76c-2955b6c1491a', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'real somite derivative (dermatome) that is non-contractile connective tissue');

-- [Intermolecular Forces] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Among the group 17 hydrides, boiling point rises steadily from \(\text{HCl}\) to \(\text{HBr}\) to \(\text{HI}\), yet \(\text{HF}\) boils far higher than an extension of that trend to the lightest member would predict. Which factor best accounts for the anomalously high boiling point of \(\text{HF}\)?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Fluorine is small and among the most electronegative elements, so the \\(\\text{H}\\) it holds can be attracted to a lone pair on a neighboring molecule with a force stronger than the trend''s dispersion-based attractions"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{HF}\\) is the lightest and smallest of the halides, giving it the largest London dispersion forces of the series and therefore the highest boiling point"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The \\(\\text{H}-\\text{F}\\) bond has the largest bond dipole of the series, so ordinary dipole-dipole attraction alone raises \\(\\text{HF}\\) above the dispersion trend"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fluorine carries more lone pairs than the heavier halogens, letting each \\(\\text{HF}\\) form more intermolecular attractions of the same type present in \\(\\text{HCl}\\), \\(\\text{HBr}\\), and \\(\\text{HI}\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.'' Across the group 17 hydrides, boiling points climb from \(\text{HCl}\) to \(\text{HBr}\) to \(\text{HI}\) because increasing molecular size strengthens London dispersion forces faster than the slightly weakening dipole-dipole attractions decline. \(\text{HF}\) breaks this trend because hydrogen bonded to a small, extremely electronegative atom (\(\text{N}\), \(\text{O}\), or \(\text{F}\)) can be attracted to a lone pair on a neighboring molecule — a hydrogen bond — an interaction far stronger than the dispersion and dipole-dipole forces that set the trend for the heavier halides. (Choice A) is correct: fluorine is small and among the most electronegative elements, so its \(\text{H}\) becomes strongly partially positive and is drawn to a fluorine lone pair on the next molecule; this hydrogen bonding is stronger than the dispersion-driven attractions that govern \(\text{HCl}\), \(\text{HBr}\), and \(\text{HI}\), which is exactly why \(\text{HF}\) sits above the extrapolated trend. (Choice B) is incorrect: being the lightest and smallest member gives \(\text{HF}\) the weakest, not the strongest, London dispersion forces of the series, so dispersion alone would predict a lower boiling point than the trend — the opposite of what is observed. (Choice C) is incorrect: although \(\text{H}-\text{F}\) does have the largest bond dipole, ordinary dipole-dipole attraction is not strong enough to explain the size of the anomaly; the textbook attributes the dramatic jump specifically to hydrogen bonding, a much stronger subset of dipole-dipole interaction that requires \(\text{H}\) bonded to \(\text{N}\), \(\text{O}\), or \(\text{F}\). (Choice D) is incorrect: the heavier halides do not hydrogen bond at all because \(\text{Cl}\), \(\text{Br}\), and \(\text{I}\) are not electronegative enough, so the anomaly is not \(\text{HF}\) forming more of the same attraction — it is \(\text{HF}\) forming a fundamentally stronger, different attraction that the others lack. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between molecular structure and intermolecular forces to explain why one member of a series deviates from a boiling-point trend, rather than simply naming a force.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '496f15d8-33e3-4666-8977-8370984ac46f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '496f15d8-33e3-4666-8977-8370984ac46f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('496f15d8-33e3-4666-8977-8370984ac46f', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'invokes the correct dispersion-size trend but backwards — small size means weaker, not stronger, dispersion'),
  ('496f15d8-33e3-4666-8977-8370984ac46f', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'true that H-F has the largest bond dipole, but ordinary dipole-dipole understates the hydrogen-bonding cause of the anomaly'),
  ('496f15d8-33e3-4666-8977-8370984ac46f', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes heavier halides also hydrogen bond and HF just does more of it, when the heavier halides do not hydrogen bond at all');

-- [Definitions & Conjugate Acid-Base Pairs] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Boron trifluoride reacts with ammonia to form the adduct \(\text{F}_3\text{B}\!-\!\text{NH}_3\), joining the boron and nitrogen atoms through a single new coordinate covalent bond. Under the Lewis model, which species acts as the Lewis acid, and what structural feature accounts for that role?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{NH}_3\\), because the lone pair on nitrogen makes it electron-rich, and the electron-rich reactant is the one that accepts the new bond"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{BF}_3\\), because the highly electronegative fluorine atoms pull electron density away, leaving boron able to donate its own pair into the bond"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{BF}_3\\), because boron has an incomplete octet with an empty valence orbital that can accept an electron pair"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{NH}_3\\), because it supplies the pair of electrons that initiates formation of the coordinate covalent bond"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC).'' In the Lewis model, an acid is the species that accepts a pair of electrons and a base is the species that donates one, and the two join through a coordinate covalent bond in which both shared electrons come from a single atom. Deciding which reactant is the acid therefore requires identifying which one has the capacity to receive an electron pair, which follows from its electronic structure rather than from any positive charge or from which reactant seems more reactive. (Choice C) is correct: boron in \(\text{BF}_3\) carries only six valence electrons, so it has an incomplete octet and an empty valence orbital; that vacancy is exactly what lets it accept the nitrogen lone pair, making \(\text{BF}_3\) the electron-pair acceptor and thus the Lewis acid, while \(\text{NH}_3\) donates its lone pair and is the Lewis base. (Choice A) is incorrect: it correctly notes that nitrogen''s lone pair makes \(\text{NH}_3\) electron-rich, but it then reverses the definition—an electron-rich species donates the pair and is the base, not the acceptor, so \(\text{NH}_3\) is the Lewis base here. (Choice B) is incorrect: it names the right species but gives a wrong mechanism; the electronegative fluorines do withdraw density, yet the Lewis acid role comes from boron''s ability to accept a pair into its empty orbital, not from boron donating a pair of its own, which it does not have to spare. (Choice D) is incorrect: the species that supplies (donates) the electron pair is by definition the Lewis base, so \(\text{NH}_3\) supplying the pair makes it the base, whereas the acid is the species that accepts that pair. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Lewis definition of an acid to a molecule''s electronic structure, recognizing that an incomplete octet with an empty orbital is what enables electron-pair acceptance.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '9d7bcff7-8848-440e-b29d-444dc6b3405b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9d7bcff7-8848-440e-b29d-444dc6b3405b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9d7bcff7-8848-440e-b29d-444dc6b3405b', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'Confuses electron-rich (donor) with electron acceptor, reversing the Lewis acid/base roles'),
  ('9d7bcff7-8848-440e-b29d-444dc6b3405b', 'B', 'misconception', 'Picks the correct species by name but attributes the acid role to electronegativity/donation rather than the empty orbital that accepts the pair'),
  ('9d7bcff7-8848-440e-b29d-444dc6b3405b', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'Treats the electron-pair donor as the acid, mistaking initiation/donation for acceptance');

-- [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In the lung, the wall of an air sac and the wall of the blood capillary pressed against it are each formed from a single layer of epithelial cells that are extremely flat and thin, so the two walls together form a barrier only a fraction of a micrometer thick. Which functional advantage does this particular epithelial architecture MOST directly provide?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "It withstands repeated mechanical abrasion, because many stacked layers of cells can be shed and replaced without exposing deeper tissue"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It shortens the distance separating the two compartments, allowing dissolved gases to move rapidly between the air and the blood"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It secretes a protective mucus film onto the surface, trapping inhaled particles before they reach the bloodstream"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It absorbs nutrients from the air sac and hands them off to the capillary for transport through the circulation"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Epithelial tissue comes in subtypes whose architecture is matched to function: a single layer of very flat cells is built for movement of substances across it, whereas multiple stacked layers, tall columnar cells, and specialized glandular cells serve protection, absorption, and secretion respectively. The text states that when squamous epithelial cells are "arranged in a single layer (simple epithelia), they facilitate diffusion in tissues, such as the areas of gas exchange in the lungs and the exchange of nutrients and waste at blood capillaries." The key is to reason from the described structure — a single, extremely thin flat layer forming a barrier a fraction of a micrometer thick — to the function that geometry enables. (Choice A) is incorrect: protection against abrasion is provided by stratified (multi-layered) squamous epithelium, which the text ties to "protection... from outside abrasion and damage"; the stem explicitly describes a single thin layer, so a many-layered shedding architecture is the opposite of what is present, and thickness would impede exchange. (Choice B) is correct: a single layer of extremely flat cells minimizes the distance a molecule must travel, and because the diffusion barrier is only a fraction of a micrometer thick, dissolved gases can move rapidly between the air in the sac and the blood in the capillary — the exact advantage the text assigns to simple squamous epithelium at gas-exchange surfaces. (Choice C) is incorrect: mucus secretion is carried out by goblet cells and glandular epithelium; secreting a mucus film requires specialized secretory cells, which the described flat, thin, non-glandular layer lacks, and a mucus coat would add distance rather than shorten it. (Choice D) is incorrect: absorbing material from a lumen and passing it into circulation is the job of tall simple columnar epithelium in the digestive tract, not of the thin flat cells lining an air sac, and gas exchange is driven by diffusion across the barrier rather than by cellular absorption. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structure-function relationship of epithelial subtypes — inferring from a single, extremely thin flat layer that its advantage is minimizing diffusion distance for exchange, rather than protection, secretion, or absorption.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '7e2de1a0-d96b-4a12-a8c3-edafdfea5bd0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7e2de1a0-d96b-4a12-a8c3-edafdfea5bd0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7e2de1a0-d96b-4a12-a8c3-edafdfea5bd0', 'A', 'misconception', 'applies the protective stratified-squamous function to a single-layer tissue'),
  ('7e2de1a0-d96b-4a12-a8c3-edafdfea5bd0', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'real epithelial function (mucus secretion) that belongs to a different subtype'),
  ('7e2de1a0-d96b-4a12-a8c3-edafdfea5bd0', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'real epithelial function (columnar absorption) applied to the wrong location and mechanism');

-- [Golgi Apparatus] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a plasma cell actively secreting antibody, an antibody molecule has been synthesized and folded and has already had short sugar chains added to it. It now arrives, inside a transport vesicle, at the cis face of a stack of flattened membranous sacs, where additional carbohydrates will be attached before it is finally routed to a secretory vesicle. From which structure did the transport vesicle that fuses with this cis face most directly bud?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The rough endoplasmic reticulum"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The Golgi apparatus"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The smooth endoplasmic reticulum"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The nuclear envelope"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' In the secretory pathway, a protein is synthesized and folded in the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER), then travels through the Golgi apparatus (the stack of flattened sacs described in the stem) before being packaged for export; the direction of flow is fixed, entering at the Golgi''s cis face and leaving at its trans face. The textbook states that "transport vesicles that formed from the ER travel to the cis face, fuse with it, and empty their contents into the Golgi apparatus'' lumen" (lines 5764-5766) and that "vesicles with the integral protein bud from the ER and fuse with the Golgi apparatus'' cis face" (line 5666), so the vesicle arriving at the cis face comes from the ER, not the Golgi. (Choice A) is correct: the RER is where the antibody was synthesized, folded, and given its first sugar chains; when the protein is "not destined to stay in the RER," it leaves via "transport vesicles that bud from the RER''s membrane" (lines 5703-5705), and these are the ER-derived vesicles that fuse with the Golgi''s cis face. (Choice B) is incorrect: the Golgi apparatus is the stack of flattened sacs described in the stem itself, so it cannot be the source of the vesicle arriving at its own cis face; vesicles leave the Golgi from the opposite (trans) face as "secretory vesicles that bud from the Golgi''s trans face" (line 5771). (Choice C) is incorrect: the smooth ER carries out "synthesis of carbohydrates, lipids, and steroid hormones; detoxification of medications and poisons; and storing calcium ions" (lines 5713-5714); it lacks ribosomes and does not synthesize the secreted antibody or feed protein-laden vesicles to the Golgi cis face. (Choice D) is incorrect: the nuclear envelope is a "double-membrane structure" whose pores "control the passage of ions, molecules, and RNA between the" nucleus and cytoplasm (lines 5410-5413); although continuous with the ER, it is not the organelle that buds the antibody-carrying transport vesicles that fuse with the Golgi. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your knowledge of the direction of flow through the endomembrane system—recognizing that the cis face receives vesicles arriving from the RER—rather than simply matching the description of sorting and packaging to the Golgi.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '77578e74-81a0-4e86-a4f4-f2b2b4f31745';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '77578e74-81a0-4e86-a4f4-f2b2b4f31745';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('77578e74-81a0-4e86-a4f4-f2b2b4f31745', 'B', 'misconception', 'Pattern-matches the stem''s structural cues (flattened sacs, glycosylation, secretory vesicle) to the Golgi without tracking that the question asks for the upstream source feeding the Golgi''s cis face'),
  ('77578e74-81a0-4e86-a4f4-f2b2b4f31745', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'Confuses the smooth ER with the rough ER; correct organelle family (endoplasmic reticulum) but the SER does not synthesize secreted proteins'),
  ('77578e74-81a0-4e86-a4f4-f2b2b4f31745', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'Names a real endomembrane structure continuous with the ER, but its role is nucleocytoplasmic transport, not budding protein-carrying vesicles to the Golgi');

-- [Oxidation Numbers: Assignment & Rules] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Hydrogen sulfide, \(\text{H}_2\text{S}\), is a neutral molecular compound. Given that hydrogen combined with a nonmetal is assigned an oxidation number of \(+1\), what is the oxidation number of the sulfur atom in \(\text{H}_2\text{S}\)?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(+2\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(-2\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(-1\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(+1\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' Oxidation numbers are assigned by rule: hydrogen combined with a nonmetal is \(+1\), and the sum of the oxidation numbers of all atoms in a molecule equals the molecule''s overall charge. Because \(\text{H}_2\text{S}\) is a neutral molecule, that sum must equal \(0\), so with two hydrogens contributing \(2(+1)=+2\), the sulfur must supply \(-2\) to bring the total to zero. (Choice B) is correct: setting up \(2(+1) + x = 0\) for the two hydrogen atoms and the single sulfur atom gives \(x = -2\), the oxidation number of sulfur. (Choice A) is incorrect: \(+2\) is the combined contribution of the two hydrogen atoms, not the value that balances them; assigning \(+2\) to sulfur would make the total \(+4\), which cannot describe a neutral molecule. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(-1\) results from balancing only a single hydrogen \((1(+1) + x = 0)\) and ignoring that the formula contains two hydrogen atoms; the subscript of \(2\) must be included in the sum. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(+1\) simply copies hydrogen''s assigned oxidation number onto sulfur, but each element is assigned its own value, and sulfur here must offset the total positive contribution of the hydrogens. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that oxidation numbers sum to the molecular charge, combining hydrogen''s known \(+1\) with the formula''s subscripts to solve for the unknown value on sulfur.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a94a0996-977c-4a06-91e0-c4b2328b3d2a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a94a0996-977c-4a06-91e0-c4b2328b3d2a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a94a0996-977c-4a06-91e0-c4b2328b3d2a', 'A', 'misconception', 'assigns the summed hydrogen contribution (+2) to sulfur instead of the balancing value'),
  ('a94a0996-977c-4a06-91e0-c4b2328b3d2a', 'C', 'misconception', 'ignores the subscript 2 and balances only one hydrogen, giving -1'),
  ('a94a0996-977c-4a06-91e0-c4b2328b3d2a', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'copies hydrogen''s given +1 oxidation number onto sulfur');

-- [Bacterial Shapes] ans=A easy skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A microbiology student stains a smear from a patient with strep throat and measures the individual bacterial cells. Each cell is roughly equal in length and width, has no distinguishable long axis, and shows no curvature or twist along its body. Which morphology term applies to cells with these dimensions?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Coccus"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Bacillus"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Spirillum"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Vibrio"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).'' OpenStax Biology sorts prokaryotes into shape categories: "cocci (spherical), bacilli (rod-shaped), and spirilli (spiral-shaped)." These categories are defined by geometry: a cell with equal length and width and no long axis is a sphere, an elongated cell has a distinct long axis (a rod), and a spiral or curved cell twists or bends along its body. The described cell has equal dimensions, no long axis, and no curvature, so it is geometrically a sphere and must be matched to the correct term. (Choice A) is correct: a cell equal in length and width with no long axis and no curvature is a sphere, and the term for a spherical bacterium is coccus (the strep throat genus Streptococcus is in fact composed of cocci). (Choice B) is incorrect: a bacillus is a rod, which by definition has a long axis clearly greater than its width; the described cell has no long axis, so it is not a rod. (Choice C) is incorrect: a spirillum is a rigid spiral with multiple twists along its body, but the described cell shows no twist, ruling out a spiral form. (Choice D) is incorrect: a vibrio is a curved, comma-shaped cell, yet the described cell has no curvature, so a curved-cell term does not apply. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the standard prokaryotic morphology terms are defined by cell geometry and match a cell of equal dimensions to the term coccus.',
  difficulty = 'easy',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = 'da4d4377-d380-4ef7-8d27-5ae94643c045';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'da4d4377-d380-4ef7-8d27-5ae94643c045';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('da4d4377-d380-4ef7-8d27-5ae94643c045', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing rod morphology with spherical because both lack curvature'),
  ('da4d4377-d380-4ef7-8d27-5ae94643c045', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'naming a real but different (spiral) morphology from the same shape vocabulary'),
  ('da4d4377-d380-4ef7-8d27-5ae94643c045', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'naming a real but different (curved/comma) morphology from the same shape vocabulary');

-- [Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Equal volumes of aqueous barium nitrate, \(\text{Ba(NO}_3)_2\), and aqueous potassium sulfate, \(\text{K}_2\text{SO}_4\), are combined in a beaker. Which statement correctly gives the net ionic equation for this mixture and identifies the spectator ions?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The insoluble product is \\(\\text{Ba(NO}_3)_2\\), so the net ionic equation is \\(\\text{Ba}^{2+} + 2\\text{NO}_3^- \\rightarrow \\text{Ba(NO}_3)_2\\), and \\(\\text{K}^+\\) and \\(\\text{SO}_4^{2-}\\) are the spectator ions."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Every possible pairing of the dissolved ions gives a soluble strong electrolyte, so no precipitate forms; all four ions are spectators and no net ionic equation can be written."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The insoluble product is \\(\\text{BaSO}_4\\), so the net ionic equation is \\(\\text{Ba}^{2+} + \\text{SO}_4^{2-} \\rightarrow \\text{BaSO}_4\\), and \\(\\text{K}^+\\) and \\(\\text{NO}_3^-\\) are the spectator ions."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The spectator ions are the pair that combines into the precipitate, so \\(\\text{Ba}^{2+}\\) and \\(\\text{SO}_4^{2-}\\) are the spectators while \\(\\text{K}^+\\) and \\(\\text{NO}_3^-\\) react to form the net product."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Stoichiometry (GC).'' A net ionic equation includes only the ions that undergo a chemical change; spectator ions appear unchanged on both sides of the complete ionic equation and are dropped. To decide which ions react, apply the solubility guidelines to each possible cation/anion pairing: mixing \(\text{Ba(NO}_3)_2\) and \(\text{K}_2\text{SO}_4\) puts \(\text{Ba}^{2+}\), \(\text{NO}_3^-\), \(\text{K}^+\), and \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\) in solution, and the two new pairings to check are \(\text{KNO}_3\) and \(\text{BaSO}_4\). (Choice C) is correct: all nitrate salts are soluble and potassium salts are soluble, so \(\text{KNO}_3\) stays dissolved, but \(\text{BaSO}_4\) is one of the insoluble sulfates and precipitates; \(\text{Ba}^{2+}\) and \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\) leave solution as \(\text{Ba}^{2+} + \text{SO}_4^{2-} \rightarrow \text{BaSO}_4\), leaving \(\text{K}^+\) and \(\text{NO}_3^-\) unchanged as spectators. (Choice A) is incorrect: it names \(\text{Ba(NO}_3)_2\) as the precipitate, but all nitrate salts are soluble, so barium nitrate cannot fall out of solution; it is one of the two dissolved reactants and no such precipitation reaction occurs. (Choice B) is incorrect: it assumes every pairing is soluble, but that ignores that \(\text{BaSO}_4\) is an insoluble sulfate; because a precipitate does form, not all four ions are spectators and a real net ionic equation exists. (Choice D) is incorrect: it reverses the definition of a spectator ion. Spectators are the ions that do NOT participate in the net reaction, so the ions that combine to form the precipitate (\(\text{Ba}^{2+}\) and \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\)) are the reacting species, not the spectators. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the solubility guidelines to each possible ion pairing to determine which ions precipitate and which remain as spectators.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8efc66f9-c2bf-4548-8773-821af6f5a49f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '8efc66f9-c2bf-4548-8773-821af6f5a49f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('8efc66f9-c2bf-4548-8773-821af6f5a49f', 'A', 'misconception', 'assigns precipitate to the wrong (soluble nitrate) pairing'),
  ('8efc66f9-c2bf-4548-8773-821af6f5a49f', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes all pairings soluble, missing the insoluble sulfate'),
  ('8efc66f9-c2bf-4548-8773-821af6f5a49f', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'inverts the definition of spectator vs reacting ion');

-- [Cytoskeleton: Cilia and Flagella] ans=A medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Under an electron microscope, the free (apical) surface of an epithelial cell from the trachea is covered by dozens of short, hair-like projections extending along nearly the entire membrane. Based on this structural profile, these projections and their function in the airway are best identified as:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "cilia, which beat in coordinated waves to move the overlying mucus layer along the surface of the cell"}, {"label": "B", "text": "flagella, which beat to drive the whole epithelial cell forward along the airway wall"}, {"label": "C", "text": "cilia, which beat to drive the whole epithelial cell forward through the overlying mucus"}, {"label": "D", "text": "microvilli, which enlarge the apical surface area but generate no beating motion of their own"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Cilia, flagella, and microvilli are all apical projections, but they differ in length, number, and function. OpenStax notes that flagella are "long, hair-like structures" that "enable an entire cell to move," with "just one flagellum or a few" per cell, whereas when cilia "are present, many of them extend along the plasma membrane''s entire surface," are "short, hair-like structures," and can "move... substances along the cell''s outer surface," citing "cilia lining the cells of the respiratory tract that trap particulate matter and move it toward your nostrils." Microvilli are non-motile "fingerlike projections" that "increase the surface area available for absorption." (Choice A) is correct: dozens of short projections spanning the whole membrane match the length and number of cilia, and in the respiratory tract cilia are motile and beat to move overlying material (mucus) along a fixed cell''s surface. (Choice B) is incorrect: flagella are long and occur singly or in small numbers, which contradicts the "dozens of short" projections observed, and flagella move the whole cell rather than sweeping surface material. (Choice C) is incorrect: the identity (cilia) and structure fit, but the function is wrong — airway cilia move the mucus along a stationary cell; they do not propel the anchored epithelial cell through its own mucus. (Choice D) is incorrect: microvilli are indeed short and numerous, so the structural description alone does not rule them out, but they are non-motile surface-area amplifiers and cannot beat to move mucus, so they are not the airway''s motile clearance structure. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how cilia, flagella, and microvilli differ in length, number, and function.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = 'c9cc067c-b8bb-4023-8257-7d23fd06ec01';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c9cc067c-b8bb-4023-8257-7d23fd06ec01';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c9cc067c-b8bb-4023-8257-7d23fd06ec01', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing cilia with flagella (wrong length/number and whole-cell motility)'),
  ('c9cc067c-b8bb-4023-8257-7d23fd06ec01', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'correct organelle but inverts which entity moves (cell vs. mucus)'),
  ('c9cc067c-b8bb-4023-8257-7d23fd06ec01', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'another short/numerous apical projection (microvilli) that is non-motile');

-- [Mitochondria] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Chloramphenicol halts protein synthesis by binding the ribosomes of bacteria but not the ribosomes that build proteins in the eukaryotic cytoplasm. When the drug is added to cultured human cells at a dose that all of it reaches, which functional consequence is most likely, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "ATP output from cellular respiration falls, because the drug shuts down protein synthesis inside the mitochondrion, which supplies components of the organelle''s own energy-generating machinery"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Bulk protein synthesis throughout the cell collapses, because the drug blocks the ribosomes on the endoplasmic reticulum and those floating in the cytoplasm that produce most cellular proteins"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The circular mitochondrial genome is degraded, because a drug that stops ribosomes from reading a message also destroys the DNA template that the message was copied from"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Import of proteins into the mitochondrion stops, because the drug blocks the cytoplasmic ribosomes that make the nucleus-encoded proteins destined for the organelle"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Mitochondria are self-replicating organelles that "have their own ribosomes and DNA" (OpenStax line 5485), a set of features they share with prokaryotes because of their bacterial ancestry; their ribosomes therefore behave like bacterial ribosomes. The cell''s cytoplasmic ribosomes are a different, eukaryotic type that "float freely in the cytoplasm" or attach to the ER and translate most cellular proteins (lines 5449-5454). Because a drug that binds only bacterial ribosomes will hit the mitochondrion''s ribosomes but spare the cytoplasmic ones, the student must trace this to a functional outcome: mitochondria carry out ATP synthesis on their inner membrane (line 5495), so blocking translation inside the organelle starves it of the proteins it builds for that machinery. (Choice A) is correct: the mitochondrial ribosomes are bacteria-like and are the only ribosomes the drug can bind, so mitochondrial translation stops; since the mitochondrion makes proteins for its own inner-membrane energy machinery, ATP production from cellular respiration drops while cytoplasmic translation continues. (Choice B) is incorrect: it inverts which ribosomes are targeted — the free and ER-bound cytoplasmic ribosomes are the eukaryotic type the drug does NOT bind, so bulk cellular protein synthesis is not the pool that collapses. (Choice C) is incorrect: this confuses the step being blocked; the drug acts on ribosomes to halt translation, not on the DNA, so the circular mitochondrial genome (line 5485) remains an intact, separate component and is not degraded. (Choice D) is incorrect: nucleus-encoded mitochondrial proteins are indeed translated on cytoplasmic ribosomes, but those ribosomes are eukaryotic and are spared by the drug, so their synthesis and import into the organelle are not what is halted. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the bacterial ancestry of the mitochondrion to identify which of two ribosome pools a bacterial-ribosome drug disables and then predict the resulting loss of the organelle''s own function.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a0f5c17a-a824-441b-84f6-2fd652bc5a9b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a0f5c17a-a824-441b-84f6-2fd652bc5a9b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a0f5c17a-a824-441b-84f6-2fd652bc5a9b', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'Swaps the targeted ribosome pool — treats cytoplasmic ribosomes as the drug''s target'),
  ('a0f5c17a-a824-441b-84f6-2fd652bc5a9b', 'C', 'misconception', 'Conflates blocking translation with destroying the DNA template'),
  ('a0f5c17a-a824-441b-84f6-2fd652bc5a9b', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'States a true fact (nuclear-encoded mito proteins made on cytoplasmic ribosomes) but those ribosomes are spared');

-- [Batteries] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A technician tests two batteries. A zinc-carbon flashlight battery runs a lamp until it goes dark and then will not usefully operate the lamp again, even after the technician connects it to a charging unit. A lead-acid car battery runs a starter motor until it is depleted, but the technician fully restores it by connecting it to an external power supply for several hours. Both batteries power their devices by the same type of electrochemical process. Which statement correctly accounts for how each battery behaves?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Both discharge as galvanic cells; the lead-acid battery can be restored because its cell reaction is reversed by an electrolytic process, whereas the zinc-carbon battery is a primary cell whose reaction is not practically reversible."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Both discharge as galvanic cells; the lead-acid battery is restored because charging reverses its reaction as a second galvanic (spontaneous) process, whereas the zinc-carbon battery lacks the electrode materials needed for that spontaneous charging step."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Both discharge as electrolytic cells; the lead-acid battery is restored because its electrolytic discharge can be run backward, whereas the zinc-carbon battery is a primary cell that discharges galvanically and therefore cannot be reversed."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The zinc-carbon battery discharges as a galvanic cell and the lead-acid battery discharges as an electrolytic cell; only the lead-acid battery can be recharged because electrolytic cells, unlike galvanic cells, are inherently reversible."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Electrochemistry (GC).'' Any battery delivers current by discharging spontaneously as a galvanic (voltaic) cell, converting chemical energy into electrical energy with \(\Delta G < 0\) and \(E_{\text{cell}} > 0\); no external power source is required during use. The distinction between the two batteries is not how they discharge but whether they can be recharged: a secondary cell (like the lead-acid battery) has a conveniently reversible reaction, so charging drives the reaction backward as an electrolytic process (a nonspontaneous reaction forced by an external power supply), while a primary cell (like the zinc-carbon dry cell) has a reaction that is not practically reversible and so is discarded once spent. (Choice A) is correct: both cells discharge galvanically, the lead-acid car battery is a secondary cell recharged by an external supply that reverses its reaction electrolytically (driving a nonspontaneous change), and the zinc-carbon flashlight battery is a primary cell whose reaction cannot be practically reversed, which is exactly why the charger fails to restore it. (Choice B) is incorrect: recharging cannot be a spontaneous galvanic step, because a reaction that is spontaneous in the discharge direction is nonspontaneous in the reverse (charging) direction and must be driven by an external power source; charging is therefore electrolytic, not galvanic, and the difference between the batteries is reversibility of the reaction, not the mere presence of electrode materials. (Choice C) is incorrect: discharging a battery to power a device releases energy spontaneously and is galvanic, never electrolytic; electrolytic operation requires an external source to drive a nonspontaneous reaction, which describes charging, not the discharge that runs the lamp or the starter motor. (Choice D) is incorrect: both batteries discharge galvanically, so the premise that the lead-acid battery discharges electrolytically is wrong, and "electrolytic cells are inherently reversible" is not the reason it recharges; rechargeability comes from the cell reaction itself being reversible (secondary cell), and the recharging step is the electrolytic process. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of galvanic and electrolytic operation and the primary/secondary classification to interpret each battery''s observed behavior rather than recall a stated fact.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c13bd1db-e1cd-49dc-8dce-c6949f7471bc';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c13bd1db-e1cd-49dc-8dce-c6949f7471bc';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c13bd1db-e1cd-49dc-8dce-c6949f7471bc', 'B', 'misconception', 'treating recharging as a spontaneous/galvanic step rather than an externally driven electrolytic one'),
  ('c13bd1db-e1cd-49dc-8dce-c6949f7471bc', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'labeling discharge as electrolytic and swapping galvanic/electrolytic roles'),
  ('c13bd1db-e1cd-49dc-8dce-c6949f7471bc', 'D', 'misconception', 'attributing rechargeability to a supposed inherent reversibility of electrolytic cells instead of to a reversible cell reaction');

-- [Cytoskeleton: Microfilaments] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A neutrophil (a white blood cell) crawls through tissue toward a bacterium. As it advances, it continuously assembles and disassembles a network of cytoskeletal fibers at its leading edge, and these same fibers serve as the tracks along which the motor protein myosin generates the force that pulls the cell body forward. Which monomer polymerizes to build the fibers responsible for this crawling motion?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Actin"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Tubulin"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Keratin"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Myosin"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' The three cytoskeletal fiber types have distinct jobs: microfilaments (actin) rapidly depolymerize and reform to let a cell change shape and crawl, and they act as the track for the motor protein myosin; microtubules (tubulin) are tracks for vesicle transport and chromosome separation; and intermediate filaments (keratin among them) are purely structural and have no role in cell movement. (Choice A) is correct: the text states that actin filaments "can depolymerize (disassemble) and reform quickly, thus enabling a cell to change its shape and move," and it names white blood cells that "move to an infection site and phagocytize the pathogen" as the example; actin also "serves as a track for the movement of a motor protein we call myosin," so actin fits both the crawling and the myosin-track clues. (Choice B) is incorrect: tubulin builds microtubules, which can also disassemble and reform quickly and do serve as tracks, but their motor partners are kinesin and dynein—not myosin—and they drive vesicle transport and chromosome movement, not the amoeboid crawling of the cell''s leading edge. (Choice C) is incorrect: keratin is a genuine intermediate-filament protein, but the text is explicit that intermediate filaments "have no role in cell movement" and are "purely structural," so they cannot be the fibers that power crawling. (Choice D) is incorrect: myosin is the motor named in the stem, not a structural monomer—it walks along the actin track rather than polymerizing into it, so it cannot be the fiber being assembled and disassembled at the leading edge. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinct functional roles of the three cytoskeletal fibers to identify which monomer both drives rapid crawling and serves as the myosin track.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ccbf2e7c-8275-4dff-be3a-236e45dc96db';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ccbf2e7c-8275-4dff-be3a-236e45dc96db';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ccbf2e7c-8275-4dff-be3a-236e45dc96db', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct-monomer-of-a-different-fiber-that-also-serves-as-a-track'),
  ('ccbf2e7c-8275-4dff-be3a-236e45dc96db', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'real-cytoskeletal-protein-with-no-movement-role'),
  ('ccbf2e7c-8275-4dff-be3a-236e45dc96db', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'motor-protein-confused-with-the-track-it-walks-on');

-- [Cell Cycle Phases & Interphase] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A cell biologist exposes a population of cultured cells to a DNA-damaging agent and then measures the amount of DNA in each arrested cell using a fluorescent dye. Every arrested cell contains only a single, unreplicated complement of DNA—none of them has copied its chromosomes. At which checkpoint were these cells most likely halted?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The G2 checkpoint, which verifies that the cell has fully prepared for mitosis after its chromosomes have been copied"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The metaphase (spindle) checkpoint, which verifies that every sister chromatid is properly attached to microtubules before the chromatids are separated"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The G1 checkpoint, which verifies that the cell is ready before the DNA-copying phase begins"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The G2 checkpoint, which verifies that damaged DNA has been repaired immediately before the cell enters mitosis"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).'' Interphase runs in the order G1 → S → G2, and a cell copies its chromosomes only during S phase, so a cell that still holds a single, unreplicated amount of DNA has never entered S phase. The textbook states that at the G1 checkpoint "the cell must be ready for DNA synthesis to occur," that at the G2 checkpoint "the cell must be fully prepared for mitosis," and that the metaphase checkpoint acts "before the signal is given to separate them during anaphase"; a checkpoint that acted only after replication could not produce cells whose DNA had never doubled. (Choice A) is incorrect: the G2 checkpoint operates after S phase, so any cell arrested there would already contain a doubled amount of DNA, which contradicts the single unreplicated complement the biologist measured. (Choice B) is incorrect: the metaphase (spindle) checkpoint acts even later—during mitosis, after both replication and G2—so cells stopped there would likewise have twice the DNA and their chromosomes condensed, not an unreplicated amount. (Choice C) is correct: because the arrested cells never copied their chromosomes, they were stopped before S phase, and the only checkpoint that acts at that transition—confirming the cell is ready before DNA copying begins—is the G1 checkpoint. (Choice D) is incorrect: although DNA damage can indeed be sensed and repaired, this option again places the arrest at G2, after replication; a cell halted there would already show a doubled DNA content, so the measured single complement rules it out despite its correct mention of damage repair. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the ordering of the interphase checkpoints to the measured DNA content, inferring that an unreplicated (never-doubled) genome means the cell was stopped before S phase and therefore at the G1 checkpoint.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '79be5e12-c80d-46de-aca3-e4e847b3c9dd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '79be5e12-c80d-46de-aca3-e4e847b3c9dd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('79be5e12-c80d-46de-aca3-e4e847b3c9dd', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct function stated for the wrong checkpoint relative to the DNA-content evidence'),
  ('79be5e12-c80d-46de-aca3-e4e847b3c9dd', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'real later-acting checkpoint that cannot match a never-replicated genome'),
  ('79be5e12-c80d-46de-aca3-e4e847b3c9dd', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'correct DNA-damage-repair fact attached to a post-replication checkpoint that the data excludes');

-- [Mitosis Phases] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A cytologist watching a live animal cell under a light microscope sees the nuclear envelope begin fragmenting into small vesicles, the nucleolus fade from view, the duplicated sister chromatids coil tightly until they first appear as discrete bodies, and the two centrosomes migrate toward opposite ends of the cell while microtubules extend from each. For the cell to advance from this point to the next mitotic stage, in which the chromosomes line up midway between the poles, which event must occur next?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Spindle microtubules must attach to the kinetochores at the centromeres of the sister chromatids"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Cohesin must degrade so the sister chromatids can split at the centromere and move to opposite poles"}, {"label": "C", "text": "DNA must replicate so that each chromosome comes to consist of two sister chromatids joined at the centromere"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Two new nuclear envelopes must reassemble around the genetic material as the chromosomes decondense into chromatin"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).'' The cluster of observed events—nuclear envelope fragmenting, nucleolus disappearing, chromatids condensing into visible bodies, and centrosomes migrating apart with microtubules extending—identifies the cell as being in prophase, the first phase of mitosis. The textbook notes that near the end of prophase the nuclear membrane has disintegrated and "the microtubules attach themselves to the centromeres that adjoin pairs of sister chromatids," where "the kinetochore is a protein structure on the centromere that is the point of attachment between the mitotic spindle and the sister chromatids." Only after this attachment can the chromatids be maneuvered into the metaphase plate. (Choice A) is correct: kinetochore–microtubule attachment is the transition event (late prophase/prometaphase) that must happen before the chromosomes can align midway between the poles at metaphase. (Choice B) is incorrect: cohesin degradation and separation of sister chromatids at the centromere define anaphase, which occurs only after alignment at metaphase, not next. (Choice C) is incorrect: DNA replication occurs during S phase of interphase and is already complete—the stem states the chromatids are duplicated—so it cannot be the next event. (Choice D) is incorrect: reassembly of nuclear envelopes around decondensing chromatin defines telophase, the final mitotic stage, which is far downstream of the observed prophase events. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your knowledge of the ordered sequence of mitotic events to determine which step follows the observed prophase changes and enables metaphase alignment.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '14d0199e-2b0e-4808-8ac2-16f5a9a7fa98';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '14d0199e-2b0e-4808-8ac2-16f5a9a7fa98';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('14d0199e-2b0e-4808-8ac2-16f5a9a7fa98', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct event from a later mitotic stage (anaphase) presented as the immediate next step'),
  ('14d0199e-2b0e-4808-8ac2-16f5a9a7fa98', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'a prerequisite that already occurred (S-phase replication) offered as the next event'),
  ('14d0199e-2b0e-4808-8ac2-16f5a9a7fa98', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'a real but far-downstream mitotic event (telophase envelope reassembly)');

-- [Mitotic Structures] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a dividing cell, a researcher inactivates one mitotic component. Afterward, the two centrosomes still separate and nucleate a bipolar spindle, and the sister chromatids remain paired, but the chromosomes never become tethered to the spindle and fail to align at the metaphase plate. Which component was most likely inactivated?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The kinetochore"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The centromere"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The cohesin complex"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The centriole"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).'' During prometaphase, spindle microtubules from the poles must physically capture the chromosomes; the textbook states that "The kinetochore is a protein structure on the centromere that is the point of attachment between the mitotic spindle and the sister chromatids." Because it is the kinetochore—not the centromere DNA it sits on—that microtubules bind, losing only the kinetochore removes the chromosome-to-spindle linkage while leaving the poles, the spindle, and the sister-chromatid pairing untouched. (Choice A) is correct: with the kinetochore gone, microtubules have nothing to grip, so chromosomes cannot be tethered or aligned even though the bipolar spindle forms and the chromatids stay paired—exactly the described phenotype. (Choice B) is incorrect: the centromere is the DNA region that joins the two sister chromatids and on which the kinetochore assembles; if the centromere itself were lost the sisters would not remain paired, which contradicts the observation that they "remain paired." (Choice C) is incorrect: cohesin is the protein that holds sister chromatids together, so inactivating it would cause the chromatids to separate prematurely rather than stay paired, and it does not mediate microtubule attachment. (Choice D) is incorrect: centrioles form the centrosomes from which the spindle emanates; if they were disabled the bipolar spindle would fail to form, yet the scenario states the spindle assembled normally. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinct roles of each mitotic structure to deduce which single loss produces spindle-intact, pairing-intact, attachment-failed chromosomes.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd90e5a06-3d3d-4d92-afa6-8525232bd48a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd90e5a06-3d3d-4d92-afa6-8525232bd48a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d90e5a06-3d3d-4d92-afa6-8525232bd48a', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflating the centromere DNA locus with the kinetochore protein complex that binds microtubules'),
  ('d90e5a06-3d3d-4d92-afa6-8525232bd48a', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'cohesin loss would separate chromatids (opposite of the observed pairing) rather than block attachment'),
  ('d90e5a06-3d3d-4d92-afa6-8525232bd48a', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'centrioles/centrosomes nucleate the spindle poles, but the scenario says the spindle formed normally');

-- [Sexual Development & Puberty] ans=B medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During puberty, the sensitivity of the reproductive axis to negative feedback declines, and the secretion of FSH and LH from the anterior pituitary progressively increases. Both are released together because a single upstream hormone binds receptors on the anterior pituitary and directly drives their secretion. Which hormone occupies this position and directly stimulates the pituitary to release both FSH and LH?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Luteinizing hormone (LH)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Leptin"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Testosterone"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO).'' In the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis, pulsatile gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) binds receptors on the anterior pituitary and directly stimulates the co-release of both gonadotropins, luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH); the gonadal sex steroids then feed back to modulate this drive. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e states that "Binding of GnRH to its receptors on the anterior pituitary gland stimulates release of the two gonadotropins: luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)," and that at puberty a "decrease of sensitivity in the hypothalamus and pituitary to negative feedback" allows "the levels of LH and FSH slowly increase." The correct choice is the hormone that sits directly upstream of the pituitary and drives both gonadotropins, not one that lies downstream or acts only as a permissive input. (Choice A) is incorrect: LH is itself one of the two gonadotropins the pituitary releases in response to the upstream signal, so it is an output of this step, not the hormone that binds the pituitary to trigger gonadotropin release. (Choice B) is correct: GnRH is the hormone that binds anterior pituitary receptors and directly stimulates secretion of both FSH and LH, placing it at the apex of the axis. (Choice C) is incorrect: leptin, secreted by adipose tissue, is a permissive metabolic signal linked to the timing of puberty onset and menarche, but it does not bind the anterior pituitary to directly evoke FSH and LH secretion. (Choice D) is incorrect: testosterone is a gonadal steroid that lies downstream of the pituitary and, at high concentrations, feeds back to inhibit GnRH and gonadotropin secretion rather than directly stimulating their release. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the causal ordering of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis and identify which hormone directly binds the anterior pituitary to drive both gonadotropins.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = '63233740-ba0c-47b4-8639-f08dd425882d';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '63233740-ba0c-47b4-8639-f08dd425882d';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('63233740-ba0c-47b4-8639-f08dd425882d', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'Confusing a downstream output (a gonadotropin) with the upstream driver of the axis'),
  ('63233740-ba0c-47b4-8639-f08dd425882d', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'Selecting a genuine but permissive/timing-related puberty signal instead of the direct pituitary driver'),
  ('63233740-ba0c-47b4-8639-f08dd425882d', 'D', 'misconception', 'Treating a downstream gonadal steroid feedback signal as if it stimulates gonadotropin release');

-- [Cleavage] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A developmental biologist collects an early sea urchin embryo at the 4-cell stage. Using a fine glass needle, she separates the four blastomeres and cultures each one in isolation. She then predicts the developmental outcome of the four isolated cells.',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Each isolated blastomere develops into a complete, normally proportioned larva, producing a set of genetically identical organisms."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Each isolated blastomere develops into a partial larva missing the structures that the other three blastomeres were fated to form."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Only the blastomere containing the vegetal cytoplasmic determinants develops into a larva, while the other three arrest."}, {"label": "D", "text": "None of the isolated blastomeres develops, because each requires ongoing contact with its neighbors to continue dividing."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' The sea urchin is an echinoderm, and echinoderms are deuterostomes, which undergo indeterminate cleavage: at this early stage the blastomeres "are not yet fully committed... to develop into specific cell types." The textbook notes that "Removal of individual blastomeres from these embryos does not result in the loss of embryonic structures," and that "twins (clones) can be produced as a result from blastomeres that have been separated from the original mass of blastomere cells." To answer, you must first identify the sea urchin''s lineage and then apply the cleavage property that follows from it. (Choice A) is correct: because each blastomere of an indeterminate-cleavage deuterostome retains the capacity to build every cell type, each isolated cell can regenerate a whole larva, and since all four derive from one zygote the products are genetically identical clones. (Choice B) is incorrect: partial larvae missing complementary structures result from determinate cleavage, which occurs in protostomes, where "Removal of a blastomere... can result in missing structures, and embryos that fail to develop"—the opposite of the sea urchin''s cleavage type. (Choice C) is incorrect: localized cytoplasmic determinants that make only one blastomere competent describe a determinate (mosaic) pattern, but in indeterminate cleavage no single blastomere monopolizes the ability to form an embryo, so the other three do not arrest. (Choice D) is incorrect: isolated deuterostome blastomeres continue to divide on their own—separation does not halt cleavage—and in fact "adjacent cells are able to compensate" when cells are lost, showing the cells are not dependent on continuous neighbor contact to proceed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the property of indeterminate cleavage—inferred from the sea urchin''s deuterostome lineage—to a novel separation experiment and predict its outcome.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '3b27a2da-6cfe-4914-9021-fb8382660eaf';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3b27a2da-6cfe-4914-9021-fb8382660eaf';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3b27a2da-6cfe-4914-9021-fb8382660eaf', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'Applies the protostome (determinate) cleavage outcome to a deuterostome embryo'),
  ('3b27a2da-6cfe-4914-9021-fb8382660eaf', 'C', 'misconception', 'Assumes localized cytoplasmic determinants (mosaic development) restrict competence to one blastomere'),
  ('3b27a2da-6cfe-4914-9021-fb8382660eaf', 'D', 'misconception', 'Believes early blastomeres cannot divide without continuous neighbor signaling');

-- [Fertilization] ans=A medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Within about a second of the first sperm fusing with a human oocyte''s plasma membrane, and roughly a minute before any structural change to the egg''s glycoprotein coat occurs, additional sperm can no longer fuse with the oocyte. This earliest, transient block to polyspermy is produced by which change at the oocyte plasma membrane?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A rapid influx of sodium ions raises the membrane potential toward zero (depolarization), and the depolarized membrane can no longer fuse with additional sperm."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A rapid efflux of potassium ions drives the membrane potential more negative (hyperpolarization), and the hyperpolarized membrane can no longer fuse with additional sperm."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Cortical granules just beneath the membrane fuse with it and release zonal inhibiting proteins into the space outside the oocyte, detaching bound sperm."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Acrosomal enzymes released at the point of contact digest the glycoprotein coat, and the softened coat can no longer admit additional sperm."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' The fast block to polyspermy is the "wave of negativity" first described by Ernest Everett Just: within about a second of the first sperm binding, the oocyte''s membrane potential changes and additional sperm can no longer fuse, well before the slower structural changes to the egg coat set in. Mechanistically this fast block is a depolarization—an influx of positive ions (sodium) that shifts the membrane potential upward from its resting negative value—and the depolarized membrane is unreceptive to further sperm fusion. (Choice A) is correct: an inward sodium current depolarizes the oocyte membrane almost instantly, and this depolarization is the fast block itself, blocking further fusion within the first second. (Choice B) is incorrect: it is also a fast, membrane-potential change, but it moves in the wrong direction—the fast block is a depolarization (membrane potential rising toward zero), not a hyperpolarization; potassium efflux driving the potential more negative is not the mechanism that prevents polyspermy. (Choice C) is incorrect: cortical granule fusion and release of zonal inhibiting proteins is the slow block (the cortical reaction), which alters the egg coat over the minute that follows and is not the near-instantaneous first response. (Choice D) is incorrect: acrosomal enzyme release degrades the glycoprotein coat to let a sperm INTO the oocyte, so it promotes fusion rather than blocking additional sperm. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the fast block to polyspermy is a rapid membrane depolarization driven by cation influx, and distinguish that specific ionic event from a hyperpolarization, the cortical reaction, and the acrosomal reaction.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = '52a652cb-8719-4cc5-b0a5-7f28eb642f48';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '52a652cb-8719-4cc5-b0a5-7f28eb642f48';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('52a652cb-8719-4cc5-b0a5-7f28eb642f48', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'Correct that the fast block is a fast electrical/membrane-potential event but reverses its direction (hyperpolarization via K+ efflux instead of depolarization via Na+ influx)'),
  ('52a652cb-8719-4cc5-b0a5-7f28eb642f48', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'Confuses the fast block with the slow block (cortical reaction / zonal inhibiting proteins)'),
  ('52a652cb-8719-4cc5-b0a5-7f28eb642f48', 'D', 'misconception', 'Confuses a sperm-admitting event (acrosomal reaction degrading the zona) with a sperm-blocking event');

-- [Cleavage] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A developmental biologist removes a single blastomere from an eight-cell embryo of species X and lets the rest develop. The resulting embryo is missing several structures, and many such embryos fail to develop at all. In a separate trial, she gently separates two blastomeres of a species-X embryo from each other and cultures each one alone. What is the most likely result of this second trial, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Neither isolated blastomere forms a complete embryo, because in this species each blastomere is already committed to only part of the body plan."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Each isolated blastomere develops into a separate complete embryo, producing identical twins, because early blastomeres in this species remain uncommitted."}, {"label": "C", "text": "One blastomere develops normally while the other stops dividing, because only the larger of the two retains a full set of maternal determinants."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Both blastomeres fuse back together and resume normal development, because separated early cells re-establish contact to restore the original cell number."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Early Embryonic Development.'' The first trial''s outcome—removing one blastomere leaves the embryo with missing structures or unable to develop—is the diagnostic signature of determinate (mosaic) cleavage, in which the developmental fate of each blastomere is already fixed and no other cell can take over its assigned contribution. The text states that in determinate cleavage "the developmental fate of each embryonic cell is already determined" and "a given cell does not have the ability to develop into any cell type other than its original destination," so "removal of a blastomere from an embryo with determinate cleavage can result in missing structures, and embryos that fail to develop." Because species X shows this signature, its early cells are already committed; twinning and compensation are properties the text assigns instead to indeterminate cleavage, so they cannot occur here. (Choice A) is correct: since each blastomere is already committed to only part of the body plan, an isolated blastomere carries only its share of the developmental program, so neither separated cell can build the whole animal—consistent with the deficient outcome seen when a blastomere is removed. (Choice B) is incorrect: separated blastomeres forming identical twins is a hallmark of indeterminate cleavage, where the text says "twins (clones) can be produced" from separated blastomeres because the cells are "not yet fully committed"; the first trial rules this out for species X. (Choice C) is incorrect: fate in determinate cleavage is set by committed developmental programs, not by which fragment inherits more cytoplasm, and the text does not describe survival being decided by relative cell size; both cells would be equally deficient rather than one being fully normal. (Choice D) is incorrect: separated blastomeres do not fuse and reconstitute the original embryo; cleavage subdivides the egg into ever-smaller cells with no mechanism described for re-merging to restore the whole, so this outcome is not predicted for either cleavage type. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the diagnostic behavior of determinate cleavage (loss of structures after blastomere removal) to infer the cleavage type and then predict a second, different experimental outcome for that same type.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd56ce092-5f7f-4b5c-8895-6a33d813f877';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd56ce092-5f7f-4b5c-8895-6a33d813f877';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d56ce092-5f7f-4b5c-8895-6a33d813f877', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'attributes indeterminate-cleavage twinning to a determinate-cleavage embryo'),
  ('d56ce092-5f7f-4b5c-8895-6a33d813f877', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes cell fate/survival is set by cytoplasmic volume or cell size rather than commitment'),
  ('d56ce092-5f7f-4b5c-8895-6a33d813f877', 'D', 'misconception', 'imagines separated early cells re-fuse to restore the original embryo');

-- [The Menstrual Cycle] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Midway through the follicular phase, rising estradiol from the developing follicles triggers negative feedback that lowers circulating gonadotropin levels. As a result, all but one of the original cohort of five or six tertiary follicles undergo atresia and die, while a single dominant follicle survives. A decline in which anterior pituitary gonadotropin most directly accounts for the death of these non-dominant follicles?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "GnRH, because its withdrawal from the anterior pituitary halts the release of all downstream reproductive hormones"}, {"label": "B", "text": "FSH, because the tertiary follicles depend on it to keep growing and surviving at this stage"}, {"label": "C", "text": "LH, because without it the granulosa and theca cells can no longer support the follicles"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Progesterone, because falling levels remove the trophic support the follicles need to persist"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO).'' During the follicular phase, FSH and LH from the anterior pituitary bind granulosa and theca cells, but they have distinct roles: FSH drives follicular growth and survival, whereas LH drives estradiol production. As estradiol rises, negative feedback reduces gonadotropin output, and because the large tertiary follicles specifically require FSH to grow and survive, the fall in FSH is what starves most of them and causes atresia. (Choice A) is incorrect: GnRH withdrawal does reduce both FSH and LH, but it acts on the anterior pituitary rather than on the follicles, and it is not itself an anterior pituitary gonadotropin as the question requires; the text pinpoints the follicles'' dependence on FSH specifically, not on GnRH. (Choice B) is correct: the text states that the large tertiary follicles "require FSH to grow and survive at this point," so "this decline in FSH caused by negative feedback leads most of them to die (atresia)," leaving one dominant follicle. (Choice C) is incorrect: LH acts on the same granulosa and theca cells, but its role is to stimulate them to "produce the sex steroid hormone estradiol," not to sustain follicle growth and survival, so a drop in LH does not directly explain the atresia. (Choice D) is incorrect: progesterone is not an anterior pituitary gonadotropin; it is secreted by the corpus luteum during the luteal phase and actually keeps "GnRH, LH, and FSH secretions low," so it suppresses rather than supports follicular development. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the negative-feedback mechanism of the follicular phase and distinguish FSH''s growth/survival role from LH''s steroidogenic role to identify which gonadotropin''s decline causes atresia.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b495dbb5-86a7-4474-b7e3-64884cb1d4b3';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b495dbb5-86a7-4474-b7e3-64884cb1d4b3';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b495dbb5-86a7-4474-b7e3-64884cb1d4b3', 'A', 'misconception', 'Blames the upstream master hormone (GnRH) for a specific downstream effect the text attributes to FSH'),
  ('b495dbb5-86a7-4474-b7e3-64884cb1d4b3', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'Attributes FSH''s growth/survival role to LH, which acts on the same cells but drives estradiol production instead'),
  ('b495dbb5-86a7-4474-b7e3-64884cb1d4b3', 'D', 'misconception', 'Treats progesterone (a luteal-phase corpus luteum hormone, not a gonadotropin) as a follicular trophic factor');

-- [Ectoderm Derivatives] ans=B easy skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Human skin contains an epidermis with its keratinized appendages (hair and nails) as well as an underlying dermis of dense connective tissue. A developmental biologist wants to identify a group of structures that all share a single embryonic germ-layer origin. Which grouping consists entirely of derivatives of the same primary germ layer?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The dermis, hair, and nails"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The epidermis, hair, and nails"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The epidermis, dermis, and hair"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The dermis, skeletal muscle, and nails"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' During gastrulation the three primary germ layers each become committed to specific tissues: the ectoderm gives rise to the nervous system and the epidermis, the mesoderm gives rise to muscle and connective tissues, and the endoderm gives rise to the epithelial linings of the digestive and respiratory tracts (OpenStax Biology 2e: "The ectoderm gives rise to the nervous system and the epidermis. The mesoderm gives rise to the muscle cells and connective tissue... The endoderm gives rise to columnar cells found in the digestive system"). Because hair and nails are keratinized appendages of the epidermis, they share the epidermis''s ectodermal origin, whereas the dermis is a connective tissue and is therefore mesodermal. (Choice A) is incorrect: hair and nails are ectodermal, but the dermis is a connective tissue derived from mesoderm, so this grouping spans two different germ layers rather than one. (Choice B) is correct: the epidermis is ectodermal, and hair and nails are keratinized epidermal appendages that arise from that same ectodermal layer, giving a single shared origin. (Choice C) is incorrect: the epidermis and hair are ectodermal, but the dermis is mesodermal connective tissue, so mixing it in breaks the single-layer requirement. (Choice D) is incorrect: the dermis and skeletal muscle are both mesodermal, but nails are ectodermal epidermal appendages, so this grouping again combines derivatives of two germ layers. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the specific germ-layer origin of the epidermis, its appendages, and the dermis, and apply the distinction between ectodermal covering and mesodermal connective tissue to sort the structures.',
  difficulty = 'easy',
  cognitive_skill = 1
