-- [Endoderm Derivatives] ans=C medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher confirms that the simple epithelium lining the lumen of the small intestine originates from the innermost of the three primary germ layers formed during gastrulation. Which additional adult structure develops from that SAME germ layer?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The epidermis of the skin, which covers the outer body surface"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The smooth muscle layer that surrounds and propels contents through the intestinal wall"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The epithelium that lines the trachea, bronchi, and alveoli of the lungs"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The melanocytes and peripheral sensory neurons distributed throughout the body"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' The innermost germ layer identified in the stem is the endoderm, which forms the lining of the digestive tract (including the stomach, intestines, liver, and pancreas) and also the lining of the trachea, bronchi, and lungs of the respiratory tract. Answering requires knowing which other structures share that endodermal origin, rather than which structures are anatomically near or grossly similar to the intestine. (Choice A) is incorrect: the epidermis of the skin is the outer epithelial covering of the body surface and arises from the ectoderm, the outermost germ layer, not the endoderm — being an epithelium is not enough to make a tissue endodermal. (Choice B) is incorrect: the muscular wall of the intestine is a trap because it belongs to the same organ as the gut lining, but specialized muscle tissue (including the muscles of the intestines) is a mesoderm derivative, not endoderm. (Choice C) is correct: the endoderm gives rise to the lining of the digestive tract and to the lining of the trachea, bronchi, and lungs, so the respiratory epithelium shares the same germ-layer origin as the small-intestinal lining. (Choice D) is incorrect: melanocytes and peripheral sensory neurons derive from the neural crest, an ectoderm-derived migratory population, not from the endoderm. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the specific adult structures that each primary germ layer produces and match a second structure to the endodermal lineage.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = 'e8a67e17-2993-47f3-8c99-43222d4dab6f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e8a67e17-2993-47f3-8c99-43222d4dab6f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e8a67e17-2993-47f3-8c99-43222d4dab6f', 'A', 'misconception', 'epithelium-implies-endoderm (any epithelial tissue must come from the innermost layer)'),
  ('e8a67e17-2993-47f3-8c99-43222d4dab6f', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'same-organ trap (intestinal muscle is part of the gut, so assumed to share the gut lining''s origin)'),
  ('e8a67e17-2993-47f3-8c99-43222d4dab6f', 'D', 'misconception', 'neural-crest-as-endoderm (migratory cells mistaken for endodermal rather than ectodermal derivatives)');

-- [Neurulation & Neural Crest] ans=A easy skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher working with frog embryos surgically removes the notochord just before neurulation begins, leaving the overlying ectoderm and all other tissues intact. Assuming the surgery itself causes no other damage, which outcome is the most likely direct consequence?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The overlying ectoderm fails to roll up into a neural tube, so no brain or spinal cord forms at that position."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A normal neural tube still forms, because the central ectoderm folds into a tube on its own without any signal from underlying tissue."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The neural tube forms normally, but the flanking somites fail to appear because the notochord''s only role is organizing the paraxial mesoderm."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The endoderm-derived gut tube fails to close, because the notochord supplies the dorsal signal that drives gut formation."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' During organogenesis, the mesoderm-derived notochord lies beneath the dorsal ectoderm and provides the inductive signal that causes the overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate and then roll up into the neural tube; OpenStax Biology 2e states that in vertebrates the notochord "is present only during embryonic development, at which time it induces the development of the neural tube." Removing the inducer before neurulation therefore removes the signal that the ectoderm depends on. (Choice A) is correct: without the notochord''s inductive signal, the overlying ectoderm has nothing to trigger neural-plate formation and folding, so the neural tube—and the brain and spinal cord it would have produced—fails to form at that site. (Choice B) is incorrect: neurulation is not autonomous; the text emphasizes that signaling determines which ectoderm becomes neural tissue, and the notochord is the source of that induction, so folding does not proceed on its own once the inducer is gone. (Choice C) is incorrect: the notochord does help pattern the paraxial mesoderm, but its role is not limited to that—it is precisely the neural tube that depends on notochord induction, so predicting a normal tube reverses the actual dependency. (Choice D) is incorrect: the gut is the endoderm-derived digestive tube, and its closure does not depend on a dorsal notochord signal; the notochord''s inductive target described in the text is the ectoderm above it, not the endoderm below. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the notochord''s role as the inducer of the neural tube to predict the outcome of removing it before neurulation.',
  difficulty = 'easy',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'dd63b20b-e4b7-4dbb-87d6-c0ab3a123274';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'dd63b20b-e4b7-4dbb-87d6-c0ab3a123274';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('dd63b20b-e4b7-4dbb-87d6-c0ab3a123274', 'B', 'misconception', 'neurulation is autonomous / self-driven without induction'),
  ('dd63b20b-e4b7-4dbb-87d6-c0ab3a123274', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'notochord patterns mesoderm but spares the neural tube (dependency reversed)'),
  ('dd63b20b-e4b7-4dbb-87d6-c0ab3a123274', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct germ-layer identity of gut, wrong inductive target (endoderm instead of ectoderm)');

-- [Fetal Hemoglobin & Oxygen Transfer] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'At the placenta, maternal and fetal blood flow past each other separated by a thin barrier, and oxygen must diffuse from the maternal side into the fetal side even though the oxygen partial pressure in the maternal blood reaching the placenta is already relatively low. Which property of fetal blood best explains why oxygen still moves reliably from the maternal circulation into the fetal circulation at this site?',
  options = '[{"text": "Fetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than maternal hemoglobin, so at a given oxygen partial pressure fetal blood reaches a greater oxygen saturation than maternal blood.", "label": "A"}, {"text": "Fetal hemoglobin has a lower affinity for oxygen than maternal hemoglobin, so it releases oxygen more readily into the placental tissue.", "label": "B"}, {"text": "Fetal blood carries a higher concentration of red blood cells than maternal blood, so it holds more total oxygen once the blood volumes are equalized.", "label": "C"}, {"text": "Maternal and fetal blood merge into a shared pool at the placenta, so already-oxygenated maternal blood flows directly into the fetal vessels.", "label": "D"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' At the placenta the maternal and fetal circulations remain separate, so oxygen crosses the placental barrier by diffusion rather than by mixing; because the oxygen dissociation curve shows saturation as a function of oxygen partial pressure, the blood whose hemoglobin has the higher affinity is more saturated at any given partial pressure. Fetal hemoglobin (two alpha and two gamma chains) has a left-shifted dissociation curve, giving it a higher oxygen affinity than adult maternal hemoglobin. (Choice A) is correct: because fetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity, at the modest oxygen partial pressure available in the placenta the fetal blood becomes more saturated than the maternal blood, which pulls oxygen across the barrier from maternal to fetal blood. (Choice B) is incorrect: a lower affinity than maternal hemoglobin would leave fetal blood less saturated than maternal blood at the same partial pressure and would favor oxygen moving the wrong way, out of the fetus; releasing oxygen more readily is the property needed at the tissues, not for loading at the placenta. (Choice C) is incorrect: although fetal blood does have a relatively high red-cell count, total oxygen content at equalized volumes does not set the direction of diffusion; oxygen moves according to partial pressure and relative saturation at the exchange surface, not according to how much total oxygen each side could theoretically hold. (Choice D) is incorrect: maternal and fetal blood do not merge into a shared pool; they are kept apart by the placental barrier and exchange gases only by diffusion, so no direct flow of maternal blood into fetal vessels occurs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the oxygen-affinity/dissociation-curve relationship to determine why the higher affinity of fetal hemoglobin drives oxygen from maternal to fetal blood across the placental barrier.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'be25794d-51d6-4fba-8896-a1ee1cb3eb5f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'be25794d-51d6-4fba-8896-a1ee1cb3eb5f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('be25794d-51d6-4fba-8896-a1ee1cb3eb5f', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'Flips the affinity relationship so oxygen would move the wrong direction'),
  ('be25794d-51d6-4fba-8896-a1ee1cb3eb5f', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'Cites a real property of fetal blood (high red-cell count) that does not govern the direction of diffusive transfer'),
  ('be25794d-51d6-4fba-8896-a1ee1cb3eb5f', 'D', 'misconception', 'Common belief that maternal and fetal blood mix directly at the placenta');

-- [Support Glia: Astrocytes, Ependymal & Microglia] ans=D medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During a viral infection that spreads into the brain, a researcher uses lineage tracing and finds that one population of central nervous system (CNS) glial cells did not arise from the neural tube like the surrounding glia, but instead descends from the same blood-borne cell lineage that gives rise to tissue macrophages elsewhere in the body. Which CNS glial cell has this origin?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Astrocytes, the star-shaped glia that buffer extracellular ion and neurotransmitter concentrations and help form the blood-brain barrier"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ependymal cells, the ciliated glia that line the ventricles and filter blood at the choroid plexus to produce cerebrospinal fluid"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Oligodendrocytes, the glia whose processes wrap multiple CNS axon segments to provide the myelin insulation that speeds conduction"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Microglia, the CNS-resident glia that carry out immune surveillance and clear cellular debris within the nervous system"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.'' The CNS contains four glial types—astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, ependymal cells, and microglia—but only microglia are thought to originate from the immune-cell (macrophage/leukocyte) lineage rather than from the neuroectoderm that produces the other glia. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e states that microglia "may originate as white blood cells, called macrophages, that become part of the CNS during early development," and that leukocytes leaving the bloodstream are "often given distinct names, such as macrophage or microglia, depending on their function," which is why microglia are called CNS-resident macrophages. The lineage clue in the stem—descent from the blood-borne macrophage line rather than the neural tube—singles out microglia. (Choice A) is incorrect: astrocytes are neural-tube-derived support cells that maintain the extracellular chemical environment and contribute to the blood-brain barrier; their described function is real, but they do not share the immune-cell lineage the stem specifies. (Choice B) is incorrect: ependymal cells are neuroectoderm-derived, line the ventricles, and filter blood at the choroid plexus to make cerebrospinal fluid, a fluid-handling role with a non-immune origin. (Choice C) is incorrect: oligodendrocytes arise from neural precursors and insulate CNS axons in myelin; the described insulation function is accurate but does not match the blood-borne origin. (Choice D) is correct: microglia are the CNS glia derived from the blood-borne immune-cell lineage and serve as the nervous system''s resident immune cells that perform surveillance and clear debris, matching the origin described in the stem. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the distinct developmental origin and immune identity that separate microglia from the other three CNS glial cell types.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = 'bbf847a0-d12b-449c-bc4f-22764e8b81cd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'bbf847a0-d12b-449c-bc4f-22764e8b81cd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('bbf847a0-d12b-449c-bc4f-22764e8b81cd', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct function for the wrong glial cell — astrocytes buffer the extracellular environment and form the BBB but are neural-tube-derived, not immune-lineage'),
  ('bbf847a0-d12b-449c-bc4f-22764e8b81cd', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'accurate ependymal fluid-handling role paired with a non-immune neuroectodermal origin'),
  ('bbf847a0-d12b-449c-bc4f-22764e8b81cd', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'accurate oligodendrocyte myelination role, but neural-precursor origin rather than blood-borne');

-- [Repolarization & Hyperpolarization] ans=D hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher perfuses a neuron with ouabain, which fully and irreversibly blocks the Na+/K+ ATPase while leaving the voltage-gated Na+ channels, voltage-gated K+ channels, and K+ leakage channels intact. Immediately afterward, a single suprathreshold stimulus is delivered, and then the neuron is stimulated repeatedly at a high rate. Which prediction is correct?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The rising phase of the first spike is normal, but the membrane cannot fall below roughly -50 mV and the hyperpolarizing overshoot is abolished, because the pump''s net export of positive charge is what carries the voltage through the final part of the falling phase; repeated firing is otherwise sustained."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Even the very first spike fails to repolarize and the membrane locks near +30 mV, because the Na+ that flooded in during the rising phase can no longer be cleared, and that Na+ must be removed before the voltage can fall."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The first spike is normal and high-rate repeated firing continues indefinitely, because the K+ leakage channels alone return Na+ and K+ to their original sides of the membrane between spikes, making the pump unnecessary for sustained activity."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The first spike shows a normal falling phase and hyperpolarizing overshoot, but as firing is repeated the Na+ and K+ gradients are progressively dissipated and not rebuilt, so successive spikes shrink and firing eventually fails."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''The Action Potential.'' The falling phase of an action potential is produced by passive K+ efflux through voltage-gated K+ channels, and the brief hyperpolarizing overshoot arises because those channels are delayed in closing — the text notes that as K+ leaves the cell "the membrane potential begins to move back toward its resting voltage. This is called repolarization," and that "those K+ channels are slightly delayed in closing, accounting for this short overshoot." The Na+/K+ ATPase does not shape any single spike; its job is slow restoration, since "after the repolarizing phase of the action potential, K+ leakage channels and the Na+/K+ pump ensure that the ions return to their original locations." Because these ionic gradients change only slightly per spike, one action potential fires normally even with the pump blocked, but without the pump those dissipated gradients cannot be rebuilt, so sustained high-rate firing runs them down. (Choice A) is incorrect: the final part of the falling phase and the overshoot are driven by continued K+ efflux, not by the pump''s small electrogenic current, so blocking the pump does not truncate repolarization or erase the overshoot of a single spike. (Choice B) is incorrect: repolarization does not require removing the Na+ that entered — Na+ entry is halted by the voltage-gated Na+ channel''s inactivation gate, and the fall is carried by K+ leaving, so the first spike repolarizes normally without the pump. (Choice C) is incorrect: leakage channels move ions down their gradients and thereby dissipate them; only the ATP-driven pump moves Na+ and K+ back against their gradients, so leak channels cannot substitute for it and repeated firing cannot be sustained indefinitely. (Choice D) is correct: the single spike''s passive K+-driven falling phase and overshoot are unaffected, while only the pump-dependent restoration of the gradients — and thus sustained repeated firing — is lost. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between the passive, channel-driven ionic basis of a single action potential and the active, pump-driven restoration of gradients to predict how a pump block differentially affects one spike versus repeated firing.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f272f205-5d82-4ab5-9136-0e98530f0afd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f272f205-5d82-4ab5-9136-0e98530f0afd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f272f205-5d82-4ab5-9136-0e98530f0afd', 'A', 'misconception', 'Overrating the electrogenic pump''s role in shaping a single spike''s falling phase and overshoot'),
  ('f272f205-5d82-4ab5-9136-0e98530f0afd', 'B', 'misconception', 'Believing repolarization requires the pump to remove entered Na+ rather than K+ efflux plus inactivation-gate closure'),
  ('f272f205-5d82-4ab5-9136-0e98530f0afd', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'Treating passive leak channels as able to rebuild gradients, making the pump unnecessary for sustained firing');

-- [Neurotransmitter Release] ans=B easy skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'An action potential arrives at a presynaptic axon terminal, and moments later the neurotransmitter-filled synaptic vesicles fuse with the presynaptic membrane and empty into the synaptic cleft. The event that most directly couples the arriving action potential to this vesicle fusion is the:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "efflux of K+ out of the terminal through voltage-gated potassium channels, restoring the resting membrane potential"}, {"label": "B", "text": "influx of Ca2+ into the terminal through voltage-gated calcium channels that open when the membrane depolarizes"}, {"label": "C", "text": "influx of Na+ into the terminal through voltage-gated sodium channels that carried the depolarization down the axon"}, {"label": "D", "text": "influx of Cl- into the terminal through ligand-gated chloride channels, driving the membrane away from threshold"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Impulse Propagation & Synapses.'' Neurotransmitter release is not triggered directly by the depolarization itself but by a specific ion that enters the terminal when the depolarization arrives. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e states that "when an action potential reaches the axon terminals, voltage-gated Ca2+ channels in the membrane of the synaptic end bulb open," and that "the Ca2+ facilitates the merging of the vesicle with the presynaptic membrane so that the neurotransmitter is released through exocytosis into the ... synaptic cleft." Thus the depolarizing action potential opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, and the resulting rise in intracellular Ca2+ is the signal that binds vesicle proteins and drives fusion. (Choice A) is incorrect: K+ efflux through voltage-gated potassium channels repolarizes the membrane back toward rest, terminating the depolarization rather than triggering vesicle fusion. (Choice B) is correct: depolarization of the terminal opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, and the influx of Ca2+ is what directly causes the vesicles to fuse with the presynaptic membrane and release transmitter. (Choice C) is incorrect: Na+ influx through voltage-gated sodium channels propagates the action potential along the axon and depolarizes the terminal, but it is the calcium that enters afterward—not the sodium itself—that couples that depolarization to fusion. (Choice D) is incorrect: Cl- entry is a hyperpolarizing/inhibitory event at postsynaptic membranes that moves the membrane away from threshold, and it neither opens in response to presynaptic depolarization here nor triggers release. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that presynaptic depolarization opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels and that the Ca2+ influx is the specific trigger for synaptic vesicle fusion.',
  difficulty = 'easy',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = '7d1fe515-2d67-488c-b94c-d08216e46a2b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '7d1fe515-2d67-488c-b94c-d08216e46a2b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('7d1fe515-2d67-488c-b94c-d08216e46a2b', 'A', 'partial_truth', 'real synaptic ion event (K+ efflux repolarization) that is not the fusion trigger'),
  ('7d1fe515-2d67-488c-b94c-d08216e46a2b', 'C', 'misconception', 'conflating the action potential''s Na+ current with the release trigger'),
  ('7d1fe515-2d67-488c-b94c-d08216e46a2b', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'Cl- influx is a real inhibitory postsynaptic event misplaced as the presynaptic release trigger');

-- [Saltatory Conduction] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A myelinated motor axon conducts an action potential toward its terminal. Voltage-gated Na+ channels are clustered at the nodes of Ranvier, and the internodal membrane is wrapped in myelin. Considering what happens to the signal in the region between two adjacent nodes, why must the nodes be spaced no farther apart than a certain distance?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Beneath the myelin the depolarization spreads passively and grows weaker with distance, so it must still exceed threshold when it reaches the next node''s Na+ channels"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The signal crosses each internode instantaneously with no loss of strength, so spacing is limited only by how tightly the myelin can be wrapped"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Voltage-gated Na+ channels beneath the myelin continuously regenerate the signal, so it would never weaken no matter how far apart the nodes were placed"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The myelin actively amplifies the signal between the nodes, and spacing them too far would overload this boosting capacity"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Impulse Propagation & Synapses.'' In a myelinated axon, voltage-gated Na+ channels are found only at the nodes of Ranvier; the internodal membrane beneath the myelin has no such channels, so it cannot regenerate the impulse. The OpenStax A&P text explains that Na+ entering at a node spreads along the inside of the axon but "the charge starts to dissipate," and that the internode distance "is the optimal distance to keep the membrane still depolarized above threshold at the next node." (Choice A) is correct: between nodes the depolarization propagates passively (electrotonically) and decays with distance, so if it is to trigger the next node''s Na+ channels it must still be above threshold on arrival — the text notes that if the node "were any farther down the axon, that depolarization would have fallen off too much for voltage-gated Na+ channels to be activated." (Choice B) is incorrect: although saltatory conduction is described as the impulse seeming to "jump," the internodal signal is not lossless — the depolarization actively dissipates as charge leaks, which is precisely why node spacing is constrained. (Choice C) is incorrect: continuous, channel-based regeneration along the whole membrane describes an unmyelinated axon; beneath the myelin "there are no voltage-gated Na+ channels," so no regeneration occurs between nodes. (Choice D) is incorrect: myelin is an electrically passive insulator, not an amplifier; it reduces charge leakage but cannot generate or boost the signal. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that internodal conduction is passive and decremental to explain why node spacing has an upper limit.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '14fe7d36-6831-49a0-9342-a94a063bd094';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '14fe7d36-6831-49a0-9342-a94a063bd094';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('14fe7d36-6831-49a0-9342-a94a063bd094', 'B', 'misconception', 'Treats the ''jumping'' of saltatory conduction as literal, lossless instantaneous transmission'),
  ('14fe7d36-6831-49a0-9342-a94a063bd094', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'Applies continuous-conduction (unmyelinated) regeneration to the internodal region, the reverse of the myelinated mechanism'),
  ('14fe7d36-6831-49a0-9342-a94a063bd094', 'D', 'misconception', 'Upgrades the myelin''s passive insulating role into active signal amplification');

-- [Sarcomere Structure] ans=A easy skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A histologist dissects a single skeletal muscle fiber and traces its contractile machinery from the smallest repeating unit up to the whole cell. Which sequence correctly orders these structures from the smallest to the largest, with each structure being a direct building block of the next?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Sarcomere → myofibril → muscle fiber"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Myofibril → sarcomere → muscle fiber"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Muscle fiber → myofibril → sarcomere"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Sarcomere → muscle fiber → myofibril"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The sarcomere is the functional (contractile) unit of skeletal muscle, defined as the region from one Z-disc to the next and built from overlapping thin (actin) and thick (myosin) filaments. Many sarcomeres joined end-to-end form a single long, cylindrical organelle called a myofibril, and hundreds to thousands of myofibrils packed in parallel make up one muscle fiber (the muscle cell). Correctly ordering the hierarchy requires knowing which structure is contained within which, not merely which terms sound anatomical. (Choice A) is correct: the smallest repeating unit is the sarcomere, sarcomeres stack end-to-end to build a myofibril, and many myofibrils fill the muscle fiber, so sarcomere → myofibril → muscle fiber ascends correctly from smallest to largest with each being a direct building block of the next. (Choice B) is incorrect: it swaps the two smallest levels, placing the myofibril inside the sarcomere; in reality the sarcomere is the sub-unit and the myofibril is the larger organelle assembled from many sarcomeres. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the entire hierarchy, listing the whole muscle fiber as the smallest unit, whereas the fiber is the largest of the three and is composed of myofibrils, which are in turn composed of sarcomeres. (Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly starts with the sarcomere but then inserts the whole muscle fiber before the myofibril, implying a fiber is a building block of a single myofibril; instead a myofibril is a sub-cellular organelle nested inside the fiber, so the fiber must come last. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the structural hierarchy in which sarcomeres compose myofibrils, which in turn compose the skeletal muscle fiber.',
  difficulty = 'easy',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = '9dfd0c5a-007a-49ee-8f95-2c5bb1fd1e19';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9dfd0c5a-007a-49ee-8f95-2c5bb1fd1e19';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9dfd0c5a-007a-49ee-8f95-2c5bb1fd1e19', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps the two smallest hierarchy levels (myofibril vs sarcomere)'),
  ('9dfd0c5a-007a-49ee-8f95-2c5bb1fd1e19', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'fully inverts the containment order (largest listed as smallest)'),
  ('9dfd0c5a-007a-49ee-8f95-2c5bb1fd1e19', 'D', 'misconception', 'misplaces the whole cell inside a single organelle');

-- [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two skeletal muscles are compared. In an extraocular muscle that aims the eye, each motor neuron branches to only about six muscle fibers, whereas in a thigh muscle used to extend the knee, each motor neuron branches to roughly a thousand fibers. Assuming both muscles are otherwise typical, what does this difference in the number of fibers per neuron most directly predict about the two muscles?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The extraocular muscle can grade its movements more finely, because activating one neuron recruits far fewer fibers at a time"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The thigh muscle can grade its movements more finely, because each of its neurons commands many fibers at once"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Both muscles grade their movements with equal precision, because every fiber in each muscle still obeys exactly one neuron"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The extraocular muscle can generate the greater maximal force per single contraction, because its neurons fire independently of one another"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' A motor unit is a single motor neuron plus all of the muscle fibers it innervates, and the textbook states that "the number of muscle fibers that are part of a motor unit corresponds to the precision of control of that muscle" (anatomy-physiology, line 26817). Because the smallest step by which the nervous system can change a muscle''s tension is the activation of one additional motor unit, a muscle built from small motor units (few fibers per neuron) changes force in tiny increments and therefore moves with fine precision, whereas a muscle built from large motor units (many fibers per neuron) jumps force in large increments and produces gross, powerful movements. (Choice A) is correct: the extraocular muscle has small motor units (~6 fibers per neuron), so activating one neuron adds only a few fibers of tension at a time, letting the nervous system grade movement finely — the textbook cites extraocular small motor units as the example that "allows for exquisite control of eye movements" (lines 17636-17641). (Choice B) is incorrect because it reverses the relationship: a neuron that commands ~1000 fibers changes force in large, coarse steps, which suits the thigh''s "simple, or ''gross,'' movements, such as powerfully extending the knee joint" (lines 17646-17648), not fine grading. (Choice C) is incorrect because although each fiber is indeed innervated by only one neuron, that shared fact does not equalize precision; precision is set by how many fibers each neuron controls, so the muscle with fewer fibers per neuron is the more finely graded one. (Choice D) is incorrect because maximal force per contraction rises with the number of fibers a neuron activates, so the thigh''s large motor units — not the extraocular muscle''s small ones — generate the greater force; the independence of neuronal firing does not create force. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between motor-unit size and the precision versus force of muscle contraction to two muscles with different innervation ratios.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '37c165ed-54c9-4c34-99f2-42c5aee77a38';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '37c165ed-54c9-4c34-99f2-42c5aee77a38';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('37c165ed-54c9-4c34-99f2-42c5aee77a38', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps which motor-unit size yields fine control'),
  ('37c165ed-54c9-4c34-99f2-42c5aee77a38', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'invokes the one-neuron-per-fiber rule to wrongly equalize precision'),
  ('37c165ed-54c9-4c34-99f2-42c5aee77a38', 'D', 'misconception', 'attributes greater maximal force to small motor units');

-- [Mendel's Laws & Epigenetics] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A pea plant heterozygous for seed color (\(Yy\)) and seed shape (\(Rr\)), with the two genes on separate chromosomes, produces the gametes \(YR\), \(Yr\), \(yR\), and \(yr\) in equal proportions. A researcher wants to identify the specific cellular event during meiosis that is directly responsible for this equal four-way gamete distribution. Which event should the researcher point to?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The random orientation of each homologous chromosome pair at the metaphase plate of meiosis I"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Crossing over between nonsister chromatids of homologous chromosomes during prophase I"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The separation of sister chromatids toward opposite poles during anaphase II"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The pairing of homologous chromosomes into tetrads during prophase I"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Mendel''s law of independent assortment states that genes on separate chromosomes sort into gametes without influencing each other, so every combination of alleles is equally likely. The textbook locates the physical basis of this law in meiosis I: because each homologous pair lines up at the metaphase plate in a random orientation independent of every other pair, a gamete that receives the chromosome carrying \(y\) is equally likely to receive the chromosome carrying \(R\) or the one carrying \(r\). (Choice A) is correct: the random alignment of each homologous pair at the metaphase plate of meiosis I is precisely the event that lets the color and shape chromosomes segregate independently, generating \(YR\), \(Yr\), \(yR\), and \(yr\) in equal proportion. (Choice B) is incorrect: crossing over during prophase I generates recombinant chromatids and new allele combinations, but it produces recombination between loci on the same chromosome; for two genes on separate chromosomes the equal four-way ratio arises from random metaphase orientation, not from crossing over. (Choice C) is incorrect: anaphase II separates sister chromatids, which are copies of a single chromosome (apart from any crossover-modified regions); this step does not determine how alleles of two different genes are combined, since that pattern is fixed earlier by how the homologous pairs orient in meiosis I. (Choice D) is incorrect: synapsis of homologs into tetrads during prophase I is a necessary preparatory step, but pairing itself does not create the independent combining of the two genes—it is the random orientation of those tetrads at metaphase I, not their formation, that yields the equal gamete classes. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your understanding of independent assortment to connect an observed gamete distribution to the specific meiotic event that causes it, distinguishing that event from other sources of meiotic variation.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '3e9c45fc-94ba-42d0-8cf7-fb83818bc99c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3e9c45fc-94ba-42d0-8cf7-fb83818bc99c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3e9c45fc-94ba-42d0-8cf7-fb83818bc99c', 'B', 'misconception', 'attributing independent assortment of unlinked genes to crossing over'),
  ('3e9c45fc-94ba-42d0-8cf7-fb83818bc99c', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'citing a real meiotic separation event that does not govern inter-gene combination'),
  ('3e9c45fc-94ba-42d0-8cf7-fb83818bc99c', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'confusing tetrad formation with the random tetrad orientation that produces assortment');

-- [Bone Structure & Marrow] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A pediatric radiologist reviews the long bones of a 9-year-old and a 30-year-old. In the child, one region of each long bone still contains a layer of hyaline cartilage that has not yet been replaced by osseous tissue; in the adult, this same region has fully ossified. Damage to this cartilage-containing region in the child would most directly impair the bone''s ability to',
  options = '[{"text": "increase in length.", "label": "A"}, {"text": "produce blood cells in red marrow.", "label": "B"}, {"text": "store triglycerides in yellow marrow.", "label": "C"}, {"text": "anchor tendons and ligaments to its surface.", "label": "D"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' In a long bone, each epiphysis meets the diaphysis at the metaphysis, a narrow region that in a growing bone contains the epiphyseal (growth) plate—a layer of hyaline cartilage. Longitudinal growth occurs as this cartilage proliferates and is progressively replaced by osseous tissue added to the diaphysis; when growth stops in early adulthood (~18–21 years) the plate ossifies into the epiphyseal line (OpenStax A&P 2e, lines 9446–9449, 9851, 9881). The scenario describes a hyaline-cartilage region present in the child but ossified in the adult—the epiphyseal plate at the metaphysis. (Choice A) is correct: the epiphyseal plate is the site of longitudinal bone growth, so destroying its hyaline cartilage in a still-growing child would most directly stunt the bone''s ability to lengthen. (Choice B) is incorrect: red-marrow hematopoiesis occurs in the spongy bone of the epiphysis, a distinct region; the growth plate''s cartilage does not produce blood cells, so damaging it would not primarily halt blood-cell production (lines 9315–9316, 9445–9446). (Choice C) is incorrect: yellow marrow stores triglycerides within the medullary cavity of the diaphysis, not in the epiphyseal-plate cartilage, so fat storage would be unaffected by damage to this region (lines 9314, 9437–9438). (Choice D) is incorrect: tendons and ligaments attach at the periosteum covering the bone''s outer surface, an entirely separate structure from the internal growth-plate cartilage (lines 9453–9454). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the functional roles of long-bone regions to predict the consequence of damaging the epiphyseal plate rather than simply recalling a region''s name.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'c7ded429-8cc8-49fd-a5ac-5c327277e068';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c7ded429-8cc8-49fd-a5ac-5c327277e068';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c7ded429-8cc8-49fd-a5ac-5c327277e068', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses growth-plate cartilage with the red-marrow hematopoiesis of the epiphysis'),
  ('c7ded429-8cc8-49fd-a5ac-5c327277e068', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses growth-plate cartilage with yellow-marrow fat storage in the diaphyseal medullary cavity'),
  ('c7ded429-8cc8-49fd-a5ac-5c327277e068', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'attributes the periosteum''s tendon/ligament attachment role to the internal growth plate');

-- [Mendelian Foundations] ans=A easy skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A geneticist compares the seed-color gene on the two members of a homologous chromosome pair in a diploid pea cell. She finds that the gene sits at the identical band on both chromosomes, yet DNA sequencing shows that the two copies differ from each other by several nucleotides. Which term names the feature that is the SAME for the seed-color gene on both chromosomes?',
  options = '[{"text": "Its locus", "label": "A"}, {"text": "Its allele", "label": "B"}, {"text": "Its genotype", "label": "C"}, {"text": "Its dominance", "label": "D"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Homologous chromosomes are matched pairs that carry the same genes in the same linear order, so a given gene maps to the identical position on both homologs; OpenStax defines the locus as the "position of a gene on a chromosome" and notes that homologs "have specific nucleotide segments called genes in exactly the same location, or locus." However, because each homolog came from a different parent, the two copies of that gene may differ in sequence, which is exactly what alleles are: "gene variants that arise by mutation and exist at the same relative locations on homologous chromosomes." The question therefore asks the student to separate what is shared (the physical position) from what differs (the sequence variant). (Choice A) is correct: the locus is the position on the chromosome, and this position is identical on the two homologs because they carry the same genes in the same order; it is the one listed feature that stays the same even though the sequences differ. (Choice B) is incorrect: the allele is the specific sequence variant occupying the locus, and the vignette states the two copies differ by several nucleotides, so the two homologs carry different alleles, not the same one. (Choice C) is incorrect: the genotype is the combination of both alleles an organism carries for the gene, a description of the pair taken together rather than something that is identical on each individual homolog. (Choice D) is incorrect: dominance describes how one allele masks another in the phenotype, a relationship between the two variants, not a feature that is the same on both chromosomes. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the locus is the fixed chromosomal position of a gene while alleles are the differing sequence variants that occupy that position on homologous chromosomes.',
  difficulty = 'easy',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = '9e67a300-927f-4cd0-b0a7-6c7a75aec5a3';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9e67a300-927f-4cd0-b0a7-6c7a75aec5a3';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9e67a300-927f-4cd0-b0a7-6c7a75aec5a3', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflates the position of a gene with the sequence variant occupying it'),
  ('9e67a300-927f-4cd0-b0a7-6c7a75aec5a3', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct genetics term for the wrong level (both alleles combined, not the shared position)'),
  ('9e67a300-927f-4cd0-b0a7-6c7a75aec5a3', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'allele-relationship concept substituted for a positional concept');

-- [Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a region of bone undergoing continuous remodeling, the cells that secrete new matrix become entrapped in the calcifying tissue and mature into a form that lacks mitotic activity. Because these mature cells cannot divide to replace themselves, the ongoing supply of new matrix-secreting cells must instead be generated by division of the:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "osteocyte"}, {"label": "B", "text": "osteoclast"}, {"label": "C", "text": "osteoblast"}, {"label": "D", "text": "osteogenic cell"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' Bone is continuously remodeled, yet the two matrix-associated cell types are mitotically inert: osteoblasts secrete the collagen-and-calcium-salt matrix and then become entrapped and change into osteocytes, and the textbook notes that both "osteoblasts and osteocytes are incapable of mitosis" (lines 9585–9588). The population of matrix-secreting cells can therefore only be replenished by a distinct dividing precursor. (Choice A) is incorrect: the osteocyte is the mature, entrapped cell described in the stem; it "maintain[s] the mineral concentration of the matrix" but lacks mitotic activity, so it cannot be the source of new cells. (Choice B) is incorrect: the osteoclast is a resorptive cell that "originate[s] from monocytes and macrophages... not from osteogenic cells" (lines 9592–9593), so it neither divides into bone-forming cells nor lays down matrix. (Choice C) is incorrect: the osteoblast is exactly the matrix-secreting cell that becomes entrapped, and the text states osteoblasts "do not divide," so it cannot regenerate its own population. (Choice D) is correct: the osteogenic cells are "undifferentiated with high mitotic activity and they are the only bone cells that divide," and "they differentiate and develop into osteoblasts" (lines 9586–9588), making them the source that sustains the matrix-secreting cell supply during remodeling. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that osteoblasts and osteocytes cannot divide to infer which remaining cell type must generate their replacements.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f3868fd1-a57d-41ba-87e0-d3a380ecf1f7';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f3868fd1-a57d-41ba-87e0-d3a380ecf1f7';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f3868fd1-a57d-41ba-87e0-d3a380ecf1f7', 'A', 'misconception', 'assumes the mature entrapped cell can regenerate itself'),
  ('f3868fd1-a57d-41ba-87e0-d3a380ecf1f7', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'confuses the resorptive/monocyte-derived cell with the bone-forming lineage'),
  ('f3868fd1-a57d-41ba-87e0-d3a380ecf1f7', 'C', 'misconception', 'picks the obvious matrix-secreting builder cell, ignoring that it cannot divide');

-- [Cartilage & Ossification] ans=C medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A histologist examines a connective tissue capping the articular end of a long bone. The tissue has a flexible matrix rich in chondroitin-sulfate proteoglycans and collagen, contains no blood vessels, and houses its resident cells in small spaces called lacunae that receive nutrients only by diffusion through the matrix. Which cell type both secretes and inhabits this tissue?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Osteocytes"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Osteoblasts"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Chondrocytes"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fibroblasts"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The described tissue is cartilage: its matrix is built from chondroitin-sulfate proteoglycans and collagen, it is avascular so nutrients must diffuse through the matrix, and its cells sit in lacunae. The two decisive clues are avascularity and the flexible proteoglycan matrix, because bone is highly vascularized and mineralized while cartilage is not; identifying the tissue from these properties is required before naming its cell. (Choice A) is incorrect: osteocytes are the mature cells of bone and, although they also occupy lacunae, they reside in a mineralized, highly vascularized matrix rather than the avascular proteoglycan matrix described, so the lacunae clue alone does not select them. (Choice B) is incorrect: osteoblasts synthesize and secrete the collagen matrix and calcium salts of new bone, a matrix that calcifies rather than remaining a flexible avascular proteoglycan gel, so they are bone-forming cells, not the cells of this tissue. (Choice C) is correct: chondrocytes secrete the chondroitin-sulfate proteoglycan and collagen matrix of cartilage and reside within its lacunae, depending on diffusion because the tissue is avascular. (Choice D) is incorrect: fibroblasts are the most abundant cell of connective tissue proper and do secrete collagen and ground substance, but they are scattered freely within a vascularized matrix and do not enclose themselves in lacunae within an avascular, chondroitin-sulfate-rich tissue. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that chondrocytes are the cells that produce and inhabit the avascular, proteoglycan-rich matrix of cartilage and distinguish them from the vascularized-matrix cells of bone and connective tissue proper.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = '9e0e0628-5814-4b4c-86ef-141af68bf5f5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9e0e0628-5814-4b4c-86ef-141af68bf5f5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9e0e0628-5814-4b4c-86ef-141af68bf5f5', 'A', 'adjacent_fact', 'lacunae-dwelling cell of the wrong skeletal tissue (bone)'),
  ('9e0e0628-5814-4b4c-86ef-141af68bf5f5', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'matrix-secreting bone cell confused for cartilage cell'),
  ('9e0e0628-5814-4b4c-86ef-141af68bf5f5', 'D', 'misconception', 'generic connective-tissue matrix producer applied to cartilage');

-- [Joints & Movement] ans=C medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two joints in the axial skeleton are noted for the same functional role: a cranial suture anchors the frontal and parietal bones over the brain, and the manubriosternal joint anchors the manubrium to the body of the sternum over the heart. The suture is a fibrous joint, whereas the manubriosternal joint is a cartilaginous joint. Into which single functional joint category do BOTH of these joints fall?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Diarthrosis"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Amphiarthrosis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Synarthrosis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Because one joint is fibrous and the other is cartilaginous, they cannot share a functional category"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' Functional classification of joints is set by the amount of mobility between the articulating bones, not by the joint''s structural material: the textbook states that the same structural class can map to different functional classes, and that both fibrous and cartilaginous joints "may be functionally classified as a synarthrosis (immobile joint)." It lists two protective joints together as synarthroses — "sutures, the fibrous joints between the bones of the skull that surround and protect the brain, and the manubriosternal joint, the cartilaginous joint that unites the manubrium and body of the sternum for protection of the heart" — because each forms a strong, immobile union that shields an internal organ. (Choice A) is incorrect because a diarthrosis is "a freely moveable joint" comprising the synovial joints of the limbs; neither a skull suture nor the manubriosternal joint permits free movement, so this is the wrong mobility extreme. (Choice B) is incorrect because an amphiarthrosis is a "slightly moveable joint" such as the fibrocartilaginous intervertebral discs and pubic symphysis, which allow limited motion; the two protective joints described here form immobile unions rather than slightly mobile ones. (Choice C) is correct: both joints form strong, immobile unions that protect an internal organ, and an immobile joint is a synarthrosis; because functional class is assigned by mobility alone, a fibrous suture and a cartilaginous manubriosternal joint can share this category even though their structures differ. (Choice D) is incorrect because it conflates structural and functional classification; the text explicitly notes that both fibrous and cartilaginous joints can be functionally classified as synarthroses, so differing structural material does not prevent two joints from sharing one functional category. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that functional joint classes are defined by mobility, that immobile protective joints are synarthroses, and that structurally different joints can share a functional category.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = 'd77d490c-d5d7-46bd-ae5f-e139ec7c9df0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd77d490c-d5d7-46bd-ae5f-e139ec7c9df0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d77d490c-d5d7-46bd-ae5f-e139ec7c9df0', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'opposite mobility extreme (freely moveable) selected for an immobile joint'),
  ('d77d490c-d5d7-46bd-ae5f-e139ec7c9df0', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses slightly-mobile amphiarthrosis with immobile synarthrosis'),
  ('d77d490c-d5d7-46bd-ae5f-e139ec7c9df0', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes structural class dictates functional class, so different structures cannot share a functional category');

-- [Dominance Patterns] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A geneticist studies a single flower-color gene by measuring the two pigment proteins it can encode: a crimson pigment and an ivory pigment. In a true-breeding crimson line, petal cells make only the crimson pigment; in a true-breeding ivory line, petal cells make only the ivory pigment; each line makes its single pigment at the same high level, roughly 100 units per cell. The two lines are crossed. In every F1 petal cell, the geneticist detects crimson pigment at about 100 units per cell AND ivory pigment at about 100 units per cell, matching the amount each pigment reaches in its own homozygous parent. Which inheritance pattern is most consistent with these measurements?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Codominance"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Incomplete dominance"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Complete dominance"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Epistasis"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The dominance pattern of a single gene is diagnosed from how the heterozygote expresses the two alleles'' products: codominance means both alleles are simultaneously and fully expressed in the heterozygote, so each parental product appears at its full homozygous level, whereas incomplete dominance produces an intermediate result in which the heterozygote falls between the two homozygotes. The key measurement here is quantitative: each pigment in the F1 reaches ~100 units, the same level it reaches in its own homozygous parent, so neither product is diluted or reduced. (Choice A) is correct: because the F1 makes both the crimson-allele product and the ivory-allele product at their full ~100-unit homozygous levels rather than at reduced levels, both alleles are being expressed independently and completely in the heterozygote, which is the defining molecular signature of codominance (as in the MN blood groups, where a heterozygote displays both the M and N antigens fully). (Choice B) is incorrect: incomplete dominance predicts an intermediate outcome — for a gene controlling one measurable pigment, the heterozygote would show a reduced or blended level (for example, roughly 50 units of a single pigment, the analog of a pink snapdragon), not each of two pigments at its full 100-unit level; the data show full, not intermediate, expression, so this pattern is ruled out. (Choice C) is incorrect: under complete dominance one allele''s product would be expressed and the other silenced, so the F1 would show only crimson (~100 units) or only ivory, whereas both products are detected at full level. (Choice D) is incorrect: epistasis is an interaction between two different genes in which one gene masks the effect of another, but this scenario involves the two alleles of a single gene and shows no masking of either product, so a two-gene interaction cannot account for the measurements. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the quantitative definitions of the dominance patterns to expression-level data and infer that full, independent expression of both allele products — rather than a reduced or intermediate level — identifies codominance.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff', 'B', 'misconception', 'conflating codominance with incomplete dominance'),
  ('d383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'defaulting to simple Mendelian dominance'),
  ('d383941b-5c82-405f-9316-b61bd2cf15ff', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'invoking a multi-gene interaction for a single-gene cross');

-- [Dominance Patterns] ans=C hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A geneticist self-crosses \(F_1\) heterozygotes from a two-allele cross and scores the \(F_2\) offspring. The offspring fall into three phenotypic classes in a 1:2:1 ratio, and molecular assays show that heterozygous individuals produce fully functional copies of both parental gene products at normal levels, with neither product masking or diluting the other. Which inheritance pattern is most consistent with these results?',
  options = '[{"text": "Incomplete dominance", "label": "A"}, {"text": "Complete dominance", "label": "B"}, {"text": "Codominance", "label": "C"}, {"text": "Multiple alleles", "label": "D"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' OpenStax notes that both codominance and incomplete dominance make the three offspring genotypes ''phenotypically distinct,'' so a heterozygote self-cross yields a 1:2:1 phenotypic ratio rather than the 3:1 seen with complete dominance. The two patterns are then distinguished at the mechanistic level: codominance is when ''both alleles for the same characteristic are simultaneously expressed in the heterozygote'' (each gene product present and functional), whereas incomplete dominance yields a blended ''intermediate phenotype.'' (Choice A) is incorrect because incomplete dominance produces a heterozygote that ''displays an intermediate phenotype'' — the two alleles blend (e.g., red \(C^RC^R\) × white \(C^WC^W\) snapdragons give pink \(C^RC^W\)) rather than each product being fully and independently present, contradicting the assay showing both products at normal, undiluted levels. (Choice B) is incorrect because under complete dominance the heterozygote resembles the dominant homozygote, collapsing the offspring into two phenotypic classes in a 3:1 ratio, not the observed three-class 1:2:1 ratio. (Choice C) is correct: the 1:2:1 phenotypic ratio establishes that heterozygotes are phenotypically distinct, and the finding that both parental products are simultaneously and fully expressed with no blending is the defining mechanism of codominance (as in the MN blood groups, where \(L^ML^N\) heterozygotes ''express both alleles equally''). (Choice D) is incorrect because multiple alleles refers to a gene for which ''more than two alleles'' exist in a population; this cross involves only two alleles and the question concerns how those alleles are expressed in the heterozygote, not how many alleles exist. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of non-Mendelian dominance patterns to both the phenotypic ratio and the molecular expression data to infer the underlying mechanism.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b27ef882-7bc5-4807-aec4-3b5393d8c7da';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b27ef882-7bc5-4807-aec4-3b5393d8c7da';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b27ef882-7bc5-4807-aec4-3b5393d8c7da', 'A', 'misconception', 'conflating codominance with incomplete dominance because both yield a 1:2:1 ratio, ignoring the blend-vs-both-expressed distinction'),
  ('b27ef882-7bc5-4807-aec4-3b5393d8c7da', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'attributing the three-class 1:2:1 ratio to complete dominance, which actually gives a two-class 3:1 ratio'),
  ('b27ef882-7bc5-4807-aec4-3b5393d8c7da', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'invoking allele count at the population level when the data are about heterozygote expression of two alleles');

-- [Mutations] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A patient with sickle-cell anemia produces a hemoglobin beta chain that is the normal full length of 147 residues. Sequencing shows the chain is identical to the normal beta chain except at position six, where valine appears in place of the usual glutamate; no other residue differs and the chain is not truncated. Which single change in the beta-globin gene best accounts for this protein?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A silent substitution that alters one codon but preserves the encoded residue"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A single-base deletion that shifts the translational reading frame"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A nonsense substitution that introduces a premature stop codon"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A missense substitution that changes one codon to specify a different residue"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Point mutations affect a single base pair; the most common are substitutions, in which one base is replaced by another, and the textbook notes these mutations ''can result in the replacement of one amino acid by another, which may alter the function of the protein.'' Sickle-cell hemoglobin is the classic example: the text states that at position six ''glutamate is replaced by valine'' through ''a single base change (point mutation),'' yielding a full-length chain with a single altered residue. You must match the observed data — a chain of normal length, unchanged everywhere except one residue swapped — to the mutation class that produces exactly that outcome. (Choice A) is incorrect because a silent substitution, ''usually due to a substitution in the third base of a codon, which often represents the same amino acid,'' leaves the protein sequence unchanged; yet here glutamate is demonstrably replaced by valine, so the change cannot be silent. (Choice B) is incorrect because a single-base deletion causes ''the alteration of the translational reading frame (a frameshift mutation),'' scrambling every codon downstream and ''usually'' yielding a nonfunctional protein — not a full-length chain differing at only one position. (Choice C) is incorrect because a nonsense substitution generates a stop codon that would ''terminate a protein early,'' producing a truncated chain, whereas the described beta chain is the normal full length of 147 residues. (Choice D) is correct: a missense substitution replaces one base, changing a single codon so it specifies a different amino acid (glutamate to valine) while leaving the codon count and reading frame intact — exactly the full-length, single-residue change observed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of silent, missense, nonsense, and frameshift mutations to infer, from the length and residue pattern of the protein, which DNA-level event occurred.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '06229ac9-f2e7-47f6-8940-75f41346e588';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '06229ac9-f2e7-47f6-8940-75f41346e588';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('06229ac9-f2e7-47f6-8940-75f41346e588', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses silent (synonymous) substitution with a substitution that changes the residue'),
  ('06229ac9-f2e7-47f6-8940-75f41346e588', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'applies frameshift/reading-frame concept despite full-length single-residue evidence'),
  ('06229ac9-f2e7-47f6-8940-75f41346e588', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'applies nonsense/premature-stop concept despite the chain being full length');

-- [Speciation & Isolation] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A field biologist observes that two bird populations mate freely where their ranges overlap. The resulting hybrid chicks hatch and grow into healthy adults, but every hybrid, when mated back to either parent population or to another hybrid, fails to produce any young of its own. Applying the biological species concept, what should the biologist conclude?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The two populations are separate species, because their offspring cannot themselves reproduce"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The two populations are one species, because they interbreed and yield healthy, surviving offspring"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The two populations are one species, because the adults are similar enough to court and mate successfully"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The two populations cannot be classified, because the biological species concept applies only to populations that are geographically separated"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Under the biological species concept, a species is a group that can interbreed and produce offspring that are both viable and fertile; the operational test is not merely whether two populations can mate, but whether their offspring can themselves reproduce, sustaining gene flow across generations. The textbook makes this precise with its eagle example: a hybrid may hatch yet be ''infertile—unable to successfully reproduce after it reached maturity,'' so ''the two species still remain separate.'' Sterile hybrids are a postzygotic barrier (hybrid sterility), where ''hybrid birth and growth'' occurs but the organism is ''unable to reproduce offspring of their own.'' (Choice A) is correct: because the hybrids grow up healthy but cannot reproduce, no fertile offspring are produced in nature, which is exactly the condition under which the biological species concept keeps the two populations as distinct species. (Choice B) is incorrect: mating and producing living young is not sufficient—the concept requires offspring that are viable AND fertile, and here the surviving hybrids are sterile, so the interbreeding does not merge the two gene pools. (Choice C) is incorrect: morphological or behavioral similarity that permits courtship does not establish conspecificity; the textbook warns that ''appearance can be misleading in suggesting an ability or inability to mate,'' and successful reproduction, not attraction, is the criterion. (Choice D) is incorrect: geographic separation describes allopatry, a mechanism that can drive speciation, but it is not a precondition for applying the concept—the concept is defined by reproductive compatibility and applies whether or not the populations overlap in space. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the biological species concept to reproductive data, recognizing that viable-but-sterile hybrids satisfy interbreeding yet still leave the populations as separate species.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '3dcaf5ef-2826-4980-8f24-ddb187b25dcd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3dcaf5ef-2826-4980-8f24-ddb187b25dcd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3dcaf5ef-2826-4980-8f24-ddb187b25dcd', 'B', 'misconception', 'equates any interbreeding/surviving offspring with conspecificity, ignoring the fertility requirement'),
  ('3dcaf5ef-2826-4980-8f24-ddb187b25dcd', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'treats morphological/behavioral similarity and willingness to mate as the species criterion'),
  ('3dcaf5ef-2826-4980-8f24-ddb187b25dcd', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses allopatric speciation (geographic separation) with a requirement of the biological species concept');

-- [Evolutionary Patterns] ans=C medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Birds and mammals both possess a four-chambered heart that fully separates oxygenated from deoxygenated blood. Fossil and phylogenetic evidence indicates that birds descend from archosaur reptiles while mammals descend from a separate synapsid lineage, and that the most recent common ancestor shared by the two groups had a heart that was NOT four-chambered. Given this evidence, the four-chambered hearts of birds and mammals are best described as:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Homologous structures produced by divergent evolution"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Vestigial structures retained from a shared ancestor"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Analogous structures produced by convergent evolution"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Homologous structures produced by convergent evolution"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' A trait shared by two lineages is homologous only if it was inherited from their common ancestor; if the same trait instead evolved separately in each lineage after they diverged, it is analogous and its appearance is the product of convergent evolution. The decisive clue here is that the most recent common ancestor of birds and mammals did NOT have a four-chambered heart, so each group must have built that heart on its own. OpenStax notes that "the four-chambered heart of birds and mammals evolved independently from a three-chambered heart. The independent evolution of the same or a similar biological trait is referred to as convergent evolution." (Choice A) is incorrect: divergent evolution describes lineages that evolve in different directions from a shared starting trait, and homology requires that the four-chambered heart came from the common ancestor—but the evidence states the ancestor lacked it, so the structure is neither homologous nor a case of divergence. (Choice B) is incorrect: vestigial structures are reduced, largely nonfunctional remnants of a feature present in an ancestor, whereas the four-chambered heart is fully functional and, per the evidence, was not present in the ancestor at all. (Choice C) is correct: because the shared ancestor lacked the four-chambered heart, birds and mammals each evolved it separately, making the hearts analogous structures generated by convergent evolution. (Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly identifies convergent evolution but pairs it with the wrong structure type—traits arising from independent evolution are analogous, not homologous, since homology by definition requires inheritance from a common ancestor. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that homology requires inheritance from a common ancestor while analogy arises from convergent evolution, and use the ancestral-state evidence to classify the trait correctly.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = 'd4902cdf-067a-4b9c-a5b8-3674a04b7c2c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd4902cdf-067a-4b9c-a5b8-3674a04b7c2c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d4902cdf-067a-4b9c-a5b8-3674a04b7c2c', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', 'Treats the shared trait as inherited (homologous/divergent) despite evidence the ancestor lacked it'),
  ('d4902cdf-067a-4b9c-a5b8-3674a04b7c2c', 'B', 'misconception', 'Confuses a functional independently-evolved trait with a nonfunctional ancestral remnant'),
  ('d4902cdf-067a-4b9c-a5b8-3674a04b7c2c', 'D', 'misconception', 'Correct process (convergent) mismatched with wrong structure type (homologous instead of analogous)');

-- [Evolutionary Patterns] ans=A medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A cave-dwelling salamander lineage and a cave-dwelling fish lineage last shared a common ancestor over 350 million years ago—an ancestor that had fully functional eyes. Both lineages independently colonized lightless caves, and today members of each group have smooth skin over the eye socket and rely on lateral-line and chemosensory organs to navigate. Reconstructing the ancestor confirms it was sighted, and the eye-reduction mutations differ between the two groups. Which macroevolutionary pattern best accounts for the shared blind phenotype in these two groups?',
  options = '[{"text": "Convergent evolution", "label": "A"}, {"text": "Divergent evolution", "label": "B"}, {"text": "Adaptive radiation", "label": "C"}, {"text": "Coevolution", "label": "D"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' Whether a shared trait reflects convergent or divergent evolution is decided by ancestry, not by how similar the organisms look: the deciding question is whether a common ancestor already had the trait (which the descendants then inherited) or whether the trait arose separately in each lineage. Here the reconstructed last common ancestor was fully sighted and the underlying eye-reduction mutations differ between the salamanders and the fish, so the blind phenotype was not inherited from a shared ancestor—it was built twice, independently, under the same selective pressure (permanent darkness). (Choice A) is correct: convergent evolution is the process by which distantly related groups that do NOT share the trait through recent common ancestry independently evolve to similar forms because of similar selective pressures; the sighted ancestor plus the different, independently acquired mutations are the signature of independent origin, so the matching blind phenotype is convergent. (Choice B) is incorrect: divergent evolution is the process by which groups sharing a recent common ancestor evolve in diverse directions from that common point, becoming more different over time; that is the opposite of what the data show, because these lineages started from a sighted ancestor and became more similar (both blind), not more different, and they are only distantly related. (Choice C) is incorrect: adaptive radiation is speciation in which a single ancestral species rapidly radiates into several new species filling distinct niches (e.g., Hawaiian honeycreepers with differently shaped beaks); the scenario involves two separate, already-distinct lineages arriving at the same phenotype, not one species multiplying into many niches. (Choice D) is incorrect: coevolution is reciprocal evolutionary change between two interacting groups that impose selection on each other (such as a plant and its pollinator); the salamanders and fish do not interact or select on one another—each responded independently to the physical environment of the cave. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that convergent evolution is defined by independent origin of a similar trait in lineages lacking recent shared ancestry for that trait, and use the ancestral state to distinguish it from divergence, radiation, and coevolution.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = '0f74a082-88c8-4236-b3da-c65c3a48f80e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0f74a082-88c8-4236-b3da-c65c3a48f80e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0f74a082-88c8-4236-b3da-c65c3a48f80e', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'picks the ancestry-sharing term when the data show independent origin'),
  ('0f74a082-88c8-4236-b3da-c65c3a48f80e', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses single-species radiation into niches with two lineages reaching one phenotype'),
  ('0f74a082-88c8-4236-b3da-c65c3a48f80e', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes shared environment implies reciprocal selection between the two groups');

-- [Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A purified digestive enzyme acts on a polypeptide substrate. A researcher tracking the reaction finds that the enzyme cleaves a single internal peptide bond, splitting the chain into two shorter fragments. When the fragments are analyzed, one carries a new free carboxyl group and the other a new free amine group at the site of the break; no new double bond forms in either fragment, no cofactor is oxidized or reduced, and nothing from the broken bond is handed off to a second substrate molecule. To which Enzyme Commission (EC) class does this enzyme belong?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Lyase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Transferase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Hydrolase"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oxidoreductase"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The IUBMB Enzyme Commission table lists six primary classes defined by the type of reaction catalyzed: oxidoreductases (EC 1, redox), transferases (EC 2, transfer of a group to a second molecule), hydrolases (EC 3, hydrolysis), lyases (EC 4, elimination forming a double bond), isomerases (EC 5), and ligases (EC 6). The diagnostic move here is to read the products: a single peptide (amide) bond is broken and the two ends emerge as a free carboxyl group and a free amine group. LibreTexts notes that cleaving a peptide bond "requires the incorporation of a water molecule to separate two amino acids and break the amide bond," with the H and OH of that water capping the new amine and carboxyl termini, and that "a class of enzymes called proteases catalyzes protein hydrolysis" — the signature of an EC 3 hydrolase. (Choice C) is correct: two complementary saturated termini (amine and carboxyl) arising from one bond cleavage, with no redox and no group passed to another molecule, is exactly the hydrolysis reaction that defines a hydrolase (EC 3). (Choice A) is incorrect: a lyase (EC 4) also breaks a single bond, but it does so by elimination that leaves a new double bond in a product; the stem states that no new double bond forms, ruling out a lyase and pointing to bond cleavage by water addition instead. (Choice B) is incorrect: a transferase (EC 2) moves a chemical group onto a second molecule, but here the split fragments are simply capped in place and nothing is delivered to another substrate. (Choice D) is incorrect: an oxidoreductase (EC 1) catalyzes electron transfer, yet the stem specifies that no cofactor is oxidized or reduced, so no redox occurs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the EC classification scheme to a described reaction, reasoning from the product signature (two saturated amine/carboxyl termini, no new double bond, no redox, no group transfer) to infer that a water molecule was added across the bond and assign the hydrolase class.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b18a7429-27ca-4500-8000-40d8ed8dfaa6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b18a7429-27ca-4500-8000-40d8ed8dfaa6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b18a7429-27ca-4500-8000-40d8ed8dfaa6', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses hydrolytic cleavage with eliminative (lyase) cleavage because both break one bond'),
  ('b18a7429-27ca-4500-8000-40d8ed8dfaa6', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'knows a group is repositioned but wrongly treats in-place capping as transfer to a second molecule'),
  ('b18a7429-27ca-4500-8000-40d8ed8dfaa6', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'picks the redox class despite the explicit no-redox statement');

-- [Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Hexokinase catalyzes the phosphorylation of glucose. Crystallographic studies show that the free enzyme adopts an open conformation, but when glucose enters the active site, the two lobes of the enzyme swing together and clamp down around the sugar, closing off the site before catalysis proceeds. Which model of substrate binding does this crystallographic observation demonstrate?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Lock-and-key model"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Conformational selection model"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Induced-fit model"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Transition-state stabilization"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Two classical models describe how a substrate binds an enzyme''s active site: in one, a rigid, pre-formed active site is already complementary to the substrate, while in the other, contact between substrate and protein changes the protein''s shape so that it wraps around the substrate. The textbook states that binding can occur "through an induced-fit model, in which contact between the reactant and the protein changes the protein''s shape, allowing it to bind," and describes the induced-fit figure as one in which "substrate interaction causes the enzyme''s shape to change to better fit the substrate." It further identifies hexokinase as the classic case, noting a "large conformational change on binding" glucose in which "the site becomes sequestered (''jaw'' clamping down on binding)." The scenario—an open enzyme that closes around glucose only after the sugar enters—matches the model in which the enzyme is remodeled by the binding event. (Choice A) is incorrect: in the lock-and-key model "the reactant fits the exact shape of the enzyme''s binding site," and the textbook figure shows substrates binding "without further modification to the enzyme''s shape," so a lock-and-key enzyme would already be complementary and would not need to swing shut around the substrate. (Choice B) is incorrect: in conformational selection the closed (bound-competent) conformation already exists in the free enzyme''s ensemble and the substrate simply binds to and stabilizes that pre-existing shape "without inducing subsequent macromolecular conformational change"; here the free enzyme is observed to be open and is reshaped only upon contact, which is the induced-fit behavior, not selection of a pre-formed conformation. (Choice D) is incorrect: transition-state stabilization refers to how an enzyme lowers a reaction''s activation energy by binding the high-energy transition state more tightly than the substrate—a feature of catalysis, not a description of the geometric change the active site undergoes when the ground-state substrate first binds. (Choice C) is correct: because the enzyme''s own shape is altered by the act of substrate binding, the observation defines the induced-fit model. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between binding models to a structural observation—recognizing that an active site which is remodeled by the substrate reflects induced fit rather than a rigid, pre-complementary or pre-selected conformation.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2990e74e-7e34-4bcf-b369-a5285f487fb6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2990e74e-7e34-4bcf-b369-a5285f487fb6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2990e74e-7e34-4bcf-b369-a5285f487fb6', 'A', 'misconception', 'confusing rigid-fit lock-and-key with the shape-changing model'),
  ('2990e74e-7e34-4bcf-b369-a5285f487fb6', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'conformational selection (pre-existing conformation) mistaken for induced fit'),
  ('2990e74e-7e34-4bcf-b369-a5285f487fb6', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'a real catalytic mechanism that does not describe binding-induced shape change');

-- [Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During each catalytic cycle, a dehydrogenase binds a small, vitamin-derived organic molecule that accepts a pair of electrons from the substrate and then dissociates completely from the active site, diffusing away as a free, reduced species that carries those electrons with it. Only after a separate reaction elsewhere restores this free molecule to its original oxidized state can it return and the enzyme catalyze another turnover. Which term best describes this small organic molecule?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Coenzyme"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Prosthetic group"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Apoenzyme"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Holoenzyme"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' LibreTexts defines cofactors as molecules that bind to enzymes and are required for catalytic activity, and divides them into metals and coenzymes, where coenzymes are ''small organic molecules that are often derived from vitamins.'' Crucially, the text notes that coenzymes ''can bind loosely with the enzyme and be released from the active site,'' so that they ''are also considered substrates for the reaction'' — the dissociable, freely-diffusing electron carrier described in the stem (the classic role of a vitamin-derived coenzyme such as NAD+, which the textbook explicitly names as a dehydrogenase''s substrate). Because this molecule leaves the enzyme entirely and is re-oxidized elsewhere in solution before it can act again, it is behaving as a loose, dissociable coenzyme, not a tightly bound cofactor. (Choice A) is correct: a coenzyme is a small, often vitamin-derived organic cofactor, and one that binds loosely, leaves the active site carrying the electrons, and is regenerated as a free species before returning is behaving as a dissociable coenzyme (co-substrate), matching every detail of the stem. (Choice B) is incorrect: the text states that ''tight-binding coenzymes are referred to as prosthetic groups'' and that such cofactors ''cannot dissociate easily from the enzyme'' — a prosthetic group stays bound and is regenerated in place, which directly contradicts a molecule that leaves the active site and diffuses away each turnover, so the loose, dissociable behavior described rules this term out. (Choice C) is incorrect: an apoenzyme is the protein enzyme ''not yet associated with a required cofactor,'' i.e., the polypeptide lacking its cofactor — it is the enzyme itself in an inactive state, not the small organic molecule that binds to it. (Choice D) is incorrect: a holoenzyme is the assembled complex of the enzyme ''bound with [its] required cofactors,'' so it names the whole catalytically active enzyme-plus-cofactor unit, not the individual small molecule that transiently binds and departs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the loose- versus tight-binding criterion for cofactors to classify a dissociable, regenerable organic molecule as a coenzyme rather than a prosthetic group, apoenzyme, or holoenzyme.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '0bc0774b-d17f-4ab2-a0c1-677deaae1279';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0bc0774b-d17f-4ab2-a0c1-677deaae1279';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0bc0774b-d17f-4ab2-a0c1-677deaae1279', 'B', 'misconception', 'Assumes any small organic cofactor is a prosthetic group, ignoring that prosthetic groups are specifically the tightly bound, non-dissociable coenzymes'),
  ('0bc0774b-d17f-4ab2-a0c1-677deaae1279', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'Confuses the small cofactor molecule with the cofactor-free protein enzyme (apoenzyme)'),
  ('0bc0774b-d17f-4ab2-a0c1-677deaae1279', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'Confuses the small cofactor molecule with the assembled enzyme-plus-cofactor complex (holoenzyme)');

-- [Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two purified enzymes, X and Y, each require a single vitamin-derived organic cofactor for activity. Both are subjected to identical, extensive dialysis that removes small dissolved molecules. Afterward, enzyme X has lost all catalytic activity, but enzyme Y retains full activity with no cofactor added back. Which statement best accounts for the different outcomes?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Enzyme Y''s cofactor is a tightly bound prosthetic group, whereas enzyme X''s cofactor is loosely bound and dissociates during dialysis"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Enzyme Y is a holoenzyme and enzyme X is a holoenzyme, so only enzyme X can release its cofactor during dialysis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Enzyme Y remained a zymogen during dialysis, whereas enzyme X was proteolytically activated and then lost activity"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Enzyme Y''s cofactor is a loosely bound coenzyme that stays in the active site, whereas enzyme X''s cofactor is a metal ion that cannot be dialyzed"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Coenzymes are small, often vitamin-derived organic cofactors, and the textbook distinguishes two binding modes: they "can bind loosely with the enzyme and be released from the active site," or "they may be tightly bound and cannot dissociate easily from the enzyme," in which case they are called prosthetic groups (LibreTexts 32722-32727). A protein that has lost its required cofactor is an inactive apoenzyme, whereas the cofactor-bound, active form is the holoenzyme (LibreTexts 32727-32728). Extensive dialysis strips only cofactors that can dissociate, so retention of activity by enzyme Y implies its cofactor could not wash out. (Choice A) is correct: enzyme Y keeps activity because its coenzyme is a tightly bound prosthetic group that "cannot dissociate easily," so dialysis leaves the holoenzyme intact, while enzyme X''s loosely bound coenzyme is released and leaves behind an inactive apoenzyme (LibreTexts 32722-32728). (Choice B) is incorrect: labeling both enzymes holoenzymes does not explain the difference, and a holoenzyme is defined as the enzyme already bound to its cofactor, so it provides no mechanism for why one enzyme would selectively lose its cofactor during dialysis (LibreTexts 32727-32728). (Choice C) is incorrect: a zymogen is an inactive precursor "activated on limited proteolysis," a change dialysis cannot cause, and a zymogen would be inactive rather than fully active, so enzyme Y cannot owe its retained activity to remaining a zymogen (LibreTexts 31298-31301, 21381). (Choice D) is incorrect: it reverses the binding logic, because a loosely bound coenzyme is precisely the type that dissociates during dialysis and would abolish activity, and metal cofactors are also small dissolved species that dialysis can remove, so this cannot explain why enzyme Y stayed active while enzyme X did not (LibreTexts 32722-32725). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the loosely-bound-coenzyme versus tightly-bound-prosthetic-group distinction to infer why identical dialysis inactivates one enzyme but not the other.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '3d6cba78-90f5-4eae-9df8-757af88cc363';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3d6cba78-90f5-4eae-9df8-757af88cc363';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3d6cba78-90f5-4eae-9df8-757af88cc363', 'B', 'partial_truth', 'Correct term (holoenzyme) applied without a causal mechanism'),
  ('3d6cba78-90f5-4eae-9df8-757af88cc363', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'Zymogen/proteolysis mechanism swapped in for cofactor binding'),
  ('3d6cba78-90f5-4eae-9df8-757af88cc363', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'Loose vs. tight binding logic reversed');

-- [Enzyme Regulation (Allosteric, Feedback, Covalent Modification, Zymogens)] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studies a metabolic enzyme and finds that its activity is raised by binding one small molecule and lowered by binding a second, different small molecule. Neither of these molecules chemically resembles the substrate, and each can be washed out to fully restore the enzyme''s baseline activity. Which regulatory mechanism is most consistent with all of these observations?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Competitive inhibition, in which each regulating molecule occupies the active site in place of the substrate"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Heterotropic allosteric regulation, in which each effector binds a regulatory site separate from the active site"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Covalent modification, in which a kinase attaches a phosphoryl group to the enzyme to change its activity"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Zymogen activation, in which a protease cleaves the enzyme to convert it from an inactive to an active form"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' In heterotropic (Type II) allosterism, a modulator binds to a site that is different from the substrate-binding site and, from there, activates or inhibits the enzyme; because a separate activator and a separate inhibitor can each bind reversibly, activity can be tuned up or down. The reference states that heterotropic allosterism ''occurs when a chemical modulator binds to a site different from the binding site for the main ligand,'' where it ''modulates (activates or inhibits) the binding affinity of the main ligand for the active or orthosteric site,'' and that ''The modulator binds at an allosteric (other) binding site'' (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 20522–20525); the text also contrasts this with ''classical competitive, non-allosteric inhibition,'' where a ligand and inhibitor compete for ''the same orthosteric site'' (lines 21322–21325). The stem''s fingerprint—two distinct small molecules, neither resembling the substrate, one raising and one lowering activity, and both freely reversible—matches only regulation at sites separate from the active site. (Choice A) is incorrect: a classical competitive inhibitor and the substrate ''bind mutually exclusively to the active site'' (line 27750; lines 21322–21323), so a competitive inhibitor competes for the very site the substrate occupies—which is why classic competitive inhibitors are substrate-like—and this is hard to reconcile with two effectors that ''neither resemble the substrate''; more decisively, competitive inhibition only decreases activity and cannot account for the molecule that raises it. (Choice B) is correct: heterotropic allosteric regulation places the activator and inhibitor at regulatory sites distinct from the active site, allowing two non-substrate-like effectors to reversibly turn activity up or down (lines 20522–20525). (Choice C) is incorrect: covalent modification such as phosphorylation adds a phosphoryl group through an enzyme-catalyzed reaction rather than by simple reversible binding of two different small molecules, so it does not fit effectors that ''can be washed out'' to restore baseline; the outline lists covalent modification as a distinct regulatory route from allosteric control (lines 6339; AAMC outline lines 1039–1048). (Choice D) is incorrect: zymogen activation is an irreversible, one-directional switch in which a protease cleaves an inactive precursor into an active enzyme (lines 21381; 22091–22092), so it cannot explain bidirectional up-and-down tuning by small molecules that wash out to restore baseline. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining features of each regulatory mechanism to the experimental clues—reversible binding of two non-substrate-like effectors that both raise and lower activity—to identify heterotropic allosteric regulation at a separate site.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '85980c9e-e18a-48c0-9f3e-df4e71384091';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '85980c9e-e18a-48c0-9f3e-df4e71384091';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('85980c9e-e18a-48c0-9f3e-df4e71384091', 'A', 'misconception', 'conflating competitive inhibition with allosteric regulation'),
  ('85980c9e-e18a-48c0-9f3e-df4e71384091', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'a real regulation mechanism (covalent modification) that fails the reversible-small-molecule clue'),
  ('85980c9e-e18a-48c0-9f3e-df4e71384091', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'a real regulation mechanism (zymogen cleavage) that fails the bidirectional/reversible clue');

-- [Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A single-substrate enzyme acts on glucose-1-phosphate, and the sole product it releases is glucose-6-phosphate. No cofactor is consumed, no water is added, and no other reactant or product is detected. The phosphate group that began on carbon 1 is found on carbon 6 of the same six-carbon backbone. Which enzyme class does this reaction belong to?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Isomerase, because the atoms are rearranged within one molecule to give a constitutional isomer"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Transferase, because a phosphate group is relocated from one position to another"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Hydrolase, because a phosphoester bond is cleaved and reformed at a new site"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Ligase, because carbon 1 and carbon 6 become joined through the phosphate"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The International Union of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology (IUBMB) sorts enzymes into six EC classes by reaction type: oxidoreductases (EC1) run redox reactions, transferases (EC2) catalyze "transfer/exchange of group from one molecule to another," hydrolases (EC3) run hydrolysis reactions, lyases (EC4) perform "elimination forming a double bond," isomerases (EC5) catalyze "conversions of geometric, stereo- or constitutive isomers," and ligases (EC6) drive "condensation of two molecules into one." The decisive question here is not whether a phosphate ends up in a new place, but whether a group is handed to a second molecule or simply relocated within the same one. (Choice A) is correct: glucose-1-phosphate and glucose-6-phosphate are constitutional (positional) isomers of one another, and the reaction repositions the phosphate on a single molecule with no atoms gained or lost and no partner molecule, which is exactly the isomerase (EC5) definition of interconverting constitutive isomers. (Choice B) is incorrect: although a phosphate does move, a transferase (EC2) transfers a group "from one molecule to another"—it requires a separate acceptor or donor molecule (as when a kinase moves phosphate from \(\text{ATP}\) onto a substrate); here only one molecule enters and only one leaves, so no intermolecular transfer occurs. (Choice C) is incorrect: a hydrolase (EC3) uses water to cleave a bond, but the reaction adds no water and leaves the phosphate esterified rather than hydrolyzed off. (Choice D) is incorrect: a ligase (EC6) joins two separate molecules into one, usually powered by \(\text{ATP}\); no second molecule is joined and no cofactor is consumed in this intramolecular rearrangement. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the EC classification scheme to a described reaction and distinguish an intramolecular rearrangement (isomerase) from an intermolecular group transfer (transferase) rather than reacting to the fact that a phosphate changed position.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '09da5d3c-49e2-4e10-929e-8ad7f8d6f21f';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '09da5d3c-49e2-4e10-929e-8ad7f8d6f21f';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('09da5d3c-49e2-4e10-929e-8ad7f8d6f21f', 'B', 'misconception', 'group moved therefore transferase'),
  ('09da5d3c-49e2-4e10-929e-8ad7f8d6f21f', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'phosphoester bond implies hydrolysis'),
  ('09da5d3c-49e2-4e10-929e-8ad7f8d6f21f', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'joining within one molecule mistaken for condensing two');

-- [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A single-active-site enzyme obeying Michaelis-Menten kinetics has a maximum velocity \(V_{max}\) of 100 µM/s and a Michaelis constant \(K_m\) of 5 mM for its substrate. A researcher runs the reaction under initial-rate conditions with the substrate concentration \([S]\) fixed at exactly 5 mM. What initial velocity \(v_0\) should the researcher expect to measure?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "25 µM/s"}, {"label": "B", "text": "50 µM/s"}, {"label": "C", "text": "75 µM/s"}, {"label": "D", "text": "100 µM/s"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' For a single-active-site enzyme, the initial velocity follows the Henri-Michaelis-Menten equation \(v_0 = \frac{V_{max}[S]}{K_m + [S]}\), which traces a rectangular hyperbola that saturates at \(V_{max}\) only when \([S]\) greatly exceeds \(K_m\) (the reference notes saturation is reached "only if S >> KS (or when S is approximately 100 x > Ks)," line 26951). The defining property of \(K_m\) is that it equals the substrate concentration at which the velocity is exactly half-maximal: the text states "S = KS when v = VM/2" (line 26944). Here \([S] = 5\text{ mM} = K_m\), so substituting gives \(v_0 = \frac{V_{max}\cdot K_m}{K_m + K_m} = \frac{V_{max}}{2}\). (Choice A) is incorrect: 25 µM/s is one-quarter of \(V_{max}\); a student would get this only by mistakenly squaring or otherwise mis-scaling the saturation fraction, but at \([S] = K_m\) the equation yields exactly one-half of \(V_{max}\), not one-quarter. (Choice B) is correct: because \([S]\) equals \(K_m\), the velocity is \(V_{max}/2 = 100/2 = 50\) µM/s, which is precisely the half-maximal velocity that defines the Michaelis constant (line 26944). (Choice C) is incorrect: 75 µM/s (three-quarters of \(V_{max}\)) would require \([S] = 3K_m\), not \([S] = K_m\); the hyperbolic curve rises toward \(V_{max}\) but has only reached its halfway point when \([S] = K_m\). (Choice D) is incorrect: 100 µM/s is \(V_{max}\) itself, which is approached only when \([S]\) is far above \(K_m\) (roughly 100-fold, line 26951); at \([S] = K_m\) the enzyme is only half-saturated, so it cannot be operating at maximum velocity. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Michaelis-Menten equation and the definition of \(K_m\) as the substrate concentration giving half-maximal velocity to compute \(v_0\) for a specified \([S]\).',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ed5411ff-158f-4d1f-91fc-1ee32c8f2a8e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ed5411ff-158f-4d1f-91fc-1ee32c8f2a8e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ed5411ff-158f-4d1f-91fc-1ee32c8f2a8e', 'A', 'misconception', 'mis-scales the saturation fraction (quarter instead of half at [S]=Km)'),
  ('ed5411ff-158f-4d1f-91fc-1ee32c8f2a8e', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'confuses [S]=Km with a higher multiple of Km giving three-quarters Vmax'),
  ('ed5411ff-158f-4d1f-91fc-1ee32c8f2a8e', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes [S]=Km means the enzyme is saturated at Vmax');

-- [Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In the second step of glycolysis, an enzyme converts glucose-6-phosphate into fructose-6-phosphate. The two compounds have identical molecular formulas; the reaction consumes and releases no water, requires no cofactor such as ATP or NAD⁺, and leaves the phosphate ester untouched throughout. The net transformation simply changes which carbon of the sugar bears the carbonyl group. Based on this chemistry, this enzyme belongs to which EC class?',
  options = '[{"text": "Isomerase", "label": "A"}, {"text": "Transferase", "label": "B"}, {"text": "Oxidoreductase", "label": "C"}, {"text": "Lyase", "label": "D"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The six EC classes are defined by the type of reaction catalyzed, and the first digit of an enzyme''s EC number encodes that class (LibreTexts Biochemistry, lines 17894-17904). The reaction described is glycolysis step 2, catalyzed by phosphoglucose isomerase (LibreTexts, line 2485), which rearranges a single substrate into a single product of identical formula. To classify it you cannot match a keyword in the stem to a class name; you must compare the substrate and product and ask what actually changed. (Choice A) is correct: glucose-6-phosphate and fructose-6-phosphate have the same molecular formula, and the enzyme merely shifts the carbonyl from C1 (an aldose) to C2 (a ketose) within one molecule, with no group gained, lost, or exchanged with a second molecule. Rearranging one molecule into an isomer of itself is the diagnostic reaction of an isomerase (EC 5). (Choice B) is incorrect: transferases (EC 2) move an intact functional group from a donor molecule to an acceptor molecule, so a student who fixates on the word ''phosphate'' in the substrate may assume a group is being handed off, but the stem states the phosphate ester is untouched and no second substrate is present, so nothing is transferred between molecules. (Choice C) is incorrect: oxidoreductases (EC 1) catalyze electron transfer, and converting an aldehyde (aldose) to a ketone (ketose) superficially resembles the aldehyde↔ketone chemistry of redox reactions; however, this interconversion proceeds through an enediol intermediate with no external electron acceptor and no cofactor such as NAD⁺, so the overall oxidation state of the sugar is unchanged. (Choice D) is incorrect: lyases (EC 4) break a C–C, C–O, or C–N bond by a non-hydrolytic, non-oxidative route, usually splitting one substrate into two products or creating a new double bond; here a single product of the same formula is formed and the carbon skeleton stays intact, so no bond is cleaved to yield a second fragment. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of the EC classes to a described reaction and infer, from the fact that one molecule is rearranged into an isomer of itself, that the enzyme is an isomerase rather than a transferase, oxidoreductase, or lyase.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8233e99d-b761-4a4f-a396-b1a512bcd631';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '8233e99d-b761-4a4f-a396-b1a512bcd631';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('8233e99d-b761-4a4f-a396-b1a512bcd631', 'B', 'misconception', 'Sees the phosphate group in the substrate name and assumes a group is being transferred, ignoring that no second molecule and no group handoff are present'),
  ('8233e99d-b761-4a4f-a396-b1a512bcd631', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'Treats the aldose-to-ketose (aldehyde-to-ketone) interconversion as a redox event, overlooking that oxidation state is unchanged and no electron acceptor/cofactor is involved'),
  ('8233e99d-b761-4a4f-a396-b1a512bcd631', 'D', 'misconception', 'Assumes forming a ketose from an aldose requires bond cleavage or double-bond formation typical of a lyase, when the skeleton stays intact and only one product forms');

-- [Cooperativity (T/R States, Sigmoidal Kinetics, Hill Coefficient)] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A biochemist assays a purified tetrameric enzyme, measuring initial velocity across a wide range of substrate concentrations. When the data are fit to the Hill equation, the best-fit Hill coefficient is \(n \approx 1.0\), and a plot of velocity versus \([S]\) is a rectangular hyperbola. Which conclusion about this enzyme is best supported by these results?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The four subunits bind substrate essentially independently, so the enzyme behaves like a simple non-cooperative Michaelis-Menten enzyme"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Binding of substrate to one subunit raises the affinity of the remaining subunits, indicating strong positive cooperativity"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Binding of substrate to one subunit lowers the affinity of the remaining subunits, indicating negative cooperativity"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Only one of the four subunits contains a functional substrate-binding site, because the Hill coefficient equals the number of binding sites"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The Hill coefficient is an empirical index of cooperativity: values greater than 1 indicate positive cooperativity (a sigmoidal curve), values less than 1 indicate negative cooperativity, and a value close to 1 with a hyperbolic curve signals no cooperativity, so the subunits bind substrate independently. The text illustrates exactly this: the cooperative phosphofructokinase isoform Pfk A has a Hill coefficient of 3.3, whereas ''Pfk B shows hyperbolic kinetics and no allosterism, with a Hill coefficient close to 1'' (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, line 21266), and the Hill coefficient is described as a parameter ''reflecting cooperativity'' (line 24226). (Choice A) is correct: a Hill coefficient near 1 combined with a rectangular-hyperbolic velocity curve is the fingerprint of a non-cooperative enzyme whose subunits act independently, matching the non-allosteric, hyperbolic behavior of Pfk B (line 21266); such an enzyme obeys ordinary Michaelis-Menten kinetics even though it is multisubunit. (Choice B) is incorrect: positive cooperativity would raise the Hill coefficient above 1 and produce a sigmoidal (S-shaped) curve, as seen for cooperative hemoglobin, where plots of fractional saturation are ''sigmoidal for Hb, suggesting cooperative binding'' (line 20391) and for Pfk A with its Hill coefficient of 3.3; the observed \(n \approx 1.0\) and hyperbolic shape rule this out, so assuming a multisubunit enzyme must be cooperative is the trap here. (Choice C) is incorrect: negative cooperativity, in which ''ligand affinities'' decrease as sites fill (line 20862), drives the Hill coefficient below 1, whereas the measured value sits at 1, not beneath it. (Choice D) is incorrect: the Hill coefficient is a fitted measure of cooperativity, not a count of binding sites; hemoglobin has four oxygen sites yet is best fit with a Hill coefficient of only 2.8 (line 20770), so \(n \approx 1\) does not mean the tetramer has just one functional site. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the meaning of the Hill coefficient and curve shape to infer that a Hill coefficient near 1 with hyperbolic kinetics reflects independent, non-cooperative substrate binding rather than any form of cooperativity.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '886f9638-af34-4ca1-8983-f38cca1d5a2c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '886f9638-af34-4ca1-8983-f38cca1d5a2c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('886f9638-af34-4ca1-8983-f38cca1d5a2c', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes any multisubunit enzyme must be positively cooperative and sigmoidal'),
  ('886f9638-af34-4ca1-8983-f38cca1d5a2c', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'correct definition of negative cooperativity but requires a Hill coefficient below 1'),
  ('886f9638-af34-4ca1-8983-f38cca1d5a2c', 'D', 'misconception', 'conflates the Hill coefficient with the number of substrate-binding sites');

-- [Local Conditions: Temperature, pH & Salinity] ans=B hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studies a newly purified enzyme whose catalytic mechanism depends on a single essential active-site histidine. Mechanistic studies show that this histidine, which has an apparent pKa of about 6, must abstract a proton from the substrate during turnover, so it can only participate in catalysis when its imidazole side chain is in the deprotonated form. Assuming this residue''s protonation state is the dominant factor governing activity, at which pH is the enzyme most likely to show its highest catalytic rate?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Around pH 6, because catalysis is fastest when the pH equals the essential residue''s pKa"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A pH somewhat above 6 (roughly pH 7-8), because that keeps the essential histidine predominantly deprotonated"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A pH well below 6 (acidic), because a low pH drives the imidazole into its catalytically required form"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The highest attainable pH (around pH 13), because the more fully deprotonated the histidine is, the greater the rate"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' An enzyme''s pH optimum reflects the pH at which its essential catalytic side chains are held in the protonation state required for catalysis; the text notes that pH ''has a marked effect on the velocity of enzyme-catalyzed reactions'' and that ''key side chains necessary for catalysis must be in the correct protonation state,'' illustrating this with a residue of ''apparent pKa of around 6'' that ''must be deprotonated for optimal activity of trypsin, which shows increases in activity with pH centered at pH 6'' (lines 28499, 28511-28512). By the Henderson-Hasselbalch relationship, an ionizable group is only ~50% deprotonated at pH = pKa and becomes predominantly (>90%) deprotonated only when pH rises roughly one to two units above its pKa; because this histidine must be deprotonated to act as the general base, activity climbs as the pH moves above 6 and peaks where the residue is largely deprotonated but before extremes of pH denature the protein. (Choice A) is incorrect: at pH equal to the pKa of about 6 the imidazole is only half deprotonated, so only about half of the enzyme population has its catalytic residue in the active form; equating the optimum with the pKa mistakes the point of 50% ionization for the point of maximal activity, which actually lies above the pKa where the deprotonated form predominates. (Choice B) is correct: because deprotonation is favored as pH rises above the pKa, a pH of roughly 7-8 leaves the essential histidine predominantly deprotonated and therefore catalytically competent, matching the text''s principle that activity increases with pH as a residue with pKa near 6 becomes deprotonated (lines 28511-28512). (Choice C) is incorrect: lowering the pH below 6 pushes the equilibrium toward the protonated imidazolium form, which is the catalytically inactive state for a residue that must be deprotonated to abstract a proton; this choice reverses the required protonation direction, confusing the acidic conditions that suit a residue needing to be protonated with those that suit one needing to be deprotonated. (Choice D) is incorrect: although raising the pH increases the fraction of deprotonated histidine, once the residue is essentially fully deprotonated (a plateau reached a couple of units above its pKa) further increases yield no additional active enzyme, and extremes of pH cause irreversible denaturation, so pH ~13 would abolish rather than maximize activity; this choice overgeneralizes ''more deprotonation is better'' past the point of diminishing returns and protein unfolding. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between a residue''s pKa and its pH-dependent protonation state to predict where the essential histidine is deprotonated, and therefore where the enzyme is most active, rather than reading an optimum off the stem or matching it to the residue''s pKa value.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '60b4bccd-8649-4eb5-b94f-2da6cc58b6f8';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '60b4bccd-8649-4eb5-b94f-2da6cc58b6f8';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('60b4bccd-8649-4eb5-b94f-2da6cc58b6f8', 'A', 'misconception', 'treating pH = pKa as the point of maximal activity rather than 50% ionization'),
  ('60b4bccd-8649-4eb5-b94f-2da6cc58b6f8', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'reversing the required protonation direction (acidic to protonate a residue that must be deprotonated)'),
  ('60b4bccd-8649-4eb5-b94f-2da6cc58b6f8', 'D', 'misconception', 'assuming more deprotonation always means more activity, ignoring the ionization plateau and pH denaturation');

-- [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher adds a small molecule to an enzyme assay and finds that the reaction slows. When the substrate concentration is raised to a very large excess, the reaction reaches the same maximum velocity it had before the molecule was added; however, at that same maximum, the enzyme now requires a higher substrate concentration to reach half of its maximal rate. Based on this kinetic behavior, where does the molecule most likely bind?',
  options = '[{"text": "The active site, so that a large excess of substrate can outcompete it and drive the enzyme back to its full maximum velocity", "label": "A"}, {"text": "A site separate from the active site on the free enzyme, lowering the maximum velocity by an amount that excess substrate cannot overcome", "label": "B"}, {"text": "Only the enzyme-substrate complex once substrate is already bound, decreasing both the maximum velocity and the substrate concentration needed for half-maximal rate", "label": "C"}, {"text": "Both the free enzyme and the enzyme-substrate complex with equal affinity, reducing the maximum velocity while leaving the half-maximal substrate concentration unchanged", "label": "D"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The diagnostic here is the pair of kinetic observations: a very large excess of substrate fully restores the original maximum velocity (\(V_M\) unchanged), while a higher substrate concentration is now needed to reach half-maximal rate (apparent \(K_M\) increased). The textbook explains that with a competitive inhibitor, ''as S increases to infinity, all E is converted to ES ... VM is not changed,'' but the inhibitor shifting the E + S equilibrium leftward means ''it would appear that the affinity of E and S has decreased,'' raising the apparent \(K_M\) (lines 27830-27836). That signature—\(V_M\) recoverable by excess substrate, \(K_M\) up—points to a molecule that occupies the active site itself, since only there can added substrate directly displace it. (Choice A) is correct: a molecule that competes for the active site is outcompeted when substrate is in large excess, restoring \(V_M\), and it raises the apparent \(K_M\) exactly as the data show, because ''substrate (S) and inhibitor (I) both bind to the same site on the enzyme'' and compete for the active site (lines 27809-27810). (Choice B) is incorrect: a molecule that binds a separate site and lowers \(V_M\) in a way excess substrate cannot overcome describes noncompetitive inhibition, in which the inhibitor ''does not enter into the pocket where the substrate ... binds'' (line 28356); that would leave \(V_M\) depressed, contradicting the observed full recovery of maximum velocity. (Choice C) is incorrect: a molecule binding only the enzyme-substrate complex describes uncompetitive inhibition, where ''I binds only to ES and not free E'' so that ''both Vm and Km decrease'' (lines 28099, 28121)—but here \(V_M\) is fully restored and \(K_M\) rises, the opposite pattern. (Choice D) is incorrect: a molecule binding free enzyme and the ES complex with equal affinity and leaving the half-maximal substrate concentration unchanged describes pure noncompetitive inhibition, in which ''KM remains unchanged'' while \(V_M\) falls (line 27766); the data instead show \(V_M\) recoverable and \(K_M\) increased. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the kinetic signature—full recovery of \(V_M\) at high substrate with an increased apparent \(K_M\)—to infer that the molecule binds the active site and competes with substrate.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '5c7ad5d0-aa16-4274-ae07-a612b3192178';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '5c7ad5d0-aa16-4274-ae07-a612b3192178';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('5c7ad5d0-aa16-4274-ae07-a612b3192178', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses noncompetitive (VM down, unrecoverable) with competitive'),
  ('5c7ad5d0-aa16-4274-ae07-a612b3192178', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses uncompetitive (both VM and KM down) with competitive'),
  ('5c7ad5d0-aa16-4274-ae07-a612b3192178', 'D', 'misconception', 'confuses pure noncompetitive (KM unchanged, VM down) with competitive');

-- [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A pharmacologist studies how a reversible small-molecule inhibitor affects a Michaelis-Menten enzyme. Comparing initial-rate data collected with and without the inhibitor, they find that the inhibitor lowers the reaction''s maximum velocity (\(V_{max}\)) but leaves the Michaelis constant (\(K_m\)) unchanged. On a Lineweaver-Burk plot, the lines for the inhibited and uninhibited reactions intersect on the x-axis. Which class of reversible inhibition do these results indicate?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Noncompetitive inhibition"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Competitive inhibition"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Uncompetitive inhibition"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Mixed inhibition"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Each class of reversible inhibition leaves a distinct fingerprint on the two Michaelis-Menten parameters, so the class can be read off the pattern of change in \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\): the reference notes that in pure noncompetitive inhibition ''\(K_m\) remains unchanged... while both parameters change in mixed inhibition'' (line 27766). Because the inhibitor here binds free enzyme and the enzyme-substrate complex with equal affinity, it lowers the apparent \(V_{max}\) but does not perturb the E + S ⇌ ES equilibrium, so \(K_m\) stays the same and the Lineweaver-Burk lines share a common x-intercept. (Choice A) is correct: a decreased \(V_{max}\) with an unchanged \(K_m\) — lines intersecting on the x-axis — is the hallmark of noncompetitive inhibition, in which the inhibitor ''binds to both E and ES'' with equal (Kis = Kii) affinity, so ''the apparent \(V_m\)... is less than the real \(V_m\)'' while ''the apparent \(K_m\)... will not change'' (lines 28225-28226, 28241-28242). (Choice B) is incorrect: competitive inhibitors bind the same site as substrate ''in a mutually exclusive fashion'' (line 27809-27810), so excess substrate outcompetes them; \(V_{max}\) is unchanged while \(K_m\) increases — the opposite of the unchanged \(K_m\) and lowered \(V_{max}\) observed here. (Choice C) is incorrect: uncompetitive inhibitors bind ''only to ES and not free E'' (line 28099), which lowers both the apparent \(K_m\) and \(V_{max}\) together (line 28152); here \(K_m\) does not fall, so the data exclude this class. (Choice D) is incorrect: mixed inhibitors bind E and ES with different affinities (Kis ≠ Kii), so ''both parameters change'' — \(V_{max}\) falls and \(K_m\) shifts (line 27766); an unchanged \(K_m\) rules out mixed inhibition and points specifically to the pure noncompetitive case. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the diagnostic effects of each inhibition class on \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) to the reported data and deduce that an unchanged \(K_m\) with a lowered \(V_{max}\) identifies noncompetitive inhibition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f3512c92-730c-409b-8c6c-59ee3300dd8e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f3512c92-730c-409b-8c6c-59ee3300dd8e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f3512c92-730c-409b-8c6c-59ee3300dd8e', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses which parameter each inhibitor class alters (thinks any inhibitor lowers activity the same way)'),
  ('f3512c92-730c-409b-8c6c-59ee3300dd8e', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'recalls uncompetitive lowers Vmax but forgets it also lowers Km'),
  ('f3512c92-730c-409b-8c6c-59ee3300dd8e', 'D', 'misconception', 'treats mixed as the default two-parameter answer, ignoring that Km is unchanged');

-- [Reversible Inhibition (Competitive, Noncompetitive, Uncompetitive, Mixed)] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher measures the kinetics of an enzyme in the presence of a reversible small-molecule inhibitor. Compared with the uninhibited reaction, both the apparent \(V_{max}\) and the apparent \(K_m\) are lowered by the same factor, and a Lineweaver–Burk plot yields a line parallel to the control. Based on these data, the inhibitor most likely binds to which of the following?',
  options = '[{"text": "Only the enzyme–substrate (ES) complex", "label": "A"}, {"text": "Only the free enzyme, at a site distinct from the active site", "label": "B"}, {"text": "Only the free enzyme, directly within the active site", "label": "C"}, {"text": "Both the free enzyme and the enzyme–substrate (ES) complex with equal affinity", "label": "D"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Each class of reversible inhibitor leaves a distinct fingerprint on \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\), so the binding target can be deduced from the kinetics rather than assumed. The text states that for uncompetitive inhibition ''both Vm and Km decrease'' to ''the same extent,'' producing ''a series of parallel lines, which is the hallmark of uncompetitive inhibition'' (lines 28121–28123), and that this occurs because the inhibitor ''binds only to ES and not free E'' after a substrate-induced conformational change opens its binding site (line 28099). The reported signature—proportional decreases in \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) with parallel double-reciprocal lines—therefore points to an inhibitor that acts only on the ES complex. (Choice A) is correct: the parallel Lineweaver–Burk lines and the proportional drop in both \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) are the defining kinetic hallmark of uncompetitive inhibition, which arises only when the inhibitor binds solely to the ES complex. (Choice B) is incorrect: an inhibitor that binds only the free enzyme at a site distinct from the active site produces allosteric competitive inhibition—the text notes that when S and I bind different sites and their binding is mutually exclusive, this ''would give the same kinetic data'' as competitive inhibition (lines 27821–27823), namely \(V_{max}\) unchanged while the apparent \(K_m\) increases (lines 27831–27832, 27845)—the opposite of the observed drop in \(K_m\). (Choice C) is incorrect: an inhibitor binding the active site of free enzyme is competitive, and the text shows this leaves \(V_{max}\) unchanged while the apparent \(K_m\) increases (lines 27831–27832, 27845)—the opposite of the observed decrease in \(K_m\). (Choice D) is incorrect: binding both E and ES with equal affinity is classic noncompetitive inhibition, which lowers \(V_{max}\) but leaves \(K_m\) unchanged because binding does not perturb the E + S ⇌ ES equilibrium (lines 28241–28242); the observed \(K_m\) decrease rules it out. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the diagnostic \(V_{max}\) and \(K_m\) signatures of the reversible inhibition types to infer the inhibitor''s binding target from kinetic data.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'b4f1e29a-10e7-4547-bbe0-d5239a177040';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b4f1e29a-10e7-4547-bbe0-d5239a177040';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b4f1e29a-10e7-4547-bbe0-d5239a177040', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'confusing the kinetic signature of one inhibition type (allosteric competitive: Km up) with uncompetitive (Km down)'),
  ('b4f1e29a-10e7-4547-bbe0-d5239a177040', 'C', 'misconception', 'assuming all inhibitors bind the active site of free enzyme'),
  ('b4f1e29a-10e7-4547-bbe0-d5239a177040', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'confusing noncompetitive (Km unchanged) with uncompetitive (Km decreased)');

-- [Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'In a flavin-dependent dehydrogenase, the FAD coenzyme is anchored to the active site through a covalent bond to a histidine side chain. During a single turnover, this FAD accepts a hydride from the substrate and is thereby reduced to FADH₂. For the enzyme to carry out a second, chemically identical catalytic cycle on a new substrate molecule, which of the following must occur?',
  options = '[{"text": "The reduced flavin, while remaining bound at the active site, must be re-oxidized back to FAD by a separate electron acceptor.", "label": "A"}, {"text": "The reduced flavin must dissociate from the active site and be exchanged for a fresh FAD molecule drawn from the cytoplasmic pool.", "label": "B"}, {"text": "The apoenzyme must first release the flavin so that a newly folded holoenzyme can re-form around it.", "label": "C"}, {"text": "An allosteric activator must bind a regulatory site to switch the reduced enzyme back into its catalytically competent conformation.", "label": "D"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The textbook distinguishes loosely bound coenzymes, which dissociate and act as substrates, from tightly or covalently bound coenzymes (prosthetic groups), which stay attached; because such a coenzyme cannot leave, it states that after its reaction ''the enzyme would no longer be able to use the cofactor in another round of catalysis until the initial state of the cofactor is reformed, which takes another chemical reaction.'' It specifically notes that FAD/FADH₂ are ''tightly or covalently attached to an enzyme, so FAD must be regenerated in each catalytic cycle.'' You must reason from the covalent attachment to what the enzyme needs before it can turn over again. (Choice A) is correct: because the flavin is covalently tethered it cannot be swapped out, so the enzyme''s spent FADH₂ must be reset in place — re-oxidized back to FAD by a separate electron acceptor — before another identical cycle can occur, exactly the in-place regeneration the text describes for FAD. (Choice B) is incorrect: dissociating and replacing the coenzyme from a free pool is how a loosely bound co-substrate behaves; a covalently bound flavin is physically incapable of leaving to be exchanged, so this route is unavailable to the enzyme. (Choice C) is incorrect: the apoenzyme is by definition the protein without its cofactor, so it cannot ''release the flavin,'' and enzymes do not unfold and re-fold between turnovers; restoring activity requires chemical regeneration of the coenzyme, not reassembly of the protein. (Choice D) is incorrect: the enzyme is not switched off by a regulatory conformational change but is chemically ''used up'' at the flavin (now FADH₂); allosteric activators modulate activity at a separate site and cannot re-oxidize a reduced coenzyme. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the consequence of tight/covalent coenzyme binding — that the coenzyme must be regenerated on the enzyme rather than exchanged — to determine what a second catalytic cycle requires.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '4f93bedf-5397-4ba5-8ecc-c8c66d75e95d';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '4f93bedf-5397-4ba5-8ecc-c8c66d75e95d';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('4f93bedf-5397-4ba5-8ecc-c8c66d75e95d', 'B', 'misconception', 'applies loosely-bound co-substrate (dissociate-and-replace) behavior to a covalently bound coenzyme'),
  ('4f93bedf-5397-4ba5-8ecc-c8c66d75e95d', 'C', 'misconception', 'confuses apoenzyme/holoenzyme terms and imagines protein reassembly instead of coenzyme regeneration'),
  ('4f93bedf-5397-4ba5-8ecc-c8c66d75e95d', 'D', 'misconception', 'treats a chemically spent coenzyme as an allosteric regulatory problem');

-- [Amino Acid Structure & Stereochemistry] ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Every standard amino acid used in proteins is assigned to the L family, and each chiral L-residue is \(S\) at the α-carbon — with one exception: L-cysteine is \(R\). A student notes that cysteine has the same L configuration as serine, alanine, and the others, so its \(R\) label cannot come from the amino group, the carboxyl group, or the α-hydrogen, which occupy identical positions in every L-residue. What is the reason L-cysteine, specifically, receives the opposite absolute-configuration descriptor from the other L-amino acids?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Cysteine''s side chain places a sulfur atom near the α-carbon; because sulfur outranks oxygen in Cahn–Ingold–Prelog priority, that side-chain branch is ranked ahead of the carboxyl group, swapping two substituent priorities and reversing the \\(R\\)/\\(S\\) label without altering the spatial arrangement."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The α-carbon of cysteine is built with the mirror-image spatial arrangement of the other L-residues, so it is genuinely inverted at that center even though it is still called L."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The thiol of cysteine ionizes to a thiolate more readily than a hydroxyl or carboxyl group, and this deprotonation raises the side chain''s priority enough to flip the descriptor from \\(S\\) to \\(R\\)."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cysteine is actually a D-amino acid that is conventionally grouped with the L-residues, and its true D reference configuration is what produces the \\(R\\) descriptor."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The L/D system fixes the α-carbon''s spatial arrangement by reference to L-glyceraldehyde (the "CORN" orientation), while the \(R\)/\(S\) descriptor is assigned separately from Cahn–Ingold–Prelog (CIP) priorities, which rank substituents by atomic number. Because the descriptor depends on how the four substituents are ranked, changing the identity of the side chain can flip \(R\)/\(S\) even when the physical arrangement is unchanged: the textbook notes that "L-cysteine is also (R)-cysteine," while "all the other L-amino acids are (S)," and that this "reflects the human decision to give a sulfur atom a higher priority than a carbon atom and does not reflect a real difference in configuration" (LibreTexts 5460–5461). (Choice A) is correct: in every standard residue three substituents on the α-carbon are the amino nitrogen, the carboxyl carbon (bonded to O, O, O), and H; the fourth is the side chain. For most residues the side-chain carbon loses priority to the carboxyl carbon, giving \(S\). In cysteine the side chain is –CH\(_2\)–SH, so its first carbon is bonded to sulfur; comparing {O,O,O} at the carboxyl carbon against {S,H,H} at the side-chain carbon, sulfur (Z = 16) outranks oxygen (Z = 8), so the side-chain branch is now ranked ahead of the carboxyl group. That swaps priorities 2 and 3 and reverses the descriptor to \(R\) with no change in the actual spatial arrangement. (Choice B) is incorrect: the text stresses the differing label "does not reflect a real difference in configuration," so cysteine''s α-carbon is not mirror-imaged relative to the other L-residues; only the ranking of its groups differs. (Choice C) is incorrect: CIP priority is set by the atomic numbers of the bonded atoms, not by how readily a group ionizes; the sulfur outranks oxygen whether or not the thiol is deprotonated, so protonation state is not the cause. (Choice D) is incorrect: cysteine is a genuine L-amino acid sharing the CORN arrangement of the others; it is not a relabeled D-residue, which is exactly why one L arrangement can still map to \(R\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply CIP priority rules to the sulfur-bearing side chain to determine why one fixed spatial configuration yields the opposite absolute-configuration descriptor.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2434f09f-b70c-408f-8396-bd484d105d84';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2434f09f-b70c-408f-8396-bd484d105d84';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2434f09f-b70c-408f-8396-bd484d105d84', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing a descriptor change with a real spatial inversion'),
  ('2434f09f-b70c-408f-8396-bd484d105d84', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'invoking real thiol ionization chemistry as a false cause for the CIP flip'),
  ('2434f09f-b70c-408f-8396-bd484d105d84', 'D', 'misconception', 'believing the R label means the residue is really D');

-- [Gastrulation] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During gastrulation in a triploblastic embryo, a researcher applies a permanent fluorescent label to every cell of one primary germ layer and then examines the fully developed animal. In the adult, the label appears in the dermis of the skin, the walls of the blood vessels, the heart, and the kidneys, but it is absent from the epidermis, from the epithelial lining of the lungs, and from the brain. Which germ layer did the researcher most likely label, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Endoderm, because it gives rise to the epithelial lining of the lungs and the digestive tract"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ectoderm, because it gives rise to the epidermis of the skin and the tissue of the brain"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Mesoderm, because it gives rise to connective tissue, circulatory structures, and the excretory organs"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Endoderm, because it gives rise to the kidneys and the walls of the blood vessels"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Gastrulation & Germ Layers.'' During gastrulation the three primary germ layers are established, and each is programmed to give rise to a specific set of adult tissues: the ectoderm forms the outer epidermis and the central nervous system, the endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts, and the mesoderm forms muscle, connective tissue, blood, and most visceral organs. To identify the labeled layer you must classify each observed tissue by its embryonic origin—recognizing, for example, that the dermis is connective tissue (mesoderm) even though the epidermis directly above it is ectoderm, and that a blood-vessel wall is mesodermal even though the space it encloses connects to organs of other origins. (Choice C) is correct: the mesoderm "gives rise to all specialized muscle tissues... connective tissues such as the skeleton and blood cells, and most other visceral organs such as the kidneys and the spleen" (OpenStax Biology 2e); the dermis is dermal connective tissue, the vessel walls and heart are muscular/connective circulatory structures, and the kidneys are the excretory visceral organs, so all four labeled tissues trace to mesoderm while the unlabeled epidermis, lung lining, and brain do not. (Choice A) is incorrect: the endoderm does form the epithelial lining of the lungs and gut, but those tissues were explicitly unlabeled in this experiment, so a layer whose derivatives are unlabeled cannot be the marked layer. (Choice B) is incorrect: the ectoderm does form the epidermis and the brain, but both of those were unlabeled as well, so ectoderm is contradicted by the same evidence that its listed derivatives carry no label. (Choice D) is incorrect: although the kidneys and blood-vessel walls were indeed labeled, these are mesodermal derivatives, not endodermal ones—the endoderm produces the lining of the gut and respiratory tract, so assigning excretory and circulatory tissues to the endoderm misidentifies their germ-layer origin. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the known derivatives of each germ layer to an observed labeling pattern in order to deduce which layer was marked.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5', 'A', 'partial_truth', 'cites a correct germ-layer fact (endoderm forms lung and gut lining) but those tissues were unlabeled, so it fails to match the evidence'),
  ('792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5', 'B', 'partial_truth', 'cites a correct germ-layer fact (ectoderm forms epidermis and brain) but those tissues were unlabeled, so it is contradicted by the evidence'),
  ('792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5', 'D', 'misconception', 'mis-assigns mesodermal excretory/circulatory tissues (kidneys, vessel walls) to the endoderm because they sound internal or fluid-related');

-- [Gestation & Birth] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During labor in a healthy full-term pregnancy, stretch of the cervix by the descending fetal head sends nerve impulses to the hypothalamus, which signals the posterior pituitary to release oxytocin; oxytocin then makes uterine smooth muscle contract. Most hormonal control in the body is arranged so that a hormone''s downstream effect ultimately dampens further release of that hormone. If the oxytocin-cervix relay during labor instead behaved like most of the body''s hormonal systems, how would the strength of uterine contractions be predicted to change as delivery approaches, and how does this differ from what actually occurs?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "It would settle toward a steady level, because a hormone''s effect normally feeds back to limit its own release; in labor the opposite happens, and contractions instead escalate until birth"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It would escalate without limit, because a hormone''s effect normally feeds back to amplify its own release; in labor this is overridden, and contractions instead hold steady until birth"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It would settle toward a steady level, because a hormone''s effect normally feeds back to limit its own release; labor works the same way, holding contractions constant until birth"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It would escalate without limit, because a hormone''s effect normally feeds back to amplify its own release; labor works the same way, and this escalation is what pushes contractions to their peak at birth"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Fetal Circulation & Birth.'' Most hormonal systems run on negative feedback: the hormone''s downstream effect loops back to dampen further release, holding the variable near a set point—this is the predominant mechanism of homeostasis (as with insulin lowering the blood glucose that triggered it). Labor is one of the rare exceptions, a positive feedback relay in which contractions drive the fetus harder against the cervix, causing more stretch, more oxytocin, and still stronger contractions, so the correct choice must both apply the negative-feedback default to the hypothetical AND recognize that real labor does the opposite. (Choice A) is correct: under the body''s usual negative feedback, oxytocin''s contractile effect would feed back to inhibit further oxytocin release, settling contractions toward a steady level; because labor is actually a positive feedback loop, contractions instead escalate until delivery, which is the observed difference. (Choice B) is incorrect: it inverts the definition of negative feedback—normal hormonal feedback limits rather than amplifies a hormone''s own release—and it also gets real labor backward by claiming contractions hold steady when they actually intensify. (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly describes the negative-feedback default but then wrongly says labor ''works the same way,'' erasing the very exception the question turns on; real contractions do not stay constant. (Choice D) is incorrect: it misstates the usual rule as amplifying (that is positive, not negative, feedback) and, although it lands on escalating contractions for real labor, it does so for the wrong reason and denies that labor differs from the typical hormonal arrangement. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of negative and positive feedback to a described signaling pathway, predict the counterfactual behavior under the body''s default control mode, and contrast it with the actual escalation seen in parturition.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '19f81735-b461-4eb2-bc4b-98850fecd6a6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '19f81735-b461-4eb2-bc4b-98850fecd6a6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('19f81735-b461-4eb2-bc4b-98850fecd6a6', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'Swaps the definitions of positive and negative feedback and inverts the real labor outcome'),
  ('19f81735-b461-4eb2-bc4b-98850fecd6a6', 'C', 'misconception', 'Applies the negative-feedback homeostatic default to labor, missing that labor is the exception'),
  ('19f81735-b461-4eb2-bc4b-98850fecd6a6', 'D', 'misconception', 'Mislabels the body''s default hormonal control as amplifying and claims labor is typical rather than exceptional');

-- [Tissues: Connective Tissue] ans=D medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Six weeks after a deep laceration to a patient''s forearm, a biopsy of the healed dermis shows a firm scar: the region is now packed with densely woven collagen fibers embedded in ground substance, giving the skin high tensile strength but noticeably less flexibility than the surrounding uninjured tissue. Notably, the biopsy sample of dermis contains no lacunae. Which resident cell of the dermis was chiefly responsible for depositing the collagen-rich matrix of this scar?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The mast cell, a granule-filled cell that becomes active early after injury when the tissue is irritated or damaged"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The macrophage, a large motile cell that is abundant at a healing wound site and migrates in from the bloodstream"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The chondrocyte, a matrix-secreting cell that sits within lacunae in its tissue"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The fibroblast, a motile, spindle-shaped cell that undergoes mitosis and is the resident cell of connective tissue proper"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'D',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).'' Connective tissue is built from protein fibers (collagen, elastic, reticular) suspended in a ground substance, and the textbook names the fibroblast as "the principal cell of connective tissues," the cell that "makes the fibers found in nearly all of the connective tissues." A dermal scar is dense fibrous connective tissue proper, so the cell that lays down its collagen-rich matrix must be the resident matrix-producer of that specific tissue — and the reported absence of lacunae is the clue that separates it from the cartilage matrix-maker. (Choice A) is incorrect: the mast cell is a granule-filled leukocyte that "produces inflammatory molecules, such as histamine" (and heparin), releasing them when tissue is irritated or damaged; it participates in the early wound response but does not synthesize the structural collagen matrix of the scar. (Choice B) is incorrect: the macrophage is a phagocyte that arises from a blood monocyte and is abundant at a healing wound, but its job is to "engulf pathogens and cellular debris," clearing the wound rather than depositing collagen. (Choice C) is incorrect: the chondrocyte truly is a matrix- and fiber-secreting cell, which makes it tempting, but the textbook specifies that chondrocytes "make the matrix and fibers of the tissue" within cartilage and are "found in spaces within the tissue called lacunae" — the biopsy explicitly contains no lacunae and is dermis, not cartilage, so a chondrocyte is not present to build this scar. (Choice D) is correct: the fibroblast is the resident cell of connective tissue proper, the tissue that makes up the dermis, and it is the cell that secretes the collagen fibers and polysaccharide-rich ground substance forming the strong, less-flexible scar; unlike the chondrocyte it does not sit in lacunae, consistent with the lacuna-free biopsy. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your knowledge of each connective-tissue cell''s distinct function and location to an observed healing outcome and infer which cell produced the collagen-rich matrix, distinguishing it from the cartilage cell that also makes matrix but resides in lacunae.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2b550209-9748-4120-b49a-bffa51b34c2a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2b550209-9748-4120-b49a-bffa51b34c2a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2b550209-9748-4120-b49a-bffa51b34c2a', 'A', 'partial_truth', 'Right tissue, wrong role — mast cell is a real dermal connective-tissue cell active in wound healing but releases inflammatory mediators rather than building matrix'),
  ('2b550209-9748-4120-b49a-bffa51b34c2a', 'B', 'adjacent_fact', 'Prominent-at-wound cell — macrophage is abundant during healing but phagocytoses debris rather than depositing collagen'),
  ('2b550209-9748-4120-b49a-bffa51b34c2a', 'C', 'misconception', 'Also-a-matrix-maker in the wrong tissue — chondrocyte secretes matrix and fibers, but in cartilage within lacunae, defeated by the lacuna-free dermal clue');

-- [Neuron Functional Types] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A histologist maps a single neuron isolated from a peripheral nerve. A lone process emerges from the cell body and, a short distance away, splits into two branches that run in opposite directions along the same fiber. One branch extends far into the periphery and the other extends toward the neuraxis; neither dendrites nor an obvious cluster of processes radiate from the cell body itself. The cell body sits within a ganglion that lies partway along the fiber''s course rather than at either of its ends. The histologist records no information about the direction in which signals travel. Based solely on this architecture, the neuron is best classified by function as:',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A sensory (afferent) neuron"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A motor (efferent) neuron"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An interneuron (association neuron)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A postganglionic autonomic motor neuron"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Nervous System Organization.'' Neurons can be grouped by structural shape (unipolar, bipolar, multipolar) or by function (sensory/afferent, motor/efferent, interneuron), and the two schemes are correlated. The described cell has a single process that emerges from the cell body and splits into two oppositely directed branches, with no dendrites radiating from the soma and the cell body seated in a ganglion partway along the fiber—this is the signature of a unipolar (pseudo-unipolar) neuron. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e states that human unipolar cells "have an axon that emerges from the cell body, but it splits" so that one end bears dendritic endings and the other synapses in the central nervous system, that "the cell bodies of unipolar neurons are always found in ganglia," and critically that "unipolar cells are exclusively sensory neurons." Because no directional information is given, the functional class must be inferred from shape and location alone, and only one shape maps to a single function. (Choice A) is correct: the one-process-that-bifurcates morphology with a ganglionic cell body lying along the pathway is the pseudo-unipolar form, and unipolar neurons are exclusively sensory (afferent), so the architecture fixes the function even without knowing the direction of signal flow. (Choice B) is incorrect: motor (efferent) neurons are multipolar, with an axon and multiple dendrites radiating from the cell body, and their somata lie in the CNS gray matter or in autonomic ganglia—not in a single bifurcating process seated in a sensory ganglion. (Choice C) is incorrect: an interneuron is multipolar and lies entirely within the CNS, so a cell whose body sits in a peripheral ganglion with a process extending into the periphery cannot be an interneuron. (Choice D) is incorrect: this is the strongest trap because a postganglionic autonomic neuron also has its cell body in a peripheral ganglion, but the text specifies that autonomic ganglionic neurons are multipolar "with dendrites radiating out around the cell body," whereas the described cell has a single process that merely splits—so the shape excludes it, and being multipolar it is efferent, not sensory. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structure-to-function correlation of neuron classification—recognizing that a single bifurcating process with a ganglionic, along-the-path cell body is the pseudo-unipolar shape and that this shape is exclusively sensory—to deduce the functional class without any direction-of-flow cue.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'a713b712-af90-4ec8-97c3-69b2217dc0d5';
