-- [Neuron Functional Types] ans=C medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During a flexor-withdrawal reflex, a subject rapidly pulls a hand away from a hot surface. Researchers selectively silence one class of neuron confined to the spinal gray matter and repeat the test. The flexor (biceps) still contracts normally and the hand still withdraws, but the antagonist extensor (triceps) now remains contracted throughout the movement, briefly co-contracting against the withdrawal instead of going slack. Every muscle in the arm remains capable of contracting when its own motor nerve is stimulated directly. Which functional class of neuron did the researchers most likely silence?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A ventral-horn efferent neuron whose axon leaves the cord and projects to the triceps"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A ventral-horn efferent neuron whose axon leaves the cord and projects to the biceps"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A spinal cell interposed between the incoming sensory afferent and the triceps'' motor neuron"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A dorsal-root afferent neuron conducting nociceptive input from the skin into the cord"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Nervous System Organization.'' In a spinal withdrawal reflex, sensory input is integrated within the gray matter before a motor response is produced, and smooth withdrawal requires the antagonist muscle to relax while the agonist contracts. Because the neuromuscular junction is strictly excitatory and skeletal muscle cannot actively relax, relaxation of the triceps cannot be commanded directly at the muscle; it can only happen if the triceps'' own motor neuron is quieted by another cell, an interposed spinal interneuron that receives the sensory signal and hyperpolarizes that motor neuron. (Choice C) is correct: OpenStax describes an interneuron in the spinal cord that, driven by the sensory neuron, acts on "the motor neuron that controls the triceps brachii" and "hyperpolarizes" it so the triceps relaxes; removing this interposed cell leaves the triceps'' motor neuron unopposed, so it keeps firing and the triceps stays contracted—exactly the observed co-contraction. (Choice A) is incorrect: silencing the triceps'' own efferent neuron would leave the triceps with no drive to contract, so it would go slack—the opposite of the observed persistent contraction—which is the trap for anyone matching "triceps" in the stem to "triceps" in the option without tracing the circuit. (Choice B) is incorrect: the biceps efferent drives the flexor contraction that the experiment reports as still normal, so it was not the silenced cell, and silencing it would weaken withdrawal rather than change the extensor. (Choice D) is incorrect: silencing the afferent would remove the trigger for the whole arc, abolishing the biceps contraction and the withdrawal as well, yet both were preserved. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the roles of sensory, motor, and interneurons to a reflex-arc perturbation and infer that antagonist relaxation depends on an interposed cell that quiets the antagonist''s motor neuron.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '46be95ca-4d35-49af-af9b-7b726954b606';
